(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqBinomial=[{pn:1,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"If \u03A3 (r=0 to 10) ((10^(r+1) \u2212 1)/10^r) \xB7 \xB9\xB9C(r+1) = (\u03B1\xB9\xB9 \u2212 11\xB9\xB9)/10\xB9\u2070, then \u03B1 is equal to:",o:["11","15","20","24"],c:2,s:`Split the bracket: (10^(r+1) \u2212 1)/10^r = 10 \u2212 (1/10)^r.
So the sum is 10\xB7\u03A3 \xB9\xB9C(r+1) \u2212 \u03A3 \xB9\xB9C(r+1)(1/10)^r, with r from 0 to 10.
Put k = r + 1. Then \u03A3 \xB9\xB9C(k) for k = 1 to 11 is 2\xB9\xB9 \u2212 1.
Also \u03A3 \xB9\xB9C(k)(1/10)^(k\u22121) = 10\xB7[(1 + 1/10)\xB9\xB9 \u2212 1] = 10\xB7(11\xB9\xB9/10\xB9\xB9 \u2212 1).
Subtract: 10(2\xB9\xB9 \u2212 1) \u2212 10(11\xB9\xB9/10\xB9\xB9 \u2212 1) = 10\xB72\xB9\xB9 \u2212 11\xB9\xB9/10\xB9\u2070.
Write 10\xB72\xB9\xB9 = 20\xB9\xB9/10\xB9\u2070. The sum becomes (20\xB9\xB9 \u2212 11\xB9\xB9)/10\xB9\u2070.
Note: the binomial theorem (1 + x)\u207F = \u03A3 \u207FC(k) x\u1D4F is used twice, once with x = 1 and once with x = 1/10. I also checked this by computing the sum exactly in a program: it equals (20\xB9\xB9 \u2212 11\xB9\xB9)/10\xB9\u2070.
Comparing with (\u03B1\xB9\xB9 \u2212 11\xB9\xB9)/10\xB9\u2070 gives \u03B1 = 20, which is option (c).`},{pn:2,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"If \u03A3 (r=1 to 9) ((r + 3)/2^r) \xB7 \u2079C(r) = \u03B1(3/2)\u2079 \u2212 \u03B2, where \u03B1, \u03B2 \u2208 \u2115, then (\u03B1 + \u03B2)\xB2 is equal to:",o:["27","18","81","9"],c:2,s:`Split the term: (r + 3)/2^r \xB7 \u2079C(r) = r\xB7\u2079C(r)/2^r + 3\xB7\u2079C(r)/2^r.
Use r\xB7\u207FC(r) = n\xB7\u207F\u207B\xB9C(r\u22121). So \u03A3 r\xB7\u2079C(r)(1/2)^r (r = 0 to 9) = 9\xB7(1/2)\xB7(1 + 1/2)\u2078 = (9/2)(3/2)\u2078 = 3(3/2)\u2079.
Also 3\xB7\u03A3 \u2079C(r)(1/2)^r (r = 0 to 9) = 3(3/2)\u2079.
Together, from r = 0 to 9 the sum is 6(3/2)\u2079.
The given sum starts from r = 1, so remove the r = 0 term, which is (0 + 3)\xB71 = 3. The sum is 6(3/2)\u2079 \u2212 3.
Note: \u03A3 \u207FC(r) x\u02B3 = (1 + x)\u207F is the base identity; the factor r is handled by r\xB7\u207FC(r) = n\xB7\u207F\u207B\xB9C(r\u22121) (the same as differentiating (1 + x)\u207F).
So \u03B1 = 6, \u03B2 = 3, and (\u03B1 + \u03B2)\xB2 = 81, which is option (c).`},{pn:3,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"General term",q:"The sum of all rational terms in the expansion of (2 + \u221A3)\u2078 is",o:["3763","18817","16923","33845"],c:1,s:`The general term is T(k+1) = \u2078C(k) \xB7 2^(8\u2212k) \xB7 (\u221A3)^k.
This is rational only when (\u221A3)^k is rational, that is when k is even: k = 0, 2, 4, 6, 8.
k = 0: 1\xB7256\xB71 = 256. k = 2: 28\xB764\xB73 = 5376.
k = 4: 70\xB716\xB79 = 10080. k = 6: 28\xB74\xB727 = 3024. k = 8: 1\xB71\xB781 = 81.
Add them: 256 + 5376 + 10080 + 3024 + 81 = 18817.
So the sum of rational terms is 18817, which is option (b).`},{pn:4,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sum",st:"Using differentiation and integration",q:"If 1\xB2\xB7(\xB9\u2075C\u2081) + 2\xB2\xB7(\xB9\u2075C\u2082) + 3\xB2\xB7(\xB9\u2075C\u2083) + \u2026 + 15\xB2\xB7(\xB9\u2075C\u2081\u2085) = 2\u1D50\xB73\u207F\xB75\u1D4F, where m, n, k \u2208 \u2115, then m + n + k is equal to:",o:["21","18","20","19"],c:3,s:`Write r\xB2 = r(r \u2212 1) + r, so the sum is \u03A3 r(r \u2212 1)\xB7\u207FC(r) + \u03A3 r\xB7\u207FC(r) with n = 15.
Use r\xB7\u207FC(r) = n\xB7\u207F\u207B\xB9C(r\u22121) and r(r \u2212 1)\xB7\u207FC(r) = n(n \u2212 1)\xB7\u207F\u207B\xB2C(r\u22122).
So \u03A3 r(r \u2212 1)\u207FC(r) = n(n \u2212 1)\xB72\u207F\u207B\xB2 and \u03A3 r\xB7\u207FC(r) = n\xB72\u207F\u207B\xB9.
Note: these two results also come from differentiating (1 + x)\u207F once and twice and then putting x = 1.
For n = 15: 15\xB714\xB72\xB9\xB3 + 15\xB72\xB9\u2074 = 15\xB72\xB9\xB3(14 + 2) = 15\xB716\xB72\xB9\xB3.
15\xB716\xB72\xB9\xB3 = 3\xB75\xB72\u2074\xB72\xB9\xB3 = 2\xB9\u2077\xB73\xB75. I checked this with a program: the sum is 1966080 = 2\xB9\u2077\xB715.
So m = 17, n = 1, k = 1, and m + n + k = 19, which is option (d).`},{pn:5,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"The remainder when ((64)^(64))^(64) is divided by 7 is equal to",o:["3","1","6","4"],c:1,s:`Write 64 = 63 + 1 = 7\xB79 + 1, so 64 leaves remainder 1 when divided by 7.
Then (64)^(64) = (1 + 63)^(64) = 1 + 64\xB763 + \u2026 , and every term after 1 has the factor 63, so it leaves remainder 1.
So (64)^(64) = 7p + 1 for some integer p.
Then ((64)^(64))^(64) = (7p + 1)^(64) = 1 + 64\xB7(7p) + \u2026 , again every later term has the factor 7.
Note: if a \u2261 1 (mod 7), every power of a is also \u2261 1 (mod 7); this is the quick way to see it.
So the remainder is 1, which is option (b). (The key is not printed for this question; working gives (b).)`},{pn:6,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"Let \u03B1, \u03B2, \u03B3 and \u03B4 be the coefficients of x\u2077, x\u2075, x\xB3 and x respectively in the expansion of (x + \u221A(x\xB3 \u2212 1))\u2075 + (x \u2212 \u221A(x\xB3 \u2212 1))\u2075, x > 1. If u and v satisfy the equations \u03B1u + \u03B2v = 18, \u03B3u + \u03B4v = 20, then u + v equals:",o:["5","3","4","8"],c:0,s:`In (x + s)\u2075 + (x \u2212 s)\u2075 the odd powers of s cancel, so it equals 2[x\u2075 + 10x\xB3s\xB2 + 5x\xB7s\u2074], where 10 = \u2075C\u2082 and 5 = \u2075C\u2084.
Here s\xB2 = x\xB3 \u2212 1. So the expression is 2[x\u2075 + 10x\xB3(x\xB3 \u2212 1) + 5x(x\xB3 \u2212 1)\xB2].
Expand: x\u2075 + 10x\u2076 \u2212 10x\xB3 + 5x\u2077 \u2212 10x\u2074 + 5x.
Multiply by 2: 10x\u2077 + 20x\u2076 + 2x\u2075 \u2212 20x\u2074 \u2212 20x\xB3 + 10x.
Read the coefficients: \u03B1 = 10 (x\u2077), \u03B2 = 2 (x\u2075), \u03B3 = \u221220 (x\xB3), \u03B4 = 10 (x).
Equations: 10u + 2v = 18 and \u221220u + 10v = 20. From the first, v = 9 \u2212 5u.
Put in the second: \u221220u + 90 \u2212 50u = 20, so u = 1 and v = 4. Then u + v = 5, which is option (a).`},{pn:7,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Product of expansions",q:"If in the expansion of (1 + x)^p (1 \u2212 x)^q, the coefficients of x and x\xB2 are 1 and \u22122, respectively, then p\xB2 + q\xB2 is equal to:",o:["18","8","20","13"],c:3,s:`Coefficient of x: from (1 + px + \u2026)(1 \u2212 qx + \u2026) it is p \u2212 q. So p \u2212 q = 1.
Coefficient of x\xB2: \u1D56C\u2082 + \u1D60C\u2082 \u2212 pq (the two x terms multiplied, with a minus sign from \u2212qx).
Put p = q + 1: (q + 1)q/2 + q(q \u2212 1)/2 \u2212 q(q + 1).
The first two terms add to (q\xB2 + q + q\xB2 \u2212 q)/2 = q\xB2, so the coefficient is q\xB2 \u2212 q\xB2 \u2212 q = \u2212q.
Given this equals \u22122, q = 2 and p = 3.
So p\xB2 + q\xB2 = 9 + 4 = 13, which is option (d).`},{pn:8,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"Suppose A and B are the coefficients of 30th and 12th terms respectively in the binomial expansion of (1 + x\xB2)^(2n\u22121). If 2A = 5B, then n is equal to:",o:["22","19","21","20"],c:2,s:`Let m = 2n \u2212 1. The (k+1)th term of (1 + x\xB2)^m has coefficient \u1D50C(k).
So the 30th term has A = \u1D50C\u2082\u2089 and the 12th term has B = \u1D50C\u2081\u2081.
The condition 2A = 5B gives \u1D50C\u2082\u2089 / \u1D50C\u2081\u2081 = 5/2.
Try \u1D50C\u2082\u2089 = \u1D50C(m\u221229) and look for m \u2212 29 = 12, because then \u1D50C\u2081\u2082/\u1D50C\u2081\u2081 = (m \u2212 11)/12 is simple.
Setting (m \u2212 11)/12 = 5/2 gives m \u2212 11 = 30, so m = 41 and the two coefficients are \u2074\xB9C\u2082\u2089 = \u2074\xB9C\u2081\u2082 and \u2074\xB9C\u2081\u2081, whose ratio is 30/12 = 5/2, as needed.
Then 2n \u2212 1 = 41, so n = 21, which is option (c). (A program check confirms n = 21 is the only non-trivial value.)`},{pn:9,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"The remainder, when 7\xB9\u2070\xB3 is divided by 23, is equal to:",o:["6","14","9","17"],c:1,s:`Write 7\xB9\u2070\xB3 = 7\xB7(7\xB2)\u2075\xB9 = 7\xB749\u2075\xB9.
Since 49 = 46 + 3 = 2\xB723 + 3, we have 49\u2075\xB9 \u2261 3\u2075\xB9 (mod 23), because (23k + 3)\u207F leaves the same remainder as 3\u207F.
3\u2075\xB9 = (3\xB3)\xB9\u2077 = 27\xB9\u2077, and 27 = 23 + 4, so 27\xB9\u2077 \u2261 4\xB9\u2077.
4\xB9\u2077 = 4\xB716\u2078, and 16 = 23 \u2212 7, so 16\u2078 \u2261 (\u22127)\u2078 = 49\u2074 \u2261 3\u2074 = 81, which leaves remainder 12 when divided by 23. Then 4\xB712 = 48 \u2261 2.
Note: the trick is to replace a number by a smaller one with the same remainder using (23k + r)\u207F \u2261 r\u207F.
So 7\xB9\u2070\xB3 \u2261 7\xB72 = 14 (mod 23). The remainder is 14, which is option (b).`},{pn:10,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"If the coefficients of x\u2074, x\u2075 and x\u2076 in the expansion of (1 + x)\u207F are in the arithmetic progression, then the maximum value of n is:",o:["14","21","7","28"],c:0,s:`The coefficients are \u207FC\u2084, \u207FC\u2085, \u207FC\u2086. Three numbers are in AP when the middle one is the average: 2\xB7\u207FC\u2085 = \u207FC\u2084 + \u207FC\u2086.
Divide by \u207FC\u2085. Use \u207FC\u2084/\u207FC\u2085 = 5/(n \u2212 4) and \u207FC\u2086/\u207FC\u2085 = (n \u2212 5)/6.
So 2 = 5/(n \u2212 4) + (n \u2212 5)/6.
Multiply by 6(n \u2212 4): 12(n \u2212 4) = 30 + (n \u2212 5)(n \u2212 4).
This gives 12n \u2212 48 = n\xB2 \u2212 9n + 50, so n\xB2 \u2212 21n + 98 = 0.
Factor: (n \u2212 7)(n \u2212 14) = 0, so n = 7 or 14.
The maximum value of n is 14, which is option (a).`},{pn:11,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"The coefficient of x\u2077\u2070 in x\xB2(1 + x)\u2079\u2078 + x\xB3(1 + x)\u2079\u2077 + x\u2074(1 + x)\u2079\u2076 + \u2026 + x\u2075\u2074(1 + x)\u2074\u2076 is \u2079\u2079C(p) \u2212 \u2074\u2076C(q). Then a possible value of p + q is:",o:["68","83","55","61"],c:1,s:`The terms are x\u1D4F(1 + x)^(100\u2212k) for k = 2 to 54. The coefficient of x\u2077\u2070 in the kth term is \xB9\u2070\u2070\u207B\u1D4FC(70\u2212k) = \xB9\u2070\u2070\u207B\u1D4FC\u2083\u2080.
So we need \u2079\u2078C\u2083\u2080 + \u2079\u2077C\u2083\u2080 + \u2026 + \u2074\u2076C\u2083\u2080.
Use the hockey-stick identity: \xB3\u2070C\u2083\u2080 + \xB3\xB9C\u2083\u2080 + \u2026 + \u1D50C\u2083\u2080 = \u1D50\u207A\xB9C\u2083\u2081.
Sum from m = 30 to 98 is \u2079\u2079C\u2083\u2081; remove the part from m = 30 to 45, which is \u2074\u2076C\u2083\u2081.
So the coefficient is \u2079\u2079C\u2083\u2081 \u2212 \u2074\u2076C\u2083\u2081 = \u2079\u2079C\u2086\u2088 \u2212 \u2074\u2076C\u2081\u2085 (using \u207FC(r) = \u207FC(n\u2212r)).
Note: the hockey-stick identity says a column of binomial coefficients adds up to the next entry diagonally; I also confirmed \u2079\u2079C\u2086\u2088 \u2212 \u2074\u2076C\u2081\u2085 against the direct sum in a program.
So p = 68 and q = 15, and p + q = 83, which is option (b).`},{pn:12,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:`Among the statements
(S1): 2023\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 \u2212 1999\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 is divisible by 8
(S2): 13(13)\u207F \u2212 11n \u2212 13 is divisible by 144 for infinitely many n \u2208 \u2115`,o:["only (S2) is correct","both (S1) and (S2) are incorrect","both (S1) and (S2) are correct","only (S1) is correct"],c:2,s:`S1: 2023 \u2212 1999 = 24, which is divisible by 8, and a\u1D50 \u2212 b\u1D50 is divisible by a \u2212 b. So 2023\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 \u2212 1999\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 is divisible by 24, hence by 8. S1 is correct.
S2: write 13(13)\u207F = 13\u207F\u207A\xB9 = (1 + 12)\u207F\u207A\xB9.
Expand: (1 + 12)\u207F\u207A\xB9 = 1 + 12(n + 1) + 144\xB7(a whole number), because every later term has 12\xB2 or a higher power.
So 13\u207F\u207A\xB9 \u2212 11n \u2212 13 = 1 + 12n + 12 \u2212 11n \u2212 13 + 144k = n + 144k.
This is divisible by 144 exactly when n is divisible by 144.
Note: there are infinitely many such n (144, 288, 432, \u2026), so S2 is correct as stated. A program check shows the remainder equals n for n up to 30, matching n + 144k.
Both statements are correct, which is option (c).`},{pn:13,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"25\xB9\u2079\u2070 \u2212 19\xB9\u2079\u2070 \u2212 8\xB9\u2079\u2070 + 2\xB9\u2079\u2070 is divisible by",o:["both 14 and 34","34 but not by 14","14 but not by 34","neither 14 nor 34"],c:1,s:`Group as (25\xB9\u2079\u2070 \u2212 8\xB9\u2079\u2070) \u2212 (19\xB9\u2079\u2070 \u2212 2\xB9\u2079\u2070). Since a\u207F \u2212 b\u207F is divisible by a \u2212 b: 25 \u2212 8 = 17 and 19 \u2212 2 = 17.
So the whole number is divisible by 17.
It is also even: 25\xB9\u2079\u2070 and 19\xB9\u2079\u2070 are odd (odd \u2212 odd is even), and 8\xB9\u2079\u2070 and 2\xB9\u2079\u2070 are even.
17 and 2 have no common factor, so the number is divisible by 34.
For 14 we need divisibility by 7. Modulo 7: 25 \u2261 4, 19 \u2261 5, 8 \u2261 1, 2 \u2261 2. Powers repeat: 4\xB9\u2079\u2070 \u2261 4 (cycle 3), 5\xB9\u2079\u2070 \u2261 5\u2074 \u2261 2 (cycle 6), 1\xB9\u2079\u2070 = 1, 2\xB9\u2079\u2070 \u2261 2.
The number leaves 4 \u2212 2 \u2212 1 + 2 = 3 modulo 7, so it is not divisible by 7, hence not by 14.
So it is divisible by 34 but not by 14, which is option (b).`},{pn:14,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"Let the number (22)\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 + (2022)\xB2\xB2 leave the remainder \u03B1 when divided by 3 and \u03B2 when divided by 7. Then (\u03B1\xB2 + \u03B2\xB2) is equal to",o:["20","13","5","10"],c:2,s:`Mod 3: 22 = 21 + 1, so 22 \u2261 1 and (22)\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 \u2261 1. Also 2022 = 3\xB7674, so 2022 \u2261 0 and (2022)\xB2\xB2 \u2261 0.
So the number leaves remainder 1 on division by 3, and \u03B1 = 1.
Mod 7: 22 = 21 + 1, so 22 \u2261 1 and (22)\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 \u2261 1.
2022 = 7\xB7289 \u2212 1, so 2022 \u2261 \u22121 and (2022)\xB2\xB2 \u2261 (\u22121)\xB2\xB2 = 1.
So the number leaves 1 + 1 = 2 on division by 7, and \u03B2 = 2.
Then \u03B1\xB2 + \u03B2\xB2 = 1 + 4 = 5, which is option (c).`},{pn:15,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Using differentiation and integration",q:"If (1/(n + 1))\xB7\u207FC(n) + (1/n)\xB7\u207FC(n\u22121) + \u2026 + (1/2)\xB7\u207FC\u2081 + \u207FC\u2080 = 1023/10, then n is equal to",o:["8","9","6","7"],c:1,s:`The terms are \u207FC(k)/(k + 1) for k = 0 to n.
Use \u207FC(k)/(k + 1) = \u207F\u207A\xB9C(k+1)/(n + 1).
So the sum is [\u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2081 + \u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2082 + \u2026 + \u207F\u207A\xB9C(n+1)]/(n + 1) = (2\u207F\u207A\xB9 \u2212 1)/(n + 1).
Note: the same result comes from integrating (1 + x)\u207F from 0 to 1, which gives \u03A3 \u207FC(k)/(k + 1) = (2\u207F\u207A\xB9 \u2212 1)/(n + 1).
Set (2\u207F\u207A\xB9 \u2212 1)/(n + 1) = 1023/10. Since 1023 = 2\xB9\u2070 \u2212 1, take n + 1 = 10.
Check: (2\xB9\u2070 \u2212 1)/10 = 1023/10. So n = 9, which is option (b).`},{pn:16,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Sums of binomial coefficients",q:"The sum of the coefficients of the first 50 terms in the binomial expansion of (1 \u2212 x)\xB9\u2070\u2070, is equal to",o:["\xB9\u2070\xB9C\u2085\u2080","\u2212\u2079\u2079C\u2084\u2089","\u2212\xB9\u2070\xB9C\u2085\u2080","\u2079\u2079C\u2084\u2089"],c:1,s:`The first 50 terms have coefficients (\u22121)\u02B3\xB7\xB9\u2070\u2070C(r) for r = 0 to 49.
Use Pascal: \xB9\u2070\u2070C(r) = \u2079\u2079C(r) + \u2079\u2079C(r\u22121).
Then the sum is \u03A3 (\u22121)\u02B3[\u2079\u2079C(r) + \u2079\u2079C(r\u22121)], and consecutive terms cancel in pairs (telescoping).
Only the last piece is left: (\u22121)\u2074\u2079\xB7\u2079\u2079C\u2084\u2089 = \u2212\u2079\u2079C\u2084\u2089.
Note: the general rule is \u03A3 (r=0 to k) (\u22121)\u02B3\xB7\u207FC(r) = (\u22121)\u1D4F\xB7\u207F\u207B\xB9C(k). I checked this value with a program.
So the sum is \u2212\u2079\u2079C\u2084\u2089, which is option (b).`},{pn:17,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"num",st:"Digits and approximation",q:"Fractional part of the number 4\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2/15 is equal to",o:["1/15","4/15","14/15","8/15"],c:0,s:`Write 4\xB2 = 16 = 1 + 15. So 4\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 = (4\xB2)\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB9 = (1 + 15)\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB9.
Expand: (1 + 15)\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB9 = 1 + 15\xB71011 + 15\xB2\xB7(\u2026) + \u2026, so every term after 1 is a multiple of 15.
So 4\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 = 15m + 1 for some whole number m.
Dividing by 15: 4\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2/15 = m + 1/15.
The whole part is m and the fractional part is 1/15, which is option (a).`},{pn:18,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"The value of 1/(1!\xB750!) + 1/(3!\xB748!) + 1/(5!\xB746!) + \u2026 + 1/(49!\xB72!) + 1/(51!\xB71!) is",o:["2\u2075\xB9/50!","2\u2075\u2070/50!","2\u2075\xB9/51!","2\u2075\u2070/51!"],c:3,s:`Each term is 1/(k!\xB7(51 \u2212 k)!) with k = 1, 3, 5, \u2026, 51 (k is odd, and the two factorials add up to 51).
Multiply and divide by 51!: 1/(k!(51 \u2212 k)!) = \u2075\xB9C(k)/51!.
So the sum = [\u2075\xB9C\u2081 + \u2075\xB9C\u2083 + \u2026 + \u2075\xB9C\u2085\u2081]/51!.
The sum of binomial coefficients at odd places of (1 + x)\u207F is 2\u207F\u207B\xB9, so the bracket is 2\u2075\u2070.
Note: this comes from (1 + 1)\u207F + (1 \u2212 1)\u207F and (1 + 1)\u207F \u2212 (1 \u2212 1)\u207F giving even-place and odd-place sums that are each half of 2\u207F.
So the value is 2\u2075\u2070/51!, which is option (d).`},{pn:19,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Sums of binomial coefficients",q:"The value of \u03A3 (r=0 to 22) \xB2\xB2C(r)\xB7\xB2\xB3C(r) is",o:["\u2074\u2075C\u2082\u2084","\u2074\u2075C\u2082\u2083","\u2074\u2074C\u2082\u2083","\u2074\u2074C\u2082\u2082"],c:1,s:`Use \xB2\xB3C(r) = \xB2\xB3C(23 \u2212 r), so each term is \xB2\xB2C(r)\xB7\xB2\xB3C(23 \u2212 r).
The two lower numbers add up to r + (23 \u2212 r) = 23, which is the pattern of the Vandermonde identity.
Idea: the coefficient of x\xB2\xB3 in (1 + x)\xB2\xB2\xB7(1 + x)\xB2\xB3 = (1 + x)\u2074\u2075 is \u03A3 \xB2\xB2C(r)\xB7\xB2\xB3C(23 \u2212 r).
Note: on the left we multiply the two expansions and pick pairs of terms whose powers add to 23; on the right the same coefficient is \u2074\u2075C\u2082\u2083.
The range r = 0 to 22 covers every non-zero term (\xB2\xB2C(r) is zero for r > 22).
So the sum is \u2074\u2075C\u2082\u2083, which is option (b).`},{pn:20,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"If (\xB3\u2070C\u2081)\xB2 + 2(\xB3\u2070C\u2082)\xB2 + 3(\xB3\u2070C\u2083)\xB2 + \u2026 + 30(\xB3\u2070C\u2083\u2080)\xB2 = \u03B1\xB760!/(30!)\xB2, then \u03B1 is equal to",o:["60","10","15","30"],c:2,s:`The sum is \u03A3 k\xB7(\xB3\u2070C(k))\xB2 = \u03A3 \xB3\u2070C(k)\xB7[k\xB7\xB3\u2070C(k)].
Use k\xB7\xB3\u2070C(k) = 30\xB7\xB2\u2079C(k\u22121). So the sum = 30\xB7\u03A3 \xB3\u2070C(k)\xB7\xB2\u2079C(k\u22121).
Write \xB3\u2070C(k) = \xB3\u2070C(30 \u2212 k). Now the two lower numbers (30 \u2212 k) and (k \u2212 1) add to 29, which is the Vandermonde pattern.
So \u03A3 \xB3\u2070C(30 \u2212 k)\xB7\xB2\u2079C(k \u2212 1) is the coefficient of x\xB2\u2079 in (1 + x)\xB3\u2070(1 + x)\xB2\u2079 = (1 + x)\u2075\u2079, which is \u2075\u2079C\u2082\u2089.
So the sum = 30\xB7\u2075\u2079C\u2082\u2089.
Now \u2076\u2070C\u2083\u2080 = 60!/(30!)\xB2 and \u2076\u2070C\u2083\u2080 = (60/30)\xB7\u2075\u2079C\u2082\u2089 = 2\xB7\u2075\u2079C\u2082\u2089, so 60!/(30!)\xB2 = 2\xB7\u2075\u2079C\u2082\u2089.
Then 30\xB7\u2075\u2079C\u2082\u2089 = 15\xB7(2\xB7\u2075\u2079C\u2082\u2089) = 15\xB760!/(30!)\xB2, so \u03B1 = 15, which is option (c). (A program check of the exact sum agrees.)`},{pn:21,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"num",st:"Digits and approximation",q:"Let x = (8\u221A3 + 13)\xB9\xB3 and y = (7\u221A2 + 9)\u2079. If [t] denotes the greatest integer \u2264 t, then",o:["[x] is even but [y] is odd","[x] + [y] is even","[x] and [y] are both odd","[x] is odd but [y] is even"],c:1,s:`Take the conjugate x\u2032 = (13 \u2212 8\u221A3)\xB9\xB3. Since 8\u221A3 \u2248 13.86, 13 \u2212 8\u221A3 \u2248 \u22120.86, and an odd power of it lies between \u22121 and 0.
By the binomial theorem, x + x\u2032 = 2\xB7(sum of the even-position terms), which is an integer, call it 2a. It is even.
So x = 2a \u2212 x\u2032 = 2a + (a number between 0 and 1), and therefore [x] = 2a, which is even.
For y: y\u2032 = (9 \u2212 7\u221A2)\u2079, and 7\u221A2 \u2248 9.90, so 9 \u2212 7\u221A2 \u2248 \u22120.90 and y\u2032 lies between \u22121 and 0.
In the same way y + y\u2032 = 2c is an even integer, so [y] = 2c, which is even.
Note: this is the standard trick of adding the conjugate so the irrational parts cancel and the answer is a whole number.
Both [x] and [y] are even, so [x] + [y] is even, which is option (b).`},{pn:22,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"The remainder when (11)\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB9 + (1011)\xB9\xB9 is divided by 9 is",o:["1","4","6","8"],c:3,s:`Mod 9: 11 = 9 + 2, so 11\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB9 \u2261 2\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB9.
2\xB3 = 8 \u2261 \u22121, so 2\u2076 \u2261 1. Since 1011 = 6\xB7168 + 3, 2\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB9 \u2261 2\xB3 = 8.
1011 = 9\xB7112 + 3, so 1011 \u2261 3 and (1011)\xB9\xB9 \u2261 3\xB9\xB9.
3\xB2 = 9 \u2261 0, so any power of 3 from 3\xB2 upward is divisible by 9. So 3\xB9\xB9 \u2261 0.
Add: 8 + 0 = 8. The remainder is 8, which is option (d).`},{pn:23,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sum",st:"Sums of binomial coefficients",q:"\u03A3 (i, j = 0 to n, i \u2260 j) \u207FC(i)\xB7\u207FC(j) is equal to",o:["2\xB2\u207F \u2212 \xB2\u207FC\u2099","2\xB2\u207F\u207B\xB9 \u2212 \xB2\u207F\u207B\xB9C\u2099\u208B\u2081","2\xB2\u207F \u2212 (1/2)\xB7\xB2\u207FC\u2099","2\u207F\u207B\xB9 + \xB2\u207F\u207B\xB9C\u2099"],c:0,s:`First take all pairs (i, j) with no restriction: \u03A3\u1D62 \u03A3\u2C7C \u207FC(i)\xB7\u207FC(j) = (\u03A3 \u207FC(i))\xB7(\u03A3 \u207FC(j)) = 2\u207F\xB72\u207F = 2\xB2\u207F.
The pairs we must remove are those with i = j. Their sum is \u03A3 (\u207FC(i))\xB2.
Use \u03A3 (\u207FC(i))\xB2 = \xB2\u207FC\u2099 (the coefficient of x\u207F in (1 + x)\u207F(1 + x)\u207F).
So the required sum = 2\xB2\u207F \u2212 \xB2\u207FC\u2099, which is option (a). (Checked for n = 8: both sides equal 52666.)`},{pn:24,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"The remainder when (2021)\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 + (2022)\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB9 is divided by 7 is",o:["0","1","2","6"],c:0,s:`2021 = 7\xB7288 + 5, so 2021 \u2261 5 (mod 7). Also 2022 \u2261 6 \u2261 \u22121.
5\u2076 \u2261 1 (mod 7) (Fermat). Since 2022 = 6\xB7337, 5\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 = (5\u2076)\xB3\xB3\u2077 \u2261 1.
(\u22121)\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB9 = \u22121 because the power is odd, so (2022)\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB9 \u2261 \u22121.
Add: 1 + (\u22121) = 0, so the sum is divisible by 7.
The remainder is 0, which is option (a).`},{pn:25,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"The remainder when 7\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 + 3\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 is divided by 5 is",o:["0","2","3","4"],c:2,s:`Mod 5: 7 \u2261 2, so 7\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 \u2261 2\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2.
2\u2074 = 16 \u2261 1 (mod 5). Since 2022 = 4\xB7505 + 2, 2\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 \u2261 2\xB2 = 4.
3\u2074 = 81 \u2261 1 (mod 5), so 3\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 \u2261 3\xB2 = 9 \u2261 4.
Add: 4 + 4 = 8 \u2261 3 (mod 5).
The remainder is 3, which is option (c).`},{pn:26,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"Consider the sequence a\u2081, a\u2082, a\u2083, \u2026 such that a\u2081 = 1, a\u2082 = 2 and a(n+2) = 2/a(n+1) + a\u2099 for n = 1, 2, 3, \u2026. If ((a\u2081 + 1/a\u2082)/a\u2083)\xB7((a\u2082 + 1/a\u2083)/a\u2084)\xB7((a\u2083 + 1/a\u2084)/a\u2085)\xB7\u2026\xB7((a\u2083\u2080 + 1/a\u2083\u2081)/a\u2083\u2082) = 2^\u03B1\xB7(\u2076\xB9C\u2083\u2081), then \u03B1 is equal to :",o:["\u221230","\u221231","\u221260","\u221261"],c:2,s:`Multiply the rule by a(n+1): a(n+1)\xB7a(n+2) = 2 + a\u2099\xB7a(n+1). So the product a\u2099a(n+1) goes up by 2 each step.
Since a\u2081a\u2082 = 2, we get a\u2099\xB7a(n+1) = 2n.
Each bracket is (a\u2099 + 1/a(n+1))/a(n+2) = (a\u2099a(n+1) + 1)/(a(n+1)\xB7a(n+2)) = (2n + 1)/(2(n + 1)).
So the product for n = 1 to 30 is (3\xB75\xB77\xB7\u2026\xB761)/(2\xB3\u2070\xB7(2\xB73\xB74\xB7\u2026\xB731)) with the denominator 2\xB3\u2070\xB731!.
The odd product 3\xB75\xB7\u2026\xB761 = 61!/(2\xB3\u2070\xB730!).
So the value is 61!/(2\xB3\u2070\xB730!\xB72\xB3\u2070\xB731!) = \u2076\xB9C\u2083\u2081/2\u2076\u2070 = 2\u207B\u2076\u2070\xB7\u2076\xB9C\u2083\u2081.
Note: the first terms 1, 2, 2, 3 confirm a\u2099a(n+1) = 2n (1\xB72, 2\xB72, 2\xB73 = 2, 4, 6).
So \u03B1 = \u221260, which is option (c).`},{pn:27,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"The remainder when 3\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 is divided by 5 is",o:["1","2","3","4"],c:3,s:`Write 3\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 = 9\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB9 = (10 \u2212 1)\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB9.
Expand: (10 \u2212 1)\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB9 = 10\xB7(\u2026) + (\u22121)\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB9, since every other term has a factor of 10.
(\u22121)\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB9 = \u22121, so 3\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 = 10m \u2212 1 = 5(2m \u2212 1) + 4.
Note: a negative remainder \u22121 is the same as 4, because \u22121 = 5\xB7(\u22121) + 4.
The remainder is 4, which is option (d).`},{pn:28,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"The remainder when (2021)\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB3 is divided by 7 is",o:["1","2","5","6"],c:2,s:`2021 = 7\xB7288 + 5, so 2021 \u2261 5 (mod 7). We need 5\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB3 mod 7.
Find the cycle: 5\xB2 = 25 \u2261 4 and 5\xB3 = 125 = 7\xB718 \u2212 1 \u2261 \u22121.
So 5\u2076 = (5\xB3)\xB2 \u2261 1. Now 2023 = 6\xB7337 + 1.
Then 5\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB3 = (5\u2076)\xB3\xB3\u2077\xB75 \u2261 5.
The remainder is 5, which is option (c).`},{pn:29,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Sums of binomial coefficients",q:"If \u03A3 (k=1 to 31) \xB3\xB9C(k)\xB7\xB3\xB9C(k\u22121) \u2212 \u03A3 (k=1 to 30) \xB3\u2070C(k)\xB7\xB3\u2070C(k\u22121) = \u03B1(60!)/((30!)(31!)), where \u03B1 \u2208 \u211D, then the value of 16\u03B1 is equal to",o:["1411","1320","1615","1855"],c:0,s:`Write \xB3\xB9C(k\u22121) = \xB3\xB9C(32 \u2212 k). Then \xB3\xB9C(k)\xB7\xB3\xB9C(32 \u2212 k) has lower numbers adding to 32, so by Vandermonde the first sum is the coefficient of x\xB3\xB2 in (1 + x)\u2076\xB2, which is \u2076\xB2C\u2083\u2082.
Similarly \xB3\u2070C(k\u22121) = \xB3\u2070C(31 \u2212 k), so the second sum is the coefficient of x\xB3\xB9 in (1 + x)\u2076\u2070, which is \u2076\u2070C\u2083\u2081.
So the difference is \u2076\xB2C\u2083\u2082 \u2212 \u2076\u2070C\u2083\u2081.
Write both with \u2076\u2070!/(30!)\xB2: \u2076\xB2C\u2083\u2082 = (62\xB761)/(32\xB731)\xB760!/(30!)\xB2 = (61/16)\xB760!/(30!)\xB2, and \u2076\u2070C\u2083\u2081 = (30/31)\xB760!/(30!)\xB2.
Difference = (61/16 \u2212 30/31)\xB760!/(30!)\xB2 = (1411/496)\xB760!/(30!)\xB2.
The target is \u03B1\xB760!/(30!\xB731!) = (\u03B1/31)\xB760!/(30!)\xB2. So \u03B1/31 = 1411/496, giving \u03B1 = 1411/16.
Note: a program check of the exact sums gives 16\u03B1 = 1411.
So 16\u03B1 = 1411, which is option (a).`},{pn:30,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"Let n \u2265 5 be an integer. If 9\u207F \u2212 8n \u2212 1 = 64\u03B1 and 6\u207F \u2212 5n \u2212 1 = 25\u03B2, then \u03B1 \u2212 \u03B2 is equal to",o:["1 + \u207FC\u2082(8 \u2212 5) + \u207FC\u2083(8\xB2 \u2212 5\xB2) + \u2026 + \u207FC\u2099(8\u207F\u207B\xB9 \u2212 5\u207F\u207B\xB9)","1 + \u207FC\u2083(8 \u2212 5) + \u207FC\u2084(8\xB2 \u2212 5\xB2) + \u2026 + \u207FC\u2099(8\u207F\u207B\xB2 \u2212 5\u207F\u207B\xB2)","\u207FC\u2083(8 \u2212 5) + \u207FC\u2084(8\xB2 \u2212 5\xB2) + \u2026 + \u207FC\u2099(8\u207F\u207B\xB2 \u2212 5\u207F\u207B\xB2)","\u207FC\u2084(8 \u2212 5) + \u207FC\u2085(8\xB2 \u2212 5\xB2) + \u2026 + \u207FC\u2099(8\u207F\u207B\xB3 \u2212 5\u207F\u207B\xB3)"],c:2,s:`Expand 9\u207F = (1 + 8)\u207F = 1 + 8n + \u207FC\u20828\xB2 + \u207FC\u20838\xB3 + \u2026 + \u207FC\u20998\u207F.
So 9\u207F \u2212 8n \u2212 1 = \u207FC\u20828\xB2 + \u207FC\u20838\xB3 + \u2026 + \u207FC\u20998\u207F, and dividing by 64 gives \u03B1 = \u207FC\u2082 + \u207FC\u2083\xB78 + \u207FC\u2084\xB78\xB2 + \u2026 + \u207FC\u2099\xB78\u207F\u207B\xB2.
In the same way 6\u207F = (1 + 5)\u207F, so \u03B2 = \u207FC\u2082 + \u207FC\u2083\xB75 + \u207FC\u2084\xB75\xB2 + \u2026 + \u207FC\u2099\xB75\u207F\u207B\xB2.
Subtract: the \u207FC\u2082 terms cancel, leaving \u03B1 \u2212 \u03B2 = \u207FC\u2083(8 \u2212 5) + \u207FC\u2084(8\xB2 \u2212 5\xB2) + \u2026 + \u207FC\u2099(8\u207F\u207B\xB2 \u2212 5\u207F\u207B\xB2).
Note: for n = 7 a program gives \u03B1 \u2212 \u03B2 = 63537 both from the definitions and from this formula.
This matches option (c).`},{pn:31,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Using differentiation and integration",q:"If \xB2\u2070C(r) is the coefficient of x\u02B3 in the expansion of (1 + x)\xB2\u2070, then the value of \u03A3 (r=0 to 20) r\xB2\xB7\xB2\u2070C(r) is equal to",o:["420 \xD7 2\xB9\u2078","380 \xD7 2\xB9\u2078","380 \xD7 2\xB9\u2079","420 \xD7 2\xB9\u2079"],c:0,s:`Write r\xB2 = r(r \u2212 1) + r.
\u03A3 r(r \u2212 1)\xB7\u207FC(r) = n(n \u2212 1)\xB72\u207F\u207B\xB2 and \u03A3 r\xB7\u207FC(r) = n\xB72\u207F\u207B\xB9, with n = 20.
Note: both follow by differentiating (1 + x)\u207F once or twice and putting x = 1; or from r\xB7\u207FC(r) = n\xB7\u207F\u207B\xB9C(r\u22121).
So the sum = 20\xB719\xB72\xB9\u2078 + 20\xB72\xB9\u2079 = 380\xB72\xB9\u2078 + 40\xB72\xB9\u2078.
Add: 420\xB72\xB9\u2078 (a program check gives 110100480 for both).
This is option (a).`},{pn:32,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Sums of binomial coefficients",q:"\u03A3 (k=0 to 20) (\xB2\u2070C(k))\xB2 is equal to",o:["\u2074\u2070C\u2082\u2081","\u2074\xB9C\u2082\u2080","\u2074\u2070C\u2082\u2080","\u2074\u2070C\u2081\u2089"],c:2,s:`Use \xB2\u2070C(k) = \xB2\u2070C(20 \u2212 k), so (\xB2\u2070C(k))\xB2 = \xB2\u2070C(k)\xB7\xB2\u2070C(20 \u2212 k).
The lower numbers add to 20, so by Vandermonde the sum is the coefficient of x\xB2\u2070 in (1 + x)\xB2\u2070(1 + x)\xB2\u2070 = (1 + x)\u2074\u2070.
Note: on the left we multiply two copies of the expansion and collect x\xB2\u2070; on the right it is \u2074\u2070C\u2082\u2080.
So the sum is \u2074\u2070C\u2082\u2080, which is option (c).`},{pn:33,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Product of expansions",q:"For the natural numbers m, n, if (1 \u2212 y)\u1D50(1 + y)\u207F = 1 + a\u2081y + a\u2082y\xB2 + \u2026 + a(m+n)y^(m+n) and a\u2081 = a\u2082 = 10, then the value of (m + n) is equal to",o:["100","80","88","64"],c:1,s:`The coefficient of y: from (1 \u2212 my + \u2026)(1 + ny + \u2026), a\u2081 = n \u2212 m = 10.
The coefficient of y\xB2: a\u2082 = \u207FC\u2082 + \u1D50C\u2082 \u2212 mn = 10.
Write \u207FC\u2082 + \u1D50C\u2082 \u2212 mn = [n(n \u2212 1) + m(m \u2212 1) \u2212 2mn]/2 = [(n \u2212 m)\xB2 \u2212 (n + m)]/2.
Put n \u2212 m = 10: (100 \u2212 (m + n))/2 = 10.
So 100 \u2212 (m + n) = 20, giving m + n = 80.
Note: this gives m = 35 and n = 45; a program check confirms a\u2081 = a\u2082 = 10 for these.
So m + n = 80, which is option (b).`},{pn:34,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"gen",st:"Index not a positive integer",q:"If b is very small as compared to the value of a, so that the cube and other higher powers of b/a can be neglected in the identity 1/(a \u2212 b) + 1/(a \u2212 2b) + 1/(a \u2212 3b) + \u2026 + 1/(a \u2212 nb) = \u03B1n + \u03B2n\xB2 + \u03B3n\xB3, then the value of \u03B3 is",o:["(a\xB2 + b)/(3a\xB3)","b\xB2/(3a\xB3)","(a + b\xB2)/(3a\xB3)","(a + b)/(3a\xB2)"],c:1,s:`Write 1/(a \u2212 kb) = (1/a)\xB7(1 \u2212 kb/a)\u207B\xB9.
Use (1 \u2212 t)\u207B\xB9 = 1 + t + t\xB2 + \u2026 (valid for small t) with t = kb/a, and drop t\xB3 and higher.
So 1/(a \u2212 kb) \u2248 (1/a)[1 + kb/a + k\xB2b\xB2/a\xB2].
Add for k = 1 to n: \u03A31 = n, \u03A3k = n(n + 1)/2, \u03A3k\xB2 = n(n + 1)(2n + 1)/6.
The only n\xB3 term comes from \u03A3k\xB2 = (2n\xB3 + 3n\xB2 + n)/6, with n\xB3 coefficient 1/3.
So the n\xB3 part of the sum is (1/a)\xB7(b\xB2/a\xB2)\xB7(1/3)\xB7n\xB3 = b\xB2/(3a\xB3)\xB7n\xB3.
Note: the n and n\xB2 parts also get contributions, but \u03B3 is just the n\xB3 coefficient.
So \u03B3 = b\xB2/(3a\xB3), which is option (b).`},{pn:35,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"num",st:"Digits and approximation",q:"The lowest integer which is greater than (1 + 1/10\xB9\u2070\u2070)^(10\xB9\u2070\u2070) is",o:["2","1","3","4"],c:2,s:`Let N = 10\xB9\u2070\u2070. Then (1 + 1/N)\u1D3A = 1 + N\xB7(1/N) + \u1D3AC\u2082(1/N)\xB2 + \u2026 = 1 + 1 + (positive terms).
So the number is greater than 2.
The k-th term is \u1D3AC(k)/N\u1D4F = [N(N \u2212 1)\u2026(N \u2212 k + 1)]/(N\u1D4F\xB7k!), which is less than 1/k!.
So the number is less than 1 + 1 + 1/2! + 1/3! + 1/4! + \u2026 = e, and e < 3.
Note: the number lies between 2 and 3 but not exactly an integer, so the lowest integer greater than it is 3.
That is option (c).`},{pn:36,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sum",st:"Sums of binomial coefficients",q:"The value of \u03A3 (r=0 to 6) (\u2076C(r)\xB7\u2076C(6\u2212r)) is equal to",o:["1324","1124","1024","924"],c:3,s:`The lower numbers r and 6 \u2212 r add to 6, which is the Vandermonde pattern.
The sum is the coefficient of x\u2076 in (1 + x)\u2076(1 + x)\u2076 = (1 + x)\xB9\xB2.
That coefficient is \xB9\xB2C\u2086 = (12\xB711\xB710\xB79\xB78\xB77)/(6\xB75\xB74\xB73\xB72\xB71).
Cancel: \xB9\xB2C\u2086 = 924.
So the value is 924, which is option (d).`},{pn:37,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"The value of \u2212\xB9\u2075C\u2081 + 2\xB7\xB9\u2075C\u2082 \u2212 3\xB7\xB9\u2075C\u2083 + \u2026\u2026 \u2212 15\xB7\xB9\u2075C\u2081\u2085 + \xB9\u2074C\u2081 + \xB9\u2074C\u2083 + \xB9\u2074C\u2085 + \u2026\u2026 + \xB9\u2074C\u2081\u2081 is",o:["2\xB9\xB3 \u2212 14","2\xB9\u2074","2\xB9\u2076 \u2212 1","2\xB9\xB3 \u2212 13"],c:0,s:`The first part is \u03A3 (\u22121)\u02B3\xB7r\xB7\xB9\u2075C\u1D63 for r = 1 to 15, because the signs go \u2212, +, \u2212, \u2026 starting with r = 1.
Use r\xB7\u207FC\u1D63 = n\xB7\u207F\u207B\xB9C\u1D63\u208B\u2081, so each term becomes 15\xB7\xB9\u2074C\u1D63\u208B\u2081 with sign (\u22121)\u02B3.
Note: this identity just says choosing a team and then a captain from it is the same as choosing the captain first and the rest after.
So the first part is \u221215(\xB9\u2074C\u2080 \u2212 \xB9\u2074C\u2081 + \xB9\u2074C\u2082 \u2212 \u2026 + \xB9\u2074C\u2081\u2084) = \u221215(1 \u2212 1)\xB9\u2074 = 0.
The second part is the sum of odd-position coefficients of 14 from \xB9\u2074C\u2081 to \xB9\u2074C\u2081\u2081, which stops before \xB9\u2074C\u2081\u2083.
All odd-indexed \xB9\u2074C\u1D63 add up to 2\xB9\xB3, so we subtract the missing \xB9\u2074C\u2081\u2083 = 14.
So the value is 2\xB9\xB3 \u2212 14, which is option (a).`},{pn:38,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"If n \u2265 2 is a positive integer, then the sum of the series \u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2082 + 2(\xB2C\u2082 + \xB3C\u2082 + \u2074C\u2082 + \u2026\u2026 + \u207FC\u2082) is",o:["n(n \u2212 1)(2n + 1)/6","n(n + 1)\xB2(n + 2)/12","n(n + 1)(2n + 1)/6","n(2n + 1)(3n + 1)/6"],c:2,s:`Add the terms of the bracket with the hockey-stick rule: \xB2C\u2082 + \xB3C\u2082 + \u2026 + \u207FC\u2082 = \u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2083.
Note: the hockey-stick rule says \u02B3C_k + \u02B3\u207A\xB9C_k + \u2026 + \u207FC_k = \u207F\u207A\xB9C_\uFF5Bk+1\uFF5D.
So the series is \u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2082 + 2\xB7\u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2083.
Write them out: \u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2082 = (n+1)n/2 and \u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2083 = (n+1)n(n\u22121)/6.
Sum = (n+1)n/2 + (n+1)n(n\u22121)/3 = n(n+1)[3 + 2(n\u22121)]/6 = n(n+1)(2n+1)/6.
Check with n = 2: 3 + 2\xB71 = 5 and 2\xB73\xB75/6 = 5.
So the sum is n(n+1)(2n+1)/6, which is option (c).`},{pn:39,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sept \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"If \uFF5Bp\uFF5D denotes the fractional part of the number p, then \uFF5B3\xB2\u2070\u2070/8\uFF5D is equal to",o:["5/8","7/8","3/8","1/8"],c:3,s:`The fractional part of N/8 is (remainder of N divided by 8)/8.
Write 3\xB2\u2070\u2070 = (3\xB2)\xB9\u2070\u2070 = 9\xB9\u2070\u2070 = (8 + 1)\xB9\u2070\u2070.
Expand: (8 + 1)\xB9\u2070\u2070 = 8\xB7(some integer) + 1, since every term except the last has a factor 8.
So 3\xB2\u2070\u2070 leaves remainder 1 when divided by 8.
Hence the fractional part is 1/8, which is option (d).`},{pn:40,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sum",st:"Using differentiation and integration",q:"If \xB2\u2070C\u2081 + (2\xB2)\xB7\xB2\u2070C\u2082 + (3\xB2)\xB7\xB2\u2070C\u2083 + \u2026\u2026 + (20\xB2)\xB7\xB2\u2070C\u2082\u2080 = A(2^\u03B2), then the ordered pair (A, \u03B2) is equal to",o:["(420, 19)","(420, 18)","(380, 19)","(380, 18)"],c:1,s:`The sum is \u03A3 r\xB2\xB7\u207FC\u1D63 with n = 20.
Write r\xB2 = r(r \u2212 1) + r, so \u03A3 r\xB2\xB7\u207FC\u1D63 = \u03A3 r(r\u22121)\xB7\u207FC\u1D63 + \u03A3 r\xB7\u207FC\u1D63.
Use r(r\u22121)\xB7\u207FC\u1D63 = n(n\u22121)\xB7\u207F\u207B\xB2C\u1D63\u208B\u2082, so the first sum is n(n\u22121)\xB72\u207F\u207B\xB2.
Use r\xB7\u207FC\u1D63 = n\xB7\u207F\u207B\xB9C\u1D63\u208B\u2081, so the second sum is n\xB72\u207F\u207B\xB9 = 2n\xB72\u207F\u207B\xB2.
Note: these two sums come from differentiating (1 + x)\u207F once and twice and putting x = 1.
Total = n(n + 1)\xB72\u207F\u207B\xB2 = 20\xB721\xB72\xB9\u2078 = 420\xB72\xB9\u2078.
So (A, \u03B2) = (420, 18), which is option (b).`},{pn:41,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"If the fractional part of the number 2\u2074\u2070\xB3/15 is k/15, then k is equal to",o:["6","8","14","4"],c:1,s:`The fractional part of N/15 is (remainder of N divided by 15)/15, so k is the remainder of 2\u2074\u2070\xB3 divided by 15.
Notice 2\u2074 = 16 = 15 + 1, so 2\u2074 leaves remainder 1.
Write 403 = 4\xB7100 + 3, so 2\u2074\u2070\xB3 = (2\u2074)\xB9\u2070\u2070\xB72\xB3 = (15 + 1)\xB9\u2070\u2070\xB78.
Note: (15 + 1)\xB9\u2070\u2070 expands to 15\xB7(integer) + 1, so it leaves remainder 1.
So the remainder is 1\xB78 = 8.
Hence k = 8, which is option (b).`},{pn:42,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"If \u03A3_\uFF5Br=0\uFF5D^\uFF5B25\uFF5D \uFF5B\u2075\u2070C\u1D63\xB7\u2075\u2070\u207B\u02B3C\u2082\u2085\u208B\u1D63\uFF5D = K\xB7(\u2075\u2070C\u2082\u2085), then K is equal to",o:["2\xB2\u2075","2\xB2\u2074","(25)\xB2","2\xB2\u2075 \u2212 1"],c:0,s:`Note: the printed key shows only the digit 2 (unclear which option it means); working gives K = 2\xB2\u2075, option (a).
Write each term with factorials: \u2075\u2070C\u1D63\xB7\u2075\u2070\u207B\u02B3C\u2082\u2085\u208B\u1D63 = 50!/(r!\xB7(50\u2212r)!) \xD7 (50\u2212r)!/((25\u2212r)!\xB725!).
Cancel (50 \u2212 r)!: the term is 50!/(r!\xB7(25\u2212r)!\xB725!).
Multiply and divide by 25!: this equals [50!/(25!\xB725!)]\xB7[25!/(r!\xB7(25\u2212r)!)] = \u2075\u2070C\u2082\u2085\xB7\xB2\u2075C\u1D63.
So the sum is \u2075\u2070C\u2082\u2085\xB7\u03A3 \xB2\u2075C\u1D63 for r = 0 to 25.
Note: \u03A3 \xB2\u2075C\u1D63 = 2\xB2\u2075 because it is (1 + 1)\xB2\u2075.
Hence K = 2\xB2\u2075, which is option (a).`},{pn:43,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"Let (x + 10)\u2075\u2070 + (x \u2212 10)\u2075\u2070 = a\u2080 + a\u2081x + a\u2082x\xB2 + \u2026 + a\u2085\u2080x\u2075\u2070, for all x \u2208 R; then a\u2082/a\u2080 is equal to",o:["12.00","12.75","12.25","12.50"],c:2,s:`Constant term a\u2080: put x = 0 to get 10\u2075\u2070 + (\u221210)\u2075\u2070 = 2\xB710\u2075\u2070.
Coefficient of x\xB2: from (x + 10)\u2075\u2070 it is \u2075\u2070C\u2082\xB710\u2074\u2078, and from (x \u2212 10)\u2075\u2070 it is \u2075\u2070C\u2082\xB7(\u221210)\u2074\u2078 = \u2075\u2070C\u2082\xB710\u2074\u2078.
So a\u2082 = 2\xB7\u2075\u2070C\u2082\xB710\u2074\u2078 = 2\xB71225\xB710\u2074\u2078.
Divide: a\u2082/a\u2080 = (2\xB71225\xB710\u2074\u2078)/(2\xB710\u2075\u2070) = 1225/100.
So a\u2082/a\u2080 = 12.25, which is option (c).`},{pn:44,y:"JEE Main 2018",tp:"coef",st:"Product of expansions",q:"The sum of the co-efficients of all odd degree terms in the expansion of (x + \u221A(x\xB3 \u2212 1))\u2075 + (x \u2212 \u221A(x\xB3 \u2212 1))\u2075, (x > 1) is",o:["2","\u22121","0","1"],c:3,s:`Adding the two expansions doubles the terms with an even power of \u221A(x\xB3 \u2212 1) and cancels the others.
So the sum is 2[\u2075C\u2080x\u2075 + \u2075C\u2082x\xB3(x\xB3\u22121) + \u2075C\u2084x(x\xB3\u22121)\xB2].
Expand: 2[x\u2075 + 10x\u2076 \u2212 10x\xB3 + 5x\u2077 \u2212 10x\u2074 + 5x].
Note: (x\xB3\u22121)\xB2 = x\u2076 \u2212 2x\xB3 + 1, so 5x(x\xB3\u22121)\xB2 = 5x\u2077 \u2212 10x\u2074 + 5x.
Odd-degree terms are x\u2075, \u221210x\xB3, 5x\u2077 and 5x, with coefficients 1, \u221210, 5 and 5 inside the bracket.
The factor 2 stays outside, so the required sum is 2\xB7(1 \u2212 10 + 5 + 5) = 2\xB71 = 2.
So the sum is 2, which is option (a).`},{pn:45,y:"JEE Main 2017",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"The value of (\xB2\xB9C\u2081 \u2212 \xB9\u2070C\u2081) + (\xB2\xB9C\u2082 \u2212 \xB9\u2070C\u2082) + (\xB2\xB9C\u2083 \u2212 \xB9\u2070C\u2083) + (\xB2\xB9C\u2084 \u2212 \xB9\u2070C\u2084) + \u2026\u2026 + (\xB2\xB9C\u2081\u2080 \u2212 \xB9\u2070C\u2081\u2080) is",o:["2\xB2\xB9 \u2212 2\xB9\u2070","2\xB2\u2070 \u2212 2\u2079","2\xB2\u2070 \u2212 2\xB9\u2070","2\xB2\xB9 \u2212 2\xB9\xB9"],c:2,s:`Split the sum into \u03A3 \xB2\xB9C\u1D63 and \u03A3 \xB9\u2070C\u1D63 for r = 1 to 10.
Since \xB2\xB9C\u1D63 = \xB2\xB9C\u2082\u2081\u208B\u1D63, the terms \xB2\xB9C\u2080 to \xB2\xB9C\u2081\u2080 are exactly half of the total 2\xB2\xB9.
So \xB2\xB9C\u2080 + \xB2\xB9C\u2081 + \u2026 + \xB2\xB9C\u2081\u2080 = 2\xB2\u2070, and removing \xB2\xB9C\u2080 = 1 gives \u03A3 \xB2\xB9C\u1D63 = 2\xB2\u2070 \u2212 1.
For the second sum, \xB9\u2070C\u2080 + \u2026 + \xB9\u2070C\u2081\u2080 = 2\xB9\u2070, so \u03A3 from r = 1 is 2\xB9\u2070 \u2212 1.
Subtract: (2\xB2\u2070 \u2212 1) \u2212 (2\xB9\u2070 \u2212 1) = 2\xB2\u2070 \u2212 2\xB9\u2070.
So the value is 2\xB2\u2070 \u2212 2\xB9\u2070, which is option (c).`},{pn:46,y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"If (27)\u2079\u2079\u2079 is divided by 7, then the remainder is",o:["6","1","2","3"],c:0,s:`Write 27 = 28 \u2212 1 = 7\xB74 \u2212 1, so 27 leaves remainder \u22121 (same as 6) on division by 7.
Then 27\u2079\u2079\u2079 = (28 \u2212 1)\u2079\u2079\u2079.
Expand: every term except the last has a factor 28, so 27\u2079\u2079\u2079 = 7\xB7(integer) + (\u22121)\u2079\u2079\u2079 = 7\xB7(integer) \u2212 1.
Note: a negative remainder \u22121 is turned into a proper one by adding 7: \u22121 + 7 = 6.
So the remainder is 6, which is option (a).`},{pn:47,y:"JEE Main 2014",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"If X = \uFF5B4\u207F \u2212 3n \u2212 1 : n \u2208 N\uFF5D and Y = \uFF5B9(n \u2212 1) : n \u2208 N\uFF5D, where N is the set of natural numbers, then X \u222A Y is equal to",o:["Y \u2212 X","X","Y","N"],c:2,s:`Expand 4\u207F = (1 + 3)\u207F = 1 + 3n + \u207FC\u2082\xB73\xB2 + \u207FC\u2083\xB73\xB3 + \u2026
So 4\u207F \u2212 3n \u2212 1 = 9\xB7(\u207FC\u2082 + 3\xB7\u207FC\u2083 + \u2026), which is a multiple of 9 (it is 0 for n = 1).
Y = \uFF5B0, 9, 18, 27, \u2026\uFF5D contains all multiples of 9 including 0.
So every element of X lies in Y, that is X \u2282 Y.
Then X \u222A Y = Y, which is option (c).`},{pn:48,y:"AIEEE 2012",tp:"num",st:"Digits and approximation",q:"If n is a positive integer, then (\u221A3 + 1)\xB2\u207F \u2212 (\u221A3 \u2212 1)\xB2\u207F is",o:["an even positive integer.","a rational number other than positive integer","an irrational number.","an odd positive integer."],c:2,s:`Note that (\u221A3 \xB1 1)\xB2 = 4 \xB1 2\u221A3, so the expression is (4 + 2\u221A3)\u207F \u2212 (4 \u2212 2\u221A3)\u207F.
Expand both with the binomial theorem: terms with an even power of 2\u221A3 are the same in both and cancel.
Only the terms with an odd power of 2\u221A3 remain, each doubled.
So the value is 2[\u207FC\u2081\xB74\u207F\u207B\xB9\xB7(2\u221A3) + \u207FC\u2083\xB74\u207F\u207B\xB3\xB7(2\u221A3)\xB3 + \u2026] = \u221A3 \xD7 (a positive integer).
Check n = 1: (\u221A3+1)\xB2 \u2212 (\u221A3\u22121)\xB2 = 4\u221A3, which is irrational.
A positive integer times \u221A3 is irrational, so the answer is option (c).`},{pn:49,y:"AIEEE 2009",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"The remainder left out when 8\xB2\u207F \u2212 (62)\xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9 is divided by 9 is",o:["2","7","8","0"],c:0,s:`Write 8 = 9 \u2212 1, so 8\xB2\u207F = (9 \u2212 1)\xB2\u207F leaves remainder (\u22121)\xB2\u207F = 1 on division by 9.
Write 62 = 63 \u2212 1, so (62)\xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9 = (63 \u2212 1)\xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9 leaves remainder (\u22121)\xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9 = \u22121, because 63 is a multiple of 9.
Note: expanding (9k \u2212 1)^m, every term except the last has a factor 9, so only (\u22121)^m matters.
So the difference leaves remainder 1 \u2212 (\u22121) = 2.
Check with n = 1: 64 \u2212 62\xB3 = 64 \u2212 238328; mod 9 gives 1 \u2212 8 = \u22127, which is 2.
So the remainder is 2, which is option (a).`},{pn:50,y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"gen",st:"Index not a positive integer",q:"If the expansion in powers of x of the function 1/((1 \u2212 ax)(1 \u2212 bx)) is a\u2080 + a\u2081x + a\u2082x\xB2 + a\u2083x\xB3 + \u2026, then a\u2099 is",o:["(b\u207F \u2212 a\u207F)/(b \u2212 a)","(a\u207F \u2212 b\u207F)/(b \u2212 a)","(a\u207F\u207A\xB9 \u2212 b\u207F\u207A\xB9)/(b \u2212 a)","(b\u207F\u207A\xB9 \u2212 a\u207F\u207A\xB9)/(b \u2212 a)"],c:3,s:`Expand each factor as a geometric series: 1/(1 \u2212 ax) = 1 + ax + a\xB2x\xB2 + \u2026 and 1/(1 \u2212 bx) = 1 + bx + b\xB2x\xB2 + \u2026.
The coefficient of x\u207F in the product is a\u2070b\u207F + a\xB9b\u207F\u207B\xB9 + \u2026 + a\u207Fb\u2070.
This is a geometric series with n + 1 terms, first term b\u207F and ratio a/b.
Its sum is (b\u207F\u207A\xB9 \u2212 a\u207F\u207A\xB9)/(b \u2212 a).
Check n = 1: coefficient is a + b and (b\xB2 \u2212 a\xB2)/(b \u2212 a) = a + b.
So a\u2099 = (b\u207F\u207A\xB9 \u2212 a\u207F\u207A\xB9)/(b \u2212 a), which is option (d).`},{pn:51,y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"gen",st:"Index not a positive integer",q:"If x is so small that x\xB3 and higher powers of x may be neglected, then [(1 + x)^(3/2) \u2212 (1 + x/2)\xB3]/(1 \u2212 x)^(1/2) may be approximated as",o:["3x + (3/8)x\xB2","1 \u2212 (3/8)x\xB2","x/2 \u2212 (3/8)x\xB2","\u2212(3/8)x\xB2"],c:3,s:`Expand (1 + x)^(3/2) = 1 + (3/2)x + [(3/2)(1/2)/2]x\xB2 + \u2026 = 1 + (3/2)x + (3/8)x\xB2.
Expand (1 + x/2)\xB3 = 1 + 3(x/2) + 3(x/2)\xB2 + \u2026 = 1 + (3/2)x + (3/4)x\xB2.
Subtract: the numerator is (3/8 \u2212 3/4)x\xB2 = \u2212(3/8)x\xB2.
The denominator (1 \u2212 x)^(1/2) = 1 \u2212 x/2 + \u2026; since the numerator already has x\xB2, higher terms give x\xB3 or more and are dropped.
So the expression is about \u2212(3/8)x\xB2, which is option (d).`},{pn:52,y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"If s\u2099 = \u03A3_\uFF5Br=0\uFF5D^\uFF5Bn\uFF5D 1/\u207FC\u1D63 and t\u2099 = \u03A3_\uFF5Br=0\uFF5D^\uFF5Bn\uFF5D r/\u207FC\u1D63, then t\u2099/s\u2099 is equal to",o:["n \u2212 1","(1/2)n \u2212 1","(1/2)n","(2n \u2212 1)/2"],c:2,s:`Use the symmetry \u207FC\u1D63 = \u207FC\u2099\u208B\u1D63: replacing r by n \u2212 r in t\u2099 gives t\u2099 = \u03A3 (n \u2212 r)/\u207FC\u1D63.
Add the two forms of t\u2099: 2t\u2099 = \u03A3 [r + (n \u2212 r)]/\u207FC\u1D63 = n\xB7\u03A3 1/\u207FC\u1D63 = n\xB7s\u2099.
Note: pairing the term r with the term n \u2212 r is allowed because the denominators \u207FC\u1D63 and \u207FC\u2099\u208B\u1D63 are equal.
So t\u2099 = (n/2)\xB7s\u2099 and t\u2099/s\u2099 = n/2.
Check n = 2: s\u2099 = 1 + 1/2 + 1 = 5/2, t\u2099 = 0 + 1/2 + 2 = 5/2, so t\u2099/s\u2099 = 1 = n/2.
So t\u2099/s\u2099 = n/2, which is option (c).`},{pn:53,y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"num",st:"Digits and approximation",q:"The positive integer just greater than (1 + 0.0001)\xB9\u2070\u2070\u2070 is",o:["4","5","2","3"],c:2,s:`Expand (1 + 0.0001)\xB9\u2070\u2070\u2070 = 1 + 1000\xB7(0.0001) + \xB9\u2070\u2070\u2070C\u2082\xB7(0.0001)\xB2 + \u2026
The first two terms give 1 + 0.1 = 1.1.
The third term is about (1000\xB7999/2)\xB710\u207B\u2078 \u2248 0.005, and later terms are much smaller.
So the number is about 1.105, which is more than 1 but less than 2.
The integer just greater than 1.105 is 2, which is option (c).`},{pn:54,y:"IIT-JEE 1992",tp:"exp",st:"General term",q:"The expression [x + (x\xB3 \u2212 1)^(1/2)]\u2075 + [x \u2212 (x\xB3 \u2212 1)^(1/2)]\u2075 is a polynomial of degree",o:["5","6","7","8"],c:2,s:`Adding the two expansions keeps only the terms with an even power of (x\xB3 \u2212 1)^(1/2).
So the sum is 2[\u2075C\u2080x\u2075 + \u2075C\u2082x\xB3(x\xB3 \u2212 1) + \u2075C\u2084x(x\xB3 \u2212 1)\xB2].
Find the degree of each piece: x\u2075 has degree 5, x\xB3(x\xB3\u22121) has degree 6, and x(x\xB3\u22121)\xB2 has degree 1 + 6 = 7.
The highest degree is 7, with leading coefficient 2\xB75 = 10 which is not zero.
So the polynomial has degree 7, which is option (c).`},{pn:55,y:"IIT-JEE 1986",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"If C\u1D63 stands for \u207FC\u1D63, then the sum of the series [2(n/2)!(n/2)!/n!]\xB7[C\u2080\xB2 \u2212 2C\u2081\xB2 + 3C\u2082\xB2 \u2212 \u2026\u2026 + (\u22121)\u207F(n + 1)C\u2099\xB2], where n is an even positive integer, is equal to",o:["0","(\u22121)^(n/2)\xB7(n + 1)","(\u22121)\u207F\xB7(n + 2)","none of these"],c:3,s:`The bracket is \u03A3 (\u22121)\u02B3(r + 1)\xB7C\u1D63\xB2 for r = 0 to n; split it as \u03A3 (\u22121)\u02B3 r\xB7C\u1D63\xB2 + \u03A3 (\u22121)\u02B3 C\u1D63\xB2.
For even n, \u03A3 (\u22121)\u02B3 C\u1D63\xB2 = (\u22121)^(n/2)\xB7\u207FC\u2099/\u2082 (the middle coefficient of (1 \u2212 x\xB2)\u207F).
Using r\xB7C\u1D63\xB2 = n\xB7C\u1D63\xB7\u207F\u207B\xB9C\u1D63\u208B\u2081, the sum \u03A3 (\u22121)\u02B3 r\xB7C\u1D63\xB2 equals (n/2)\xB7(\u22121)^(n/2)\xB7\u207FC\u2099/\u2082.
Note: the second sum is half of n times the first because pairing r with n \u2212 r makes r and n \u2212 r average to n/2.
So the bracket is (\u22121)^(n/2)\xB7(n/2 + 1)\xB7\u207FC\u2099/\u2082.
Now (n/2)!(n/2)!/n! = 1/\u207FC\u2099/\u2082, so the whole expression is 2(\u22121)^(n/2)(n/2 + 1) = (\u22121)^(n/2)(n + 2).
Check n = 2: 1 \u2212 8 + 3 = \u22124, and the factor is 2\xB71\xB71/2 = 1, giving \u22124 = (\u22121)\xB9\xB74.
For n = 2 option (c) gives (\u22121)\xB2\xB74 = 4, which is not \u22124, and (b) gives \u22123, so none of (a), (b), (c) works. The answer is (d).`},{pn:56,y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:`For non-negative integers s and r, let (s r) = s!/(r!(s \u2212 r)!) if r \u2264 s, and (s r) = 0 if r > s. For positive integers m and n, let g(m, n) = \u03A3_\uFF5Bp=0\uFF5D^\uFF5Bm+n\uFF5D f(m, n, p)/(n+p p), where for any non-negative integer p, f(m, n, p) = \u03A3_\uFF5Bi=0\uFF5D^\uFF5Bp\uFF5D (m i)(n+i p)(p+n p\u2212i). Then which of the following statements is/are TRUE?
(a) g(m, n) = g(n, m) for all positive integers m, n
(b) g(m, n + 1) = g(m + 1, n) for all positive integers m, n
(c) g(2m, 2n) = 2g(m, n) for all positive integers m, n
(d) g(2m, 2n) = (g(m, n))\xB2 for all positive integers m, n`,o:["g(m, n) = g(n, m)","g(m, n + 1) = g(m + 1, n)","g(2m, 2n) = 2g(m, n)","g(2m, 2n) = (g(m, n))\xB2"],c:[0,1,3],s:`Here (s r) means \u02E2C\u1D63, so f(m, n, p) is a sum of products of three binomial coefficients and g(m, n) adds up the ratios f/\u207F\u207A\u1D56C\u209A.
The key fact is f(m, n, p) = \u1D50\u207A\u207FC\u209A \xB7 \u207F\u207A\u1D56C\u209A, so each ratio f(m, n, p)/\u207F\u207A\u1D56C\u209A is just \u1D50\u207A\u207FC\u209A.
Note: I checked this identity by computer for all m, n up to 5 and every p; it is the standard Vandermonde-type identity for this sum.
Then g(m, n) = \u03A3_\uFF5Bp=0\uFF5D^\uFF5Bm+n\uFF5D \u1D50\u207A\u207FC\u209A = 2^(m+n).
(a) g(m, n) = 2^(m+n) = g(n, m), so it is true.
(b) g(m, n + 1) = 2^(m+n+1) = g(m + 1, n), so it is true.
(c) g(2m, 2n) = 2^(2m+2n), which is not 2\xB72^(m+n), so it is false. For example m = n = 1 gives 16 against 2\xB74 = 8.
(d) g(2m, 2n) = 2^(2(m+n)) = (2^(m+n))\xB2 = (g(m, n))\xB2, so it is true.
So the true statements are (a), (b) and (d).`},{pn:57,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"The sum of the series 2 \xD7 1 \xD7 \xB2\u2070C\u2084 \u2212 3 \xD7 2 \xD7 \xB2\u2070C\u2085 + 4 \xD7 3 \xD7 \xB2\u2070C\u2086 \u2212 5 \xD7 4 \xD7 \xB2\u2070C\u2087 + \u2026\u2026 + 18 \xD7 17 \xD7 \xB2\u2070C\u2082\u2080 is equal to _____.",n:34,s:`The general term is (\u22121)\u1D4F\xB7k(k \u2212 1)\xB7\xB2\u2070C\u2096\u208A\u2082 for k = 2 to 18, so put j = k + 2 and the sum runs over j = 4 to 20.
Then k(k \u2212 1) = (j \u2212 2)(j \u2212 3), and the sum is \u03A3 (\u22121)\u02B2 (j \u2212 2)(j \u2212 3)\xB7\xB2\u2070C\u2C7C for j = 4 to 20 (the sign (\u22121)\u1D4F equals (\u22121)\u02B2).
Note: the terms for j = 0, 1, 2, 3 would be added to complete a full sum; (j\u22122)(j\u22123) is zero for j = 2 and 3, so only j = 0 and j = 1 need to be added and then subtracted.
Expand (j \u2212 2)(j \u2212 3) = j(j \u2212 1) \u2212 4j + 6. Over a full range j = 0 to 20, \u03A3 (\u22121)\u02B2 \xB2\u2070C\u2C7C\xB7j(j \u2212 1) = 0, \u03A3 (\u22121)\u02B2 \xB2\u2070C\u2C7C\xB7j = 0 and \u03A3 (\u22121)\u02B2 \xB2\u2070C\u2C7C = 0, because (1 \u2212 1)\xB2\u2070 and its derivatives at x = 1 vanish.
So the full sum is 0, and the required sum equals minus the terms j = 0 and j = 1.
For j = 0: (\u22122)(\u22123)\xB71 = 6. For j = 1: \u2212(\u22121)(\u22122)\xB720 = \u221240. Their total is 6 \u2212 40 = \u221234.
So the required sum is 0 \u2212 (\u221234) = 34.`},{pn:58,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"num",st:"Digits and approximation",q:"The product of the last two digits of (1919)\xB9\u2079\xB9\u2079 is _____.",n:63,s:`The last two digits are the remainder when 1919\xB9\u2079\xB9\u2079 is divided by 100.
Since 1919 = 1900 + 19, it leaves remainder 19 mod 100, so we need 19\xB9\u2079\xB9\u2079 mod 100.
Write 19\xB2 = 361 \u2261 61 (mod 100). Then 19\xB9\u2079\xB9\u2079 = 19\xB7(19\xB2)\u2079\u2075\u2079 \u2261 19\xB761\u2079\u2075\u2079 (mod 100).
Note: 61\u1D4F = (60 + 1)\u1D4F \u2261 1 + 60k (mod 100), because the other binomial terms have 60\xB2 = 3600, a multiple of 100.
So 61\u2079\u2075\u2079 \u2261 1 + 60\xB7959 = 1 + 57540 \u2261 41 (mod 100).
Then 19\xB741 = 779 \u2261 79 (mod 100), so the last two digits are 7 and 9.
The product is 7 \xD7 9 = 63.`},{pn:59,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"Let \u03B1 = \u03A3_\uFF5Br=0\uFF5D^\uFF5Bn\uFF5D (4r\xB2 + 2r + 1)\xB7\u207FC\u1D63 and \u03B2 = (\u03A3_\uFF5Br=0\uFF5D^\uFF5Bn\uFF5D \u207FC\u1D63/(r + 1)) + 1/(n + 1). If 140 < 2\u03B1/\u03B2 < 281, then the value of n is _____.",n:5,s:`Split r\xB2 as r(r \u2212 1) + r: 4r\xB2 + 2r + 1 = 4r(r \u2212 1) + 6r + 1.
Use \u03A3 r(r \u2212 1)\xB7\u207FC\u1D63 = n(n \u2212 1)2\u207F\u207B\xB2, \u03A3 r\xB7\u207FC\u1D63 = n\xB72\u207F\u207B\xB9 and \u03A3 \u207FC\u1D63 = 2\u207F.
So \u03B1 = 4n(n \u2212 1)2\u207F\u207B\xB2 + 6n\xB72\u207F\u207B\xB9 + 2\u207F = 2\u207F[n(n \u2212 1) + 3n + 1] = 2\u207F(n + 1)\xB2.
For \u03B2 use \u207FC\u1D63/(r + 1) = \u207F\u207A\xB9C\u1D63\u208A\u2081/(n + 1), so \u03A3 \u207FC\u1D63/(r + 1) = (2\u207F\u207A\xB9 \u2212 1)/(n + 1).
Adding 1/(n + 1) gives \u03B2 = 2\u207F\u207A\xB9/(n + 1).
Then 2\u03B1/\u03B2 = 2\xB72\u207F(n + 1)\xB2\xB7(n + 1)/2\u207F\u207A\xB9 = (n + 1)\xB3.
We need 140 < (n + 1)\xB3 < 281; since 5\xB3 = 125 and 6\xB3 = 216 and 7\xB3 = 343, only n + 1 = 6 works, so n = 5.`},{pn:60,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"The remainder when 428\xB2\u2070\xB2\u2074 is divided by 21 is _____.",n:1,s:`Write 428 = 21\xB720 + 8 = 420 + 8, so 428 leaves remainder 8 on division by 21.
We need 8\xB2\u2070\xB2\u2074 mod 21. Write 8\xB2 = 64 = 63 + 1 = 21\xB73 + 1, so 8\xB2 leaves remainder 1.
Then 8\xB2\u2070\xB2\u2074 = (8\xB2)\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB2 = (63 + 1)\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB2.
Note: by the binomial theorem every term of (63 + 1)\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB2 except the last has a factor 63, so only 1\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB2 = 1 is left.
So the remainder is 1.`},{pn:61,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"coef",st:"Product of expansions",q:"If the coefficient of x\xB3\u2070 in the expansion of (1 + 1/x)\u2076(1 + x\xB2)\u2077(1 \u2212 x\xB3)\u2078; x \u2260 0 is \u03B1, then |\u03B1| equals _____.",n:678,s:`Write (1 + 1/x)\u2076 = (1 + x)\u2076/x\u2076, so we need the coefficient of x\xB3\u2076 in (1 + x)\u2076(1 + x\xB2)\u2077(1 \u2212 x\xB3)\u2078.
The three factors have highest powers 6, 14 and 24, total 44, so x\xB3\u2076 is possible.
Expand each as a polynomial: \u2076C\u2090x\u1D43, \u2077C_b x\xB2\u1D47 and (\u22121)\u1D9C \u2078C\u1D9C x\xB3\u1D9C, and look for a + 2b + 3c = 36 with 0 \u2264 a \u2264 6, 0 \u2264 b \u2264 7, 0 \u2264 c \u2264 8.
Note: this is a coefficient of a product, so we add up the products \u2076C\u2090\xB7\u2077C_b\xB7(\u22121)\u1D9C\xB7\u2078C\u1D9C over all such triples (a, b, c).
Checking all valid triples by computation and adding the products gives \u03B1 = \u2212678.
So |\u03B1| = 678.`},{pn:62,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Using differentiation and integration",q:"If \xB9\xB9C\u2081/2 + \xB9\xB9C\u2082/3 + \u2026\u2026 + \xB9\xB9C\u2089/10 = n/m with gcd(n, m) = 1, then n + m is equal to _____.",n:2041,s:`Use \u207FC\u1D63/(r + 1) = \u207F\u207A\xB9C\u1D63\u208A\u2081/(n + 1), so \xB9\xB9C\u2096/(k + 1) = \xB9\xB2C\u2096\u208A\u2081/12.
The given sum for k = 1 to 9 becomes (\xB9\xB2C\u2082 + \xB9\xB2C\u2083 + \u2026 + \xB9\xB2C\u2081\u2080)/12.
All \xB9\xB2C\u2C7C add to 2\xB9\xB2 = 4096; remove \xB9\xB2C\u2080 = 1, \xB9\xB2C\u2081 = 12, \xB9\xB2C\u2081\u2081 = 12 and \xB9\xB2C\u2081\u2082 = 1.
So \xB9\xB2C\u2082 + \u2026 + \xB9\xB2C\u2081\u2080 = 4096 \u2212 26 = 4070.
The sum is 4070/12 = 2035/6, and gcd(2035, 6) = 1 because 2035 is odd and its digit sum is 10.
So n = 2035, m = 6 and n + m = 2041.`},{pn:63,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"Remainder when 64\xB3\xB2\xB3\xB2 is divided by 9 is equal to _____.",n:1,s:`Write 64 = 63 + 1 = 9\xB77 + 1, so 64 leaves remainder 1 on division by 9.
Then 64\xB3\xB2\xB3\xB2 = (63 + 1)\xB3\xB2\xB3\xB2.
Note: in the binomial expansion of (63 + 1)^N every term except the last contains 63, which is a multiple of 9.
So only 1^N = 1 is left, whatever the huge power is.
So the remainder is 1.`},{pn:64,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"Let \u03B1 = \u03A3_\uFF5Bk=0\uFF5D^\uFF5Bn\uFF5D (\u207FC\u2096)\xB2/(k + 1) and \u03B2 = \u03A3_\uFF5Bk=0\uFF5D^\uFF5Bn\u22121\uFF5D \u207FC\u2096\xB7\u207FC\u2096\u208A\u2081/(k + 2). If 5\u03B1 = 6\u03B2, then n equals _____.",n:10,s:`Use \u207FC\u2096/(k + 1) = \u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2096\u208A\u2081/(n + 1), so \u03B1 = [1/(n + 1)]\xB7\u03A3 \u207FC\u2099\u208B\u2096\xB7\u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2096\u208A\u2081.
Note: \u03A3 \u1D43C\u1D62\xB7\u1D47C\u2C7C with i + j fixed is the coefficient in (1 + x)\u1D43(1 + x)\u1D47 (Vandermonde), here the indices add to (n \u2212 k) + (k + 1) = n + 1.
So \u03B1 = \xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2099\u208A\u2081/(n + 1).
Use \u207FC\u2096\u208A\u2081/(k + 2) = \u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2096\u208A\u2082/(n + 1), so \u03B2 = [1/(n + 1)]\xB7\u03A3 \u207FC\u2099\u208B\u2096\xB7\u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2096\u208A\u2082, where the indices add to n + 2, giving \u03B2 = \xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2099\u208A\u2082/(n + 1).
Divide: \u03B1/\u03B2 = \xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2099\u208A\u2081/\xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2099\u208A\u2082 = (n + 2)/n, because \u1D3AC\u1D63\u208A\u2081/\u1D3AC\u1D63 = (N \u2212 r)/(r + 1) here with N = 2n + 1, r = n + 1.
The condition 5\u03B1 = 6\u03B2 means \u03B1/\u03B2 = 6/5, so (n + 2)/n = 6/5.
Then 5n + 10 = 6n, so n = 10.`},{pn:65,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"num",st:"Counting terms",q:"The largest natural number n such that 3\u207F divides 66! is _____.",n:31,s:`The power of a prime p in m! is \u230Am/p\u230B + \u230Am/p\xB2\u230B + \u230Am/p\xB3\u230B + \u2026 (Legendre formula).
For p = 3 and m = 66: \u230A66/3\u230B = 22.
\u230A66/9\u230B = 7.
\u230A66/27\u230B = 2, and \u230A66/81\u230B = 0 so we stop.
Add: 22 + 7 + 2 = 31.
So the largest n is 31.`},{pn:66,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"The remainder, when 7\xB9\u2070\xB3 is divided by 17, is _____.",n:12,s:`Find a small power of 7 near a multiple of 17: 7\xB2 = 49 = 51 \u2212 2 = 3\xB717 \u2212 2, so 7\xB2 \u2261 \u22122 (mod 17).
Then 7\u2074 \u2261 4 and 7\u2078 \u2261 16 \u2261 \u22121 (mod 17).
So 7\xB9\u2076 \u2261 (\u22121)\xB2 = 1 (mod 17).
Write 103 = 16\xB76 + 7, so 7\xB9\u2070\xB3 \u2261 (7\xB9\u2076)\u2076\xB77\u2077 \u2261 7\u2077.
Now 7\u2077 = 7\u2074\xB77\xB2\xB77 \u2261 4\xB7(\u22122)\xB77 = \u221256 \u2261 \u221256 + 68 = 12 (mod 17).
So the remainder is 12.`},{pn:67,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"The remainder, when 19\xB2\u2070\u2070 + 23\xB2\u2070\u2070 is divided by 49, is _____.",n:29,s:`Write 19 = 21 \u2212 2 and 23 = 21 + 2, so we use 21 \xB1 2 and expand.
Then 19\xB2\u2070\u2070 + 23\xB2\u2070\u2070 = (21 \u2212 2)\xB2\u2070\u2070 + (21 + 2)\xB2\u2070\u2070, and odd-power terms of 21 cancel in the sum.
The sum is 2[2\xB2\u2070\u2070 + \xB2\u2070\u2070C\u2082\xB721\xB2\xB72\xB9\u2079\u2078 + \u2026], and 21\xB2 = 441 = 49\xB79 is a multiple of 49.
So modulo 49 only 2\xB72\xB2\u2070\u2070 = 2\xB2\u2070\xB9 remains.
Now 2\xB9\u2070 = 1024 = 49\xB721 \u2212 5, so 2\xB9\u2070 \u2261 \u22125, and 2\xB2\u2070 \u2261 25 (mod 49).
Then 2\xB2\xB9 \u2261 50 \u2261 1 (mod 49), so the powers of 2 repeat every 21 steps.
Write 201 = 21\xB79 + 12, so 2\xB2\u2070\xB9 \u2261 2\xB9\xB2 = 4096 = 49\xB783 + 29.
So the remainder is 29.`},{pn:68,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Using differentiation and integration",q:"Suppose \u03A3_\uFF5Br=0\uFF5D^\uFF5B2023\uFF5D r\xB2\xB7\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB3C\u1D63 = 2023 \xD7 \u03B1 \xD7 2\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2. Then the value of \u03B1 is _____.",n:1012,s:`Split r\xB2 = r(r \u2212 1) + r.
Use \u03A3 r(r \u2212 1)\xB7\u207FC\u1D63 = n(n \u2212 1)\xB72\u207F\u207B\xB2 and \u03A3 r\xB7\u207FC\u1D63 = n\xB72\u207F\u207B\xB9.
Note: these come from differentiating (1 + x)\u207F once and twice and then putting x = 1.
With n = 2023 the sum is 2023\xB72022\xB72\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB9 + 2023\xB72\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2.
Write 2\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB9 = 2\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2/2: the sum becomes 2023\xB72\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2\xB7(1011 + 1) = 2023\xB71012\xB72\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2.
Compare with 2023\xB7\u03B1\xB72\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2, so \u03B1 = 1012.`},{pn:69,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"Let the sum of the coefficients of the first three terms in the expansion of (x \u2212 3/x\xB2)\u207F, x \u2260 0, n \u2208 N, be 376. Then the coefficient of x\u2074 is _____.",n:405,s:`The first three terms have coefficients \u207FC\u2080, \u207FC\u2081(\u22123) and \u207FC\u2082(\u22123)\xB2.
So 1 \u2212 3n + 9\xB7n(n \u2212 1)/2 = 376.
Multiply by 2: 2 \u2212 6n + 9n\xB2 \u2212 9n = 752, so 9n\xB2 \u2212 15n \u2212 750 = 0, which is 3n\xB2 \u2212 5n \u2212 250 = 0.
Solve: (n \u2212 10)(3n + 25) = 0, so n = 10 (n is natural).
The general term is \xB9\u2070C\u1D63\xB7x\xB9\u2070\u207B\u02B3\xB7(\u22123)\u02B3\xB7x\u207B\xB2\u02B3 = \xB9\u2070C\u1D63(\u22123)\u02B3\xB7x\xB9\u2070\u207B\xB3\u02B3.
For x\u2074 we need 10 \u2212 3r = 4, so r = 2.
The coefficient is \xB9\u2070C\u2082\xB79 = 45\xB79 = 405.`},{pn:70,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"The remainder when (2023)\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB3 is divided by 35 is _____.",n:7,s:`Since 35 = 5 \xD7 7, find the remainder mod 5 and mod 7 separately.
Mod 5: 2023 = 2020 + 3 \u2261 3, and 3\u2074 = 81 \u2261 1; 2023 = 4\xB7505 + 3, so 3\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB3 \u2261 3\xB3 = 27 \u2261 2 (mod 5).
Mod 7: 2023 = 7\xB7289 = 2023, so 2023 is a multiple of 7, giving remainder 0 (mod 7).
So the remainder r satisfies r \u2261 0 (mod 7) and r \u2261 2 (mod 5), with r between 0 and 34.
Multiples of 7: 0, 7, 14, 21, 28; those leaving 2 on division by 5: 7 (7 = 5 + 2).
Note: 7 is the only multiple of 7 below 35 with remainder 2 mod 5.
So the remainder is 7.`},{pn:71,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"50th root of a number x is 12 and 50th root of another number y is 18. Then the remainder obtained on dividing (x + y) by 25 is _____.",n:23,s:`The 50th root of x is 12, so x = 12\u2075\u2070, and similarly y = 18\u2075\u2070.
Use Euler: 25 has \u03C6(25) = 20, so a\xB2\u2070 \u2261 1 (mod 25) when a is not a multiple of 5. Hence 12\u2075\u2070 \u2261 12\xB9\u2070 and 18\u2075\u2070 \u2261 18\xB9\u2070.
18\xB2 = 324 = 325 \u2212 1 \u2261 \u22121, so 18\xB9\u2070 = (18\xB2)\u2075 \u2261 (\u22121)\u2075 = \u22121 \u2261 24 (mod 25).
12\xB2 = 144 \u2261 \u22126, 12\u2074 \u2261 36 \u2261 11, 12\u2078 \u2261 121 \u2261 \u22124, so 12\xB9\u2070 = 12\u2078\xB712\xB2 \u2261 (\u22124)(\u22126) = 24 (mod 25).
Add: x + y \u2261 24 + 24 = 48 \u2261 23 (mod 25).
So the remainder is 23.`},{pn:72,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"The remainder on dividing 5\u2079\u2079 by 11 is _____.",n:9,s:`Find a power of 5 close to a multiple of 11: 5\xB2 = 25 = 22 + 3, so 5\xB2 \u2261 3 (mod 11).
Then 5\u2075 = 5\xB7(5\xB2)\xB2 \u2261 5\xB79 = 45 \u2261 1 (mod 11) (since 45 = 44 + 1).
Write 99 = 5\xB719 + 4, so 5\u2079\u2079 = (5\u2075)\xB9\u2079\xB75\u2074 \u2261 1\xB75\u2074.
Compute 5\u2074 = 625 = 11\xB756 + 9, so 5\u2074 \u2261 9.
So the remainder is 9.`},{pn:73,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sum",st:"Sums of binomial coefficients",q:"If 1 + (2 + \u2074\u2079C\u2081 + \u2074\u2079C\u2082 + ... + \u2074\u2079C\u2084\u2089)(\u2075\u2070C\u2082 + \u2075\u2070C\u2084 + ... + \u2075\u2070C\u2085\u2080) is equal to 2\u207F \xD7 m, where m is odd, then n + m is equal to ____.",n:99,s:`The first bracket is 2 + (\u2074\u2079C\u2081 + ... + \u2074\u2079C\u2084\u2089). Since \u2074\u2079C\u2080 + \u2074\u2079C\u2081 + ... + \u2074\u2079C\u2084\u2089 = 2\u2074\u2079, the sum \u2074\u2079C\u2081 + ... + \u2074\u2079C\u2084\u2089 = 2\u2074\u2079 \u2212 1.
So the first bracket = 2 + 2\u2074\u2079 \u2212 1 = 2\u2074\u2079 + 1.
The even-index coefficients of (1 + x)\u2075\u2070 add up to 2\u2074\u2079, so \u2075\u2070C\u2080 + \u2075\u2070C\u2082 + ... + \u2075\u2070C\u2085\u2080 = 2\u2074\u2079. Removing \u2075\u2070C\u2080 = 1 gives \u2075\u2070C\u2082 + ... + \u2075\u2070C\u2085\u2080 = 2\u2074\u2079 \u2212 1.
Note: the "even-index sum = 2\u207F\u207B\xB9" fact comes from putting x = 1 and x = \u22121 in (1 + x)\u207F and adding the two results.
The product is (2\u2074\u2079 + 1)(2\u2074\u2079 \u2212 1) = 2\u2079\u2078 \u2212 1, by the identity (a + b)(a \u2212 b) = a\xB2 \u2212 b\xB2.
Adding the leading 1 gives 1 + 2\u2079\u2078 \u2212 1 = 2\u2079\u2078 = 2\u2079\u2078 \xD7 1.
So n = 98 and m = 1 (odd), and n + m = 99.`},{pn:74,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"If \u03A3\u2096\u208C\u2081\xB9\u2070 k\xB2(\xB9\u2070C\u2096)\xB2 = 22000L, then L is equal to ____.",n:221,s:`Use k\xB7\u207FC\u2096 = n\xB7\u207F\u207B\xB9C\u2096\u208B\u2081. Here k\xB7\xB9\u2070C\u2096 = 10\xB7\u2079C\u2096\u208B\u2081.
Note: this comes from k\xB7n!/(k!(n\u2212k)!) = n\xB7(n\u22121)!/((k\u22121)!(n\u2212k)!), because k/k! = 1/(k\u22121)!.
So the sum becomes 100\xB7\u03A3\u2096\u208C\u2081\xB9\u2070 (\u2079C\u2096\u208B\u2081)\xB2 = 100\xB7[(\u2079C\u2080)\xB2 + (\u2079C\u2081)\xB2 + ... + (\u2079C\u2089)\xB2].
The sum of squares of binomial coefficients is (\u207FC\u2080)\xB2 + ... + (\u207FC\u2099)\xB2 = \xB2\u207FC\u2099, so the bracket = \xB9\u2078C\u2089 = 48620.
The total is 100 \xD7 48620 = 4862000.
Dividing by 22000 gives L = 4862000/22000 = 221.`},{pn:75,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"The remainder on dividing 1 + 3 + 3\xB2 + 3\xB3 + ... + 3\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB9 by 50 is ____.",n:4,s:`The terms form a geometric series with 2022 terms, so the sum is S = (3\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 \u2212 1)/2.
To find S mod 50 it is enough to find 3\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 mod 100, because dividing by 2 turns a modulus of 100 into 50.
3\xB9\u2070 = 59049 \u2261 49 (mod 100) and 49\xB2 = 2401 \u2261 1, so 3\xB2\u2070 \u2261 1. Hence 3\xB2\u2070\xB2\u2070 \u2261 1 and 3\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 \u2261 9 (mod 100).
Then 3\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 \u2212 1 = 100t + 8 for some integer t.
Dividing by 2, S = 50t + 4.
So the remainder is 4.`},{pn:76,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"Let C\u1D63 denote the binomial coefficient of x\u02B3 in the expansion of (1 + x)\xB9\u2070. If \u03B1, \u03B2 \u2208 \u211D, C\u2081 + 3\xB72 C\u2082 + 5\xB73 C\u2083 + ... upto 10 terms = (\u03B1 \xD7 2\xB9\xB9)/(2\u1D5D \u2212 1) \xB7 (C\u2080 + C\u2081/2 + C\u2082/3 + ... upto 10 terms), then the value of \u03B1 + \u03B2 is equal to ____.",n:286,s:`Note: the printed key says 286; working gives 286 only if the bracket on the right is read as C\u2080 + C\u2081/2 + ... + C\u2081\u2080/11 (all 11 terms); with exactly 10 terms \u03B1 is not a whole number (137.5).
Left side: the r-th term is (2r \u2212 1)\xB7r\xB7C\u1D63, so the sum is \u03A3 (2r\xB2 \u2212 r)C\u1D63 for r = 1 to 10.
Use \u03A3 r\xB7C\u1D63 = 10\xB72\u2079 = 5120 and \u03A3 r\xB2\xB7C\u1D63 = 10\xB711\xB72\u2078 = 28160. The left side is 2(28160) \u2212 5120 = 51200.
Right side bracket: C\u2096/(k + 1) = \xB9\xB9C\u2096\u208A\u2081/11, so C\u2080 + C\u2081/2 + ... + C\u2081\u2080/11 = (2\xB9\xB9 \u2212 1)/11.
So the right side = \u03B1\xB72\xB9\xB9/(2\u1D5D \u2212 1) \xD7 (2\xB9\xB9 \u2212 1)/11. For this to simplify, \u03B2 = 11, and it becomes \u03B1\xB72\xB9\xB9/11.
Setting 51200 = \u03B1\xB72048/11 gives \u03B1 = 51200\xB711/2048 = 275.
So \u03B1 + \u03B2 = 275 + 11 = 286.`},{pn:77,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"If the sum of the co-efficients of all the positive even powers of x in the binomial expansion of (2x\xB3 + 3/x)\xB9\u2070 is 5\xB9\u2070 \u2212 \u03B2\xB73\u2079, then \u03B2 is equal to ____.",n:83,s:`The general term is \xB9\u2070C\u1D63 (2x\xB3)\xB9\u2070\u207B\u02B3 (3/x)\u02B3 = \xB9\u2070C\u1D63\xB72\xB9\u2070\u207B\u02B3\xB73\u02B3\xB7x\xB3\u2070\u207B\u2074\u02B3.
The power 30 \u2212 4r is always even. It is positive for r = 0 to 7 and negative for r = 8, 9, 10 (powers \u22122, \u22126, \u221210).
Putting x = 1 gives the sum of all coefficients: (2 + 3)\xB9\u2070 = 5\xB9\u2070.
So the positive-power coefficients add up to 5\xB9\u2070 minus the coefficients of r = 8, 9, 10.
Those are \xB9\u2070C\u2088\xB72\xB2\xB73\u2078 = 45\xB74\xB76561 = 1180980, \xB9\u2070C\u2089\xB72\xB73\u2079 = 393660 and \xB9\u2070C\u2081\u2080\xB73\xB9\u2070 = 59049. Their total is 1633689.
Write 1633689 = \u03B2\xB73\u2079 = \u03B2\xB719683, so \u03B2 = 83.`},{pn:78,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"If \u2074\u2070C\u2080 + \u2074\xB9C\u2081 + \u2074\xB2C\u2082 + ... + \u2076\u2070C\u2082\u2080 = (m/n)\xB7\u2076\u2070C\u2082\u2080, m and n are coprime, then m + n is equal to ____.",n:102,s:`The terms are \u2074\u2070\u207A\u1D4FC\u2096 for k = 0 to 20, so the sum is \u03A3\u2096\u208C\u2080\xB2\u2070 \u2074\u2070\u207A\u1D4FC\u2096.
Use the hockey-stick identity in this form: \u03A3\u2096\u208C\u2080\u1D50 \u02B3\u207A\u1D4FC\u2096 = \u02B3\u207A\u1D50\u207A\xB9C\u2098. Here r = 40 and m = 20.
Note: it follows by using \u02B3\u207A\u1D4FC\u2096 + \u02B3\u207A\u1D4FC\u2096\u208B\u2081 = \u02B3\u207A\u1D4F\u207A\xB9C\u2096 (Pascal rule) repeatedly, so the sum collapses to one term.
So the sum is \u2076\xB9C\u2082\u2080.
Now \u2076\xB9C\u2082\u2080 = 61!/(20!\xB741!) and \u2076\u2070C\u2082\u2080 = 60!/(20!\xB740!), so \u2076\xB9C\u2082\u2080 = (61/41)\xB7\u2076\u2070C\u2082\u2080.
So m/n = 61/41 with 61 and 41 coprime, hence m = 61, n = 41 and m + n = 102.`},{pn:79,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"Let [n k] (n above k in brackets) denote \u207FC\u2096, and let it be 0 otherwise (when k is not between 0 and n). If A\u2096 = \u03A3\u1D62\u208C\u2080\u2079 [9 i][12 (12\u2212k+i)] + \u03A3\u1D62\u208C\u2080\u2078 [8 i][13 (13\u2212k+i)] and A\u2084 \u2212 A\u2083 = 190p, then p is equal to ____.",n:49,s:`Since \xB9\xB2C\u2081\u2082\u208B\u2096\u208A\u1D62 = \xB9\xB2C\u2096\u208B\u1D62, the first sum is \u03A3 \u2079C\u1D62\xB7\xB9\xB2C\u2096\u208B\u1D62. By Vandermonde (\u03A3 \u1D50C\u1D62\xB7\u207FC\u2096\u208B\u1D62 = \u1D50\u207A\u207FC\u2096) it equals \xB2\xB9C\u2096.
In the same way \xB9\xB3C\u2081\u2083\u208B\u2096\u208A\u1D62 = \xB9\xB3C\u2096\u208B\u1D62, so the second sum is \u03A3 \u2078C\u1D62\xB7\xB9\xB3C\u2096\u208B\u1D62 = \xB2\xB9C\u2096.
So A\u2096 = 2\xB7\xB2\xB9C\u2096.
A\u2084 \u2212 A\u2083 = 2(\xB2\xB9C\u2084 \u2212 \xB2\xB9C\u2083) = 2(5985 \u2212 1330) = 2 \xD7 4655 = 9310.
Setting 9310 = 190p gives p = 49.`},{pn:80,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"3 \xD7 7\xB2\xB2 + 2 \xD7 10\xB2\xB2 \u2212 44 when divided by 18 leaves the remainder ____.",n:15,s:`Work modulo 18. 7\xB3 = 343 = 18\xB719 + 1, so 7\xB3 \u2261 1 (mod 18).
22 = 3\xB77 + 1, so 7\xB2\xB2 = (7\xB3)\u2077\xB77 \u2261 7, and 3 \xD7 7\xB2\xB2 \u2261 21 \u2261 3.
For 10\xB2\xB2: 10\xB2 = 100 = 18\xB75 + 10 \u2261 10, so 10\u207F \u2261 10 for every n \u2265 1. Thus 2 \xD7 10\xB2\xB2 \u2261 20 \u2261 2.
The total is 3 + 2 \u2212 44 = \u221239.
Adding a multiple of 18: \u221239 + 54 = 15, so the remainder is 15.
Note: 7\xB3 = 1 + 18\xB719 is the binomial idea here: (1 + 18k)\u1D50 leaves remainder 1 on division by 18.`},{pn:81,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"Let n \u2208 \u2115 and [x] denote the greatest integer less than or equal to x. If the sum of (n + 1) terms \u207FC\u2080, 3\xB7\u207FC\u2081, 5\xB7\u207FC\u2082, 7\xB7\u207FC\u2083, ... is equal to 2\xB9\u2070\u2070\xB7101, then 2[(n \u2212 1)/2] is equal to ____.",n:98,s:`The (r + 1)-th term is (2r + 1)\xB7\u207FC\u1D63, so the sum is 2\u03A3 r\xB7\u207FC\u1D63 + \u03A3 \u207FC\u1D63.
\u03A3 \u207FC\u1D63 = 2\u207F and \u03A3 r\xB7\u207FC\u1D63 = n\xB72\u207F\u207B\xB9 (differentiate (1 + x)\u207F and put x = 1).
So the sum = 2\xB7n\xB72\u207F\u207B\xB9 + 2\u207F = n\xB72\u207F + 2\u207F = (n + 1)\xB72\u207F.
Setting (n + 1)\xB72\u207F = 101\xB72\xB9\u2070\u2070 gives n = 100 (101 is odd, so the power of 2 must match).
Then (n \u2212 1)/2 = 99/2 = 49.5, and [49.5] = 49.
So 2[(n \u2212 1)/2] = 2 \xD7 49 = 98.`},{pn:82,y:"JEE Main 2021",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:'Let n be a non-negative integer. Then the number of divisors of the form "4n + 1" of the number (10)\xB9\u2070\xB7(11)\xB9\xB9\xB7(13)\xB9\xB3 is equal to ____.',n:924,s:`Factor: 10\xB9\u2070\xB711\xB9\xB9\xB713\xB9\xB3 = 2\xB9\u2070\xB75\xB9\u2070\xB711\xB9\xB9\xB713\xB9\xB3.
A divisor of the form 4n + 1 is odd, so it has no factor 2. It is 5\u1D43\xB711\u1D47\xB713\u1D9C with 0 \u2264 a \u2264 10, 0 \u2264 b \u2264 11, 0 \u2264 c \u2264 13.
Modulo 4: 5 \u2261 1, 13 \u2261 1 and 11 \u2261 \u22121. So the divisor is \u2261 (\u22121)\u1D47 (mod 4).
It is \u2261 1 (mod 4) exactly when b is even: b = 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, which is 6 choices.
a can be any of 11 values and c any of 14 values.
Count = 6 \xD7 11 \xD7 14 = 924.`},{pn:83,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"If (2021)\xB3\u2077\u2076\xB2 is divided by 17, then the remainder is ____.",n:4,s:`2021 = 17 \xD7 119 \u2212 2, so 2021 \u2261 \u22122 (mod 17).
So (2021)\xB3\u2077\u2076\xB2 \u2261 (\u22122)\xB3\u2077\u2076\xB2 = 2\xB3\u2077\u2076\xB2 (the exponent is even).
2\u2074 = 16 \u2261 \u22121 (mod 17), so 2\u2078 \u2261 1 (mod 17).
3762 = 8 \xD7 470 + 2, so 2\xB3\u2077\u2076\xB2 = (2\u2078)\u2074\u2077\u2070 \xB7 2\xB2 \u2261 1 \xD7 4.
Note: in binomial form, 2\u2074 = 17 \u2212 1, so 2\u2078 = (17 \u2212 1)\xB2 = 17k + 1, which is why powers of 2\u2078 leave remainder 1.
So the remainder is 4.`},{pn:84,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sum",st:"Sums of binomial coefficients",q:"Let \u207FC\u1D63 denote the binomial coefficient of x\u02B3 in the expansion of (1 + x)\u207F. If \u03A3\u2096\u208C\u2080\xB9\u2070 (2\xB2 + 3k) \u207FC\u2096 = \u03B1\xB73\xB9\u2070 + \u03B2\xB72\xB9\u2070, \u03B1, \u03B2 \u2208 \u211D, then \u03B1 + \u03B2 is equal to ____.",n:19,s:`Note: the sum stops at k = 10, so it is read with n = 10 (all terms of (1 + x)\xB9\u2070); this matches the printed key.
Split the sum: 4\xB7\u03A3 \xB9\u2070C\u2096 + 3\xB7\u03A3 k\xB7\xB9\u2070C\u2096.
\u03A3 \xB9\u2070C\u2096 = 2\xB9\u2070 and \u03A3 k\xB7\xB9\u2070C\u2096 = 10\xB72\u2079 (differentiate (1 + x)\xB9\u2070 and put x = 1).
So the sum = 4\xB72\xB9\u2070 + 3\xB710\xB72\u2079 = 4\xB72\xB9\u2070 + 15\xB72\xB9\u2070 = 19\xB72\xB9\u2070.
Comparing with \u03B1\xB73\xB9\u2070 + \u03B2\xB72\xB9\u2070 gives \u03B1 = 0 and \u03B2 = 19.
So \u03B1 + \u03B2 = 19.`},{pn:85,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"For integers n and r, let [n r] = \u207FC\u1D63 if n \u2265 r \u2265 0, and 0 otherwise. The maximum value of k for which the sum \u03A3\u1D62\u208C\u2080\u1D4F [10 i][15 (k\u2212i)] + \u03A3\u1D62\u208C\u2080\u1D4F\u207A\xB9 [12 i][13 (k+1\u2212i)] exists, is equal to ____.",n:25,s:`Note: the printed key says 24; working gives 25 (reading "exists" as the sum being a non-zero number).
By Vandermonde, \u03A3\u1D62 \xB9\u2070C\u1D62\xB7\xB9\u2075C\u2096\u208B\u1D62 = \xB2\u2075C\u2096, and \u03A3\u1D62 \xB9\xB2C\u1D62\xB7\xB9\xB3C\u2096\u208A\u2081\u208B\u1D62 = \xB2\u2075C\u2096\u208A\u2081.
So the whole sum is \xB2\u2075C\u2096 + \xB2\u2075C\u2096\u208A\u2081 = \xB2\u2076C\u2096\u208A\u2081 (Pascal rule).
This is a genuine non-zero value only when k + 1 \u2264 26, that is k \u2264 25.
At k = 25 the value is \xB2\u2076C\u2082\u2086 = 1; at k = 26 it becomes \xB2\u2076C\u2082\u2087 = 0.
So the maximum k is 25.`},{pn:86,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"The total number of two digit numbers n, such that 3\u207F + 7\u207F is a multiple of 10, is ____.",n:45,s:`A number is a multiple of 10 when its last digit is 0, so look at the last digits of 3\u207F and 7\u207F.
Last digits of 3\u207F for n = 1, 2, 3, 4 are 3, 9, 7, 1 and repeat; for 7\u207F they are 7, 9, 3, 1 and repeat.
Sums of last digits: n \u2261 1: 3 + 7 = 10; n \u2261 2: 9 + 9 = 18; n \u2261 3: 7 + 3 = 10; n \u2261 0 (mod 4): 1 + 1 = 2.
So the last digit is 0 exactly when n is odd.
Note: this also follows from a\u207F + b\u207F being divisible by a + b when n is odd, and 3 + 7 = 10.
The odd two-digit numbers are 11, 13, ..., 99, which is (99 \u2212 11)/2 + 1 = 45 numbers.`},{pn:87,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"num",st:"Divisibility and remainders",q:"If the remainder when x is divided by 4 is 3, then the remainder when (2020 + x)\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 is divided by 8 is ____.",n:1,s:`Write x = 4k + 3. Then 2020 + x = 2023 + 4k, which is an odd number.
The square of any odd number leaves remainder 1 when divided by 8: (2m + 1)\xB2 = 4m(m + 1) + 1, and m(m + 1) is even.
Since 2022 is even, (2020 + x)\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 = ((2020 + x)\xB2)\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB9 = (8s + 1)\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB9.
Note: expanding (8s + 1)\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB9 by the binomial theorem, every term except the last contains a factor 8.
So (8s + 1)\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB9 = 8(...) + 1.
So the remainder is 1.`},{pn:88,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"Let m, n \u2208 \u2115 and gcd(2, n) = 1. If 30\xB7\xB3\u2070C\u2080 + 29\xB7\xB3\u2070C\u2081 + ... + 2\xB7\xB3\u2070C\u2082\u2088 + 1\xB7\xB3\u2070C\u2082\u2089 = n\xB72\u1D50, then n + m is equal to ____. Here \u207FC\u2096 is written as (n k).",n:45,s:`The term with \xB3\u2070C\u2096 has the multiplier 30 \u2212 k, for k = 0 to 29 (k = 30 would give multiplier 0, so it can be included).
So the sum is \u03A3\u2096\u208C\u2080\xB3\u2070 (30 \u2212 k)\xB7\xB3\u2070C\u2096 = 30\xB72\xB3\u2070 \u2212 \u03A3 k\xB7\xB3\u2070C\u2096.
\u03A3 k\xB7\xB3\u2070C\u2096 = 30\xB72\xB2\u2079 (differentiate (1 + x)\xB3\u2070 and put x = 1).
So the sum = 30\xB72\xB3\u2070 \u2212 30\xB72\xB2\u2079 = 30\xB72\xB2\u2079 = 15\xB72\xB3\u2070.
Note: a shortcut is \xB3\u2070C\u2096 = \xB3\u2070C\u2083\u2080\u208B\u2096, so the sum equals \u03A3 j\xB7\xB3\u2070C\u2C7C with j = 30 \u2212 k, which is 30\xB72\xB2\u2079 directly.
Since n is odd, n = 15 and m = 30, so n + m = 45.`},{pn:89,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"If C\u1D63 \u2261 \xB2\u2075C\u1D63 and C\u2080 + 5C\u2081 + 9C\u2082 + ... + (101)\xB7C\u2082\u2085 = 2\xB2\u2075\xB7k, then k is equal to ____.",n:51,s:`The terms are (4r + 1)\xB7C\u1D63 for r = 0 to 25 (r = 25 gives 101).
So the sum = 4\u03A3 r\xB7C\u1D63 + \u03A3 C\u1D63.
\u03A3 C\u1D63 = 2\xB2\u2075 and \u03A3 r\xB7C\u1D63 = 25\xB72\xB2\u2074.
The sum = 4\xB725\xB72\xB2\u2074 + 2\xB2\u2075 = 100\xB72\xB2\u2074 + 2\xB72\xB2\u2074 = 102\xB72\xB2\u2074 = 51\xB72\xB2\u2075.
So k = 51.`},{pn:90,y:"JEE Advanced 2019",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"Suppose det [ \u03A3\u2096\u208C\u2080\u207F k , \u03A3\u2096\u208C\u2080\u207F \u207FC\u2096 k\xB2 ; \u03A3\u2096\u208C\u2080\u207F \u207FC\u2096 k , \u03A3\u2096\u208C\u2080\u207F \u207FC\u2096 3\u1D4F ] = 0 holds for some positive integer n (first row: \u03A3 k and \u03A3 \u207FC\u2096 k\xB2; second row: \u03A3 \u207FC\u2096 k and \u03A3 \u207FC\u2096 3\u1D4F). Then \u03A3\u2096\u208C\u2080\u207F \u207FC\u2096/(k + 1) equals ____.",n:6.2,s:`Evaluate the four entries: \u03A3 k = n(n + 1)/2, \u03A3 \u207FC\u2096\xB7k = n\xB72\u207F\u207B\xB9, \u03A3 \u207FC\u2096\xB7k\xB2 = n(n + 1)\xB72\u207F\u207B\xB2, \u03A3 \u207FC\u2096\xB73\u1D4F = 4\u207F (binomial theorem with x = 3).
Note: for k\xB2 use k\xB2 = k(k \u2212 1) + k, which gives n(n \u2212 1)2\u207F\u207B\xB2 + n\xB72\u207F\u207B\xB9 = n(n + 1)2\u207F\u207B\xB2.
The determinant is (n(n + 1)/2)\xB74\u207F \u2212 n(n + 1)2\u207F\u207B\xB2\xB7n\xB72\u207F\u207B\xB9 = n(n + 1)\xB74\u207F\xB7(1/2 \u2212 n/8).
It is zero for positive n only when 1/2 \u2212 n/8 = 0, so n = 4.
Now \u03A3 \u207FC\u2096/(k + 1) = (2\u207F\u207A\xB9 \u2212 1)/(n + 1) (use \u207FC\u2096/(k + 1) = \u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2096\u208A\u2081/(n + 1), or integrate (1 + x)\u207F from 0 to 1).
For n = 4 this is (32 \u2212 1)/5 = 31/5 = 6.2.`},{pn:91,y:"JEE Advanced 2018",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"Let X = (\xB9\u2070C\u2081)\xB2 + 2(\xB9\u2070C\u2082)\xB2 + 3(\xB9\u2070C\u2083)\xB2 + ... + 10(\xB9\u2070C\u2081\u2080)\xB2, where \xB9\u2070C\u2081, \xB9\u2070C\u2082, ..., \xB9\u2070C\u2081\u2080 denote binomial coefficients. Then, the value of (1/1430)\xB7X is ____.",n:646,s:`The general term is r\xB7(\xB9\u2070C\u1D63)\xB2. Write r\xB7\xB9\u2070C\u1D63 = 10\xB7\u2079C\u1D63\u208B\u2081, so the term becomes 10\xB7\u2079C\u1D63\u208B\u2081\xB7\xB9\u2070C\u1D63.
Also \xB9\u2070C\u1D63 = \xB9\u2070C\u2081\u2080\u208B\u1D63, so X = 10\xB7\u03A3 \u2079C\u1D63\u208B\u2081\xB7\xB9\u2070C\u2081\u2080\u208B\u1D63.
The lower numbers (r \u2212 1) and (10 \u2212 r) add up to 9, so by Vandermonde the sum is \xB9\u2079C\u2089.
So X = 10\xB7\xB9\u2079C\u2089 = 10 \xD7 92378 = 923780.
Dividing by 1430: 923780/1430 = 646.`},{pn:92,y:"JEE Advanced 2016",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"Let m be the smallest positive integer such that the coefficient of x\xB2 in the expansion of (1 + x)\xB2 + (1 + x)\xB3 + ... + (1 + x)\u2074\u2079 + (1 + mx)\u2075\u2070 is (3n + 1)\xB7\u2075\xB9C\u2083 for some positive integer n. Then the value of n is ____.",n:5,s:`The coefficient of x\xB2 in (1 + x)\u1D4F is \u1D4FC\u2082, so the first part gives \xB2C\u2082 + \xB3C\u2082 + ... + \u2074\u2079C\u2082 = \u2075\u2070C\u2083 (hockey-stick identity).
In (1 + mx)\u2075\u2070 the coefficient of x\xB2 is m\xB2\xB7\u2075\u2070C\u2082. Total = \u2075\u2070C\u2083 + m\xB2\xB7\u2075\u2070C\u2082.
Numbers: \u2075\u2070C\u2083 = 19600, \u2075\u2070C\u2082 = 1225 and \u2075\xB9C\u2083 = 20825. So 19600 + 1225m\xB2 = (3n + 1)\xB720825.
Dividing by 1225: 16 + m\xB2 = (3n + 1)\xB717, so m\xB2 = 51n + 1.
m = 1 gives n = 0, which is not allowed. Trying m = 2, 3, ... (checked by computation) the first value that works is m = 16, since 16\xB2 \u2212 1 = 255 = 51 \xD7 5.
So m = 16 and n = 5.`},{pn:93,y:"JEE Advanced 2015",tp:"coef",st:"Product of expansions",q:"The coefficient of x\u2079 in the expansion of (1 + x)(1 + x\xB2)(1 + x\xB3)...(1 + x\xB9\u2070\u2070) is ____.",n:8,s:`Each factor (1 + x\u1D4F) either contributes 1 or x\u1D4F. A term x\u2079 comes from choosing different k values that add up to 9.
So the coefficient is the number of ways to write 9 as a sum of distinct positive integers (the limit 100 is no restriction).
List by number of parts: 1 part: 9. 2 parts: 8+1, 7+2, 6+3, 5+4. 3 parts: 6+2+1, 5+3+1, 4+3+2.
4 or more distinct parts need at least 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10 > 9, so there are none.
Total = 1 + 4 + 3 = 8.`},{pn:94,y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"sum",st:"Using differentiation and integration",q:"Prove that 3!/(2(n + 3)) = \u03A3\u1D63\u208C\u2080\u207F (\u22121)\u02B3 \xB7 \u207FC\u1D63/(\u02B3\u207A\xB3C\u1D63).",a:"Proved: the sum equals 3/(n + 3) = 3!/(2(n + 3)).",s:`Write 1/\u02B3\u207A\xB3C\u1D63 = r!\xB73!/(r + 3)!.
Use the Beta integral \u222B\u2080\xB9 x\u02B3(1 \u2212 x)\xB2 dx = r!\xB72!/(r + 3)!. So r!\xB73!/(r + 3)! = 3\u222B\u2080\xB9 x\u02B3(1 \u2212 x)\xB2 dx.
Then \u03A3 (\u22121)\u02B3 \u207FC\u1D63/\u02B3\u207A\xB3C\u1D63 = 3\u222B\u2080\xB9 (1 \u2212 x)\xB2 \xB7 \u03A3 \u207FC\u1D63(\u2212x)\u02B3 dx.
By the binomial theorem \u03A3 \u207FC\u1D63(\u2212x)\u02B3 = (1 \u2212 x)\u207F.
So the sum = 3\u222B\u2080\xB9 (1 \u2212 x)\u207F\u207A\xB2 dx = 3/(n + 3).
Since 3!/(2(n + 3)) = 6/(2(n + 3)) = 3/(n + 3), the two sides are equal. Hence proved.`},{pn:95,y:"IIT-JEE 1993",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"Prove that \u03A3\u1D63\u208C\u2081\u1D4F (\u22123)\u02B3\u207B\xB9 \xB3\u207FC\u2082\u1D63\u208B\u2081 = 0, where k = 3n/2 and n is an even positive integer.",a:"Proved: the sum is 0.",s:`Let w = 1 + i\u221A3 = 2(cos 60\xB0 + i sin 60\xB0). By De Moivre, w\xB3\u207F = 2\xB3\u207F(cos n\u03C0 + i sin n\u03C0), because 3n \xD7 60\xB0 = n\u03C0.
Since sin n\u03C0 = 0 for integer n, the imaginary part of w\xB3\u207F is 0.
By the binomial theorem w\xB3\u207F = \u03A3 \xB3\u207FC\u2C7C (i\u221A3)\u02B2. The imaginary part comes from the odd powers j = 2r \u2212 1.
(i\u221A3)\xB2\u02B3\u207B\xB9 = i\u221A3\xB7(i\xB2\xB73)\u02B3\u207B\xB9 = i\u221A3\xB7(\u22123)\u02B3\u207B\xB9. So the imaginary part is \u221A3\xB7\u03A3 (\u22123)\u02B3\u207B\xB9\xB7\xB3\u207FC\u2082\u1D63\u208B\u2081.
The odd index 2r \u2212 1 goes up to 3n \u2212 1 when r = 3n/2 = k, so this sum is exactly the one in the question.
So \u221A3\xB7\u03A3 = 0, hence \u03A3 = 0. Hence proved.`},{pn:96,y:"IIT-JEE 1992",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"If \u03A3\u1D63\u208C\u2080\xB2\u207F a\u1D63(x \u2212 2)\u02B3 = \u03A3\u1D63\u208C\u2080\xB2\u207F b\u1D63(x \u2212 3)\u02B3 and a\u2096 = 1 for all k \u2265 n, then show that b\u2099 = \xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2099\u208A\u2081.",a:"Proved: b\u2099 = \xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2099\u208A\u2081.",s:`Put x \u2212 3 = t. Then x \u2212 2 = t + 1, and the left side is \u03A3 a\u1D63(1 + t)\u02B3.
The coefficient of t\u207F in a\u1D63(1 + t)\u02B3 is a\u1D63\xB7\u02B3C\u2099, which is non-zero only for r \u2265 n.
So b\u2099 = \u03A3\u1D63\u208C\u2099\xB2\u207F a\u1D63\xB7\u02B3C\u2099, and a\u1D63 = 1 for r \u2265 n gives b\u2099 = \u207FC\u2099 + \u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2099 + ... + \xB2\u207FC\u2099.
By the hockey-stick identity, this sum is \xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2099\u208A\u2081.
Hence b\u2099 = \xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2099\u208A\u2081.`},{pn:97,y:"IIT-JEE 1989",tp:"sum",st:"Using differentiation and integration",q:"Prove that C\u2080 \u2212 2\xB2C\u2081 + 3\xB2C\u2082 \u2212 ... + (\u22121)\u207F(n + 1)\xB2C\u2099 = 0, n > 1 where C\u1D63 = \u207FC\u1D63.",a:"Proved for n > 2: the sum is 0. (For n = 2 the sum is 2, checked directly.)",s:`Note: the printed condition is n > 1, but for n = 2 the sum is 1 \u2212 8 + 9 = 2, not 0; the identity holds for n > 2, which is what is proved here.
The sum is \u03A3(\u22121)\u02B3(r + 1)\xB2C\u1D63. Write (r + 1)\xB2 = r(r \u2212 1) + 3r + 1.
So the sum = \u03A3(\u22121)\u02B3 r(r \u2212 1)C\u1D63 + 3\u03A3(\u22121)\u02B3 r\xB7C\u1D63 + \u03A3(\u22121)\u02B3 C\u1D63.
Start from (1 \u2212 x)\u207F = \u03A3(\u22121)\u02B3 C\u1D63 x\u02B3. At x = 1, \u03A3(\u22121)\u02B3 C\u1D63 = 0.
Differentiate: \u2212n(1 \u2212 x)\u207F\u207B\xB9 = \u03A3(\u22121)\u02B3 r\xB7C\u1D63 x\u02B3\u207B\xB9. At x = 1 the left side is 0 (n > 1), so \u03A3(\u22121)\u02B3 r\xB7C\u1D63 = 0.
Differentiate again: n(n \u2212 1)(1 \u2212 x)\u207F\u207B\xB2 = \u03A3(\u22121)\u02B3 r(r \u2212 1)C\u1D63 x\u02B3\u207B\xB2. At x = 1 the left side is 0 when n > 2.
So for n > 2 all three parts are 0 and the sum is 0.`},{pn:98,y:"IIT-JEE 1988",tp:"sum",st:"Digits and approximation",q:"Let R = (5\u221A5 + 11)\xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9 and f = R \u2212 [R], where [\xB7] denotes the greatest integer function. Prove that Rf = 4\xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9.",a:"Proved: Rf = 4\xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9.",s:`Let f\u2032 = (5\u221A5 \u2212 11)\xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9. Since 5\u221A5 \u2248 11.18, we have 0 < 5\u221A5 \u2212 11 < 1, so 0 < f\u2032 < 1.
Now R \u2212 f\u2032 = (5\u221A5 + 11)\xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9 \u2212 (5\u221A5 \u2212 11)\xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9 = 2[\xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2081(5\u221A5)\xB2\u207F\xB711 + \xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2083(5\u221A5)\xB2\u207F\u207B\xB2\xB711\xB3 + ...].
Note: only the odd powers of 11 survive the subtraction, and then (5\u221A5) appears with even powers, which are whole numbers, so the bracket is an integer.
So R \u2212 f\u2032 is an integer. R = (integer) + f\u2032 with 0 < f\u2032 < 1 means [R] = R \u2212 f\u2032 and f = f\u2032.
Then Rf = (5\u221A5 + 11)\xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9(5\u221A5 \u2212 11)\xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9 = ((5\u221A5)\xB2 \u2212 11\xB2)\xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9 = (125 \u2212 121)\xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9 = 4\xB2\u207F\u207A\xB9. Hence proved.`},{pn:99,y:"IIT-JEE 1984",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"Given S\u2099 = 1 + q + q\xB2 + ... + q\u207F, S\u2032\u2099 = 1 + (q + 1)/2 + ((q + 1)/2)\xB2 + ... + ((q + 1)/2)\u207F, q \u2260 1. Prove that \u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2081 + \u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2082S\u2081 + \u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2083S\u2082 + ... + \u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2099\u208A\u2081S\u2099 = 2\u207FS\u2032\u2099.",a:"Proved: both sides equal ((q + 1)\u207F\u207A\xB9 \u2212 2\u207F\u207A\xB9)/(q \u2212 1).",s:`Since q \u2260 1, S\u2096 = (q\u1D4F\u207A\xB9 \u2212 1)/(q \u2212 1).
The left side is \u03A3\u2096\u208C\u2080\u207F \u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2096\u208A\u2081 S\u2096 = (1/(q \u2212 1))\xB7\u03A3\u2096\u208C\u2080\u207F \u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2096\u208A\u2081(q\u1D4F\u207A\xB9 \u2212 1).
Put j = k + 1 (from 1 to n + 1): \u03A3 \u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2C7C q\u02B2 \u2212 \u03A3 \u207F\u207A\xB9C\u2C7C = [(1 + q)\u207F\u207A\xB9 \u2212 1] \u2212 [2\u207F\u207A\xB9 \u2212 1] = (1 + q)\u207F\u207A\xB9 \u2212 2\u207F\u207A\xB9.
So the left side = ((1 + q)\u207F\u207A\xB9 \u2212 2\u207F\u207A\xB9)/(q \u2212 1).
The right side: S\u2032\u2099 is a geometric series with ratio (q + 1)/2, so S\u2032\u2099 = (((q + 1)/2)\u207F\u207A\xB9 \u2212 1)/((q + 1)/2 \u2212 1) = 2(((q + 1)\u207F\u207A\xB9 \u2212 2\u207F\u207A\xB9)/2\u207F\u207A\xB9)/(q \u2212 1).
Multiply by 2\u207F: 2\u207FS\u2032\u2099 = ((q + 1)\u207F\u207A\xB9 \u2212 2\u207F\u207A\xB9)/(q \u2212 1), the same as the left side. Hence proved.`},{pn:100,y:"IIT-JEE 1983",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"If (1 + x)\u207F = C\u2080 + C\u2081x + C\u2082x\xB2 + ... + C\u2099x\u207F, then show that the sum of the products of the C\u1D62's taken two at a time, represented by \u03A3 \u03A3\u2080\u2264\u1D62<\u2C7C\u2264\u2099 C\u1D62C\u2C7C, is equal to 2\xB2\u207F\u207B\xB9 \u2212 (2n)!/(2(n!)\xB2).",a:"Proved: \u03A3\u03A3 C\u1D62C\u2C7C = 2\xB2\u207F\u207B\xB9 \u2212 (2n)!/(2(n!)\xB2).",s:`Use (C\u2080 + C\u2081 + ... + C\u2099)\xB2 = \u03A3C\u1D62\xB2 + 2\u03A3\u03A3\u208D\u1D62<\u2C7C\u208E C\u1D62C\u2C7C.
The left side is (2\u207F)\xB2 = 2\xB2\u207F.
\u03A3C\u1D62\xB2 = \xB2\u207FC\u2099 = (2n)!/(n!)\xB2 (compare the coefficient of x\u207F in (1 + x)\u207F(x + 1)\u207F).
So 2\xB7\u03A3\u03A3 C\u1D62C\u2C7C = 2\xB2\u207F \u2212 (2n)!/(n!)\xB2.
Dividing by 2: \u03A3\u03A3 C\u1D62C\u2C7C = 2\xB2\u207F\u207B\xB9 \u2212 (2n)!/(2(n!)\xB2). Hence proved.`},{pn:101,y:"IIT-JEE 1979",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"Prove that C\u2081\xB2 \u2212 2\xB7C\u2082\xB2 + 3\xB7C\u2083\xB2 \u2212 ... \u2212 2n\xB7C\u2082\u2099\xB2 = (\u22121)\u207F\u207B\xB9\xB7n\xB7C\u2099, where C\u1D63 = \xB2\u207FC\u1D63.",a:"Proved: the sum is (\u22121)\u207F\u207B\xB9 n\xB7C\u2099.",s:`The sum is S = \u03A3\u1D63\u208C\u2081\xB2\u207F (\u22121)\u02B3\u207B\xB9 r\xB7C\u1D63\xB2 with C\u1D63 = \xB2\u207FC\u1D63.
Use r\xB7\xB2\u207FC\u1D63 = 2n\xB7\xB2\u207F\u207B\xB9C\u1D63\u208B\u2081. Then S = 2n\xB7\u03A3\u1D63 (\u22121)\u02B3\u207B\xB9 \xB2\u207F\u207B\xB9C\u1D63\u208B\u2081 \xB7 \xB2\u207FC\u1D63.
Replace \xB2\u207FC\u1D63 by \xB2\u207FC\u2082\u2099\u208B\u1D63. The sum \u03A3(\u22121)\u02B3\u207B\xB9 \xB2\u207F\u207B\xB9C\u1D63\u208B\u2081\xB7\xB2\u207FC\u2082\u2099\u208B\u1D63 is the coefficient of x\xB2\u207F\u207B\xB9 in (1 \u2212 x)\xB2\u207F\u207B\xB9(1 + x)\xB2\u207F.
Note: the coefficient of x\u02B3\u207B\xB9 in (1 \u2212 x)\xB2\u207F\u207B\xB9 is (\u22121)\u02B3\u207B\xB9 \xB2\u207F\u207B\xB9C\u1D63\u208B\u2081, and the coefficient of x\xB2\u207F\u207B\u02B3 in (1 + x)\xB2\u207F is \xB2\u207FC\u2082\u2099\u208B\u1D63; the powers add to 2n \u2212 1.
Now (1 \u2212 x)\xB2\u207F\u207B\xB9(1 + x)\xB2\u207F = (1 \u2212 x\xB2)\xB2\u207F\u207B\xB9(1 + x). The x\xB2\u207F\u207B\xB9 term needs x from (1 + x) and x\xB2\u207F\u207B\xB2 = (x\xB2)\u207F\u207B\xB9 from (1 \u2212 x\xB2)\xB2\u207F\u207B\xB9.
That coefficient is (\u22121)\u207F\u207B\xB9\xB7\xB2\u207F\u207B\xB9C\u2099\u208B\u2081.
So S = 2n\xB7(\u22121)\u207F\u207B\xB9\xB7\xB2\u207F\u207B\xB9C\u2099\u208B\u2081. Since \xB2\u207FC\u2099 = 2\xB7\xB2\u207F\u207B\xB9C\u2099\u208B\u2081, 2n\xB7\xB2\u207F\u207B\xB9C\u2099\u208B\u2081 = n\xB7\xB2\u207FC\u2099, so S = (\u22121)\u207F\u207B\xB9\xB7n\xB7C\u2099. Hence proved.`},{pn:102,y:"IIT-JEE 1978",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"Prove that (\xB2\u207FC\u2080)\xB2 \u2212 (\xB2\u207FC\u2081)\xB2 + (\xB2\u207FC\u2082)\xB2 \u2212 ... + (\xB2\u207FC\u2082\u2099)\xB2 = (\u22121)\u207F\xB7\xB2\u207FC\u2099.",a:"Proved: the sum is (\u22121)\u207F\xB7\xB2\u207FC\u2099.",s:`The sum is \u03A3\u1D63\u208C\u2080\xB2\u207F (\u22121)\u02B3 (\xB2\u207FC\u1D63)\xB2. Use \xB2\u207FC\u1D63 = \xB2\u207FC\u2082\u2099\u208B\u1D63 to write each square as \xB2\u207FC\u1D63\xB7\xB2\u207FC\u2082\u2099\u208B\u1D63.
Consider (1 \u2212 x)\xB2\u207F(1 + x)\xB2\u207F. The coefficient of x\xB2\u207F in it is \u03A3 (\u22121)\u02B3 \xB2\u207FC\u1D63\xB7\xB2\u207FC\u2082\u2099\u208B\u1D63, which is our sum.
But (1 \u2212 x)\xB2\u207F(1 + x)\xB2\u207F = (1 \u2212 x\xB2)\xB2\u207F.
The x\xB2\u207F term of (1 \u2212 x\xB2)\xB2\u207F comes from (\u2212x\xB2)\u207F, giving (\u22121)\u207F\xB7\xB2\u207FC\u2099.
So the sum = (\u22121)\u207F\xB7\xB2\u207FC\u2099. Hence proved.`},{pn:103,y:"IIT-JEE 1983",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"If (1 + ax)\u207F = 1 + 8x + 24x\xB2 + ... then a = ____, n = ____.",a:"a = 2, n = 4",s:`The expansion is (1 + ax)\u207F = 1 + n(ax) + n(n \u2212 1)/2\xB7(ax)\xB2 + ...
Comparing the coefficient of x: na = 8.
Comparing the coefficient of x\xB2: n(n \u2212 1)a\xB2/2 = 24.
Square the first: n\xB2a\xB2 = 64. Divide the second by this: (n \u2212 1)/(2n) = 24/64 = 3/8.
So 8(n \u2212 1) = 6n, which gives 2n = 8 and n = 4.
Then a = 8/n = 2. So a = 2 and n = 4.`},{pn:104,y:"IIT-JEE 1982",tp:"num",st:"Digits and approximation",q:"The larger of 99\u2075\u2070 + 100\u2075\u2070 and 101\u2075\u2070 is ____.",a:"101\u2075\u2070",s:`Write 101\u2075\u2070 = (100 + 1)\u2075\u2070 and 99\u2075\u2070 = (100 \u2212 1)\u2075\u2070.
Subtract: 101\u2075\u2070 \u2212 99\u2075\u2070 = 2[\u2075\u2070C\u2081\xB7100\u2074\u2079 + \u2075\u2070C\u2083\xB7100\u2074\u2077 + ...], because the even-power terms cancel.
The first term alone is 2 \xD7 50 \xD7 100\u2074\u2079 = 100 \xD7 100\u2074\u2079 = 100\u2075\u2070.
All the other terms are positive, so 101\u2075\u2070 \u2212 99\u2075\u2070 > 100\u2075\u2070.
So 101\u2075\u2070 > 99\u2075\u2070 + 100\u2075\u2070, and the larger number is 101\u2075\u2070.`},{pn:105,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"The term independent of x in the expansion of ((x + 1)/(x^(2/3) \u2212 x^(1/3) + 1) \u2212 (x \u2212 1)/(x \u2212 x^(1/2)))\xB9\u2070, x > 1, is:",o:["120","240","210","150"],c:2,s:`Let a = x^(1/3). Then x + 1 = a\xB3 + 1 = (a + 1)(a\xB2 \u2212 a + 1), so the first fraction simplifies to x^(1/3) + 1.
For the second, x \u2212 1 = (\u221Ax \u2212 1)(\u221Ax + 1) and x \u2212 \u221Ax = \u221Ax(\u221Ax \u2212 1), so it becomes (\u221Ax + 1)/\u221Ax = 1 + x^(\u22121/2).
The difference is x^(1/3) + 1 \u2212 1 \u2212 x^(\u22121/2) = x^(1/3) \u2212 x^(\u22121/2).
The general term of (x^(1/3) \u2212 x^(\u22121/2))\xB9\u2070 is \xB9\u2070C\u1D63(\u22121)\u02B3 x^((10 \u2212 r)/3 \u2212 r/2).
The power is zero when (10 \u2212 r)/3 = r/2, that is 20 \u2212 2r = 3r, so r = 4.
The term is \xB9\u2070C\u2084 = 210, which is option (c).`},{pn:106,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"General term",q:"In the expansion of (\u221B2 + 1/\u221B3)\u207F, n \u2208 \u2115, if the ratio of 15th term from the beginning to the 15th term from the end is 1/6, then the value of \u207FC\u2083 is",o:["4960","4060","1040","2300"],c:3,s:`The 15th term from the beginning is T\u2081\u2085 = \u207FC\u2081\u2084\xB7(2^(1/3))\u207F\u207B\xB9\u2074\xB7(3^(\u22121/3))\xB9\u2074.
The 15th term from the end is the 15th term of the reversed expansion: \u207FC\u2081\u2084\xB7(3^(\u22121/3))\u207F\u207B\xB9\u2074\xB7(2^(1/3))\xB9\u2074.
Dividing, the binomial coefficients cancel. The power of 2 is (n \u2212 14)/3 \u2212 14/3 = (n \u2212 28)/3 and the power of 3 is \u221214/3 + (n \u2212 14)/3 = (n \u2212 28)/3, so the ratio is 6^((n \u2212 28)/3).
Set 6^((n \u2212 28)/3) = 1/6 = 6\u207B\xB9, so (n \u2212 28)/3 = \u22121 and n = 25.
Then \xB2\u2075C\u2083 = 25\xB724\xB723/6 = 2300, which is option (d).`},{pn:107,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"num",st:"Counting terms",q:"The number of integral terms in the expansion of (5^(1/2) + 7^(1/8))\xB9\u2070\xB9\u2076 is",o:["129","127","130","128"],c:3,s:`The general term is \xB9\u2070\xB9\u2076C\u1D63\xB75^((1016 \u2212 r)/2)\xB77^(r/8), with r = 0, 1, ..., 1016.
The term is an integer when both exponents are whole numbers, because 5 and 7 are primes.
7^(r/8) is whole when r is a multiple of 8.
Then 1016 \u2212 r is a multiple of 8 too (1016 = 8 \xD7 127), so (1016 \u2212 r)/2 is whole as well.
r = 0, 8, 16, ..., 1016 gives 1016/8 + 1 = 127 + 1 = 128 values.
So there are 128 integral terms, option (d).`},{pn:108,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"For some n \u2260 10, let the coefficients of the 5th, 6th and 7th terms in the binomial expansion of (1 + x)\u207F\u207A\u2074 be in A.P. Then the largest coefficient in the expansion of (1 + x)\u207F\u207A\u2074 is :",o:["35","20","10","70"],c:0,s:`Let m = n + 4. The 5th, 6th and 7th coefficients are \u1D50C\u2084, \u1D50C\u2085, \u1D50C\u2086.
In an A.P. the middle one is the average: 2\xB7\u1D50C\u2085 = \u1D50C\u2084 + \u1D50C\u2086.
Divide by \u1D50C\u2085 using \u1D50C\u2084/\u1D50C\u2085 = 5/(m \u2212 4) and \u1D50C\u2086/\u1D50C\u2085 = (m \u2212 5)/6: 2 = 5/(m \u2212 4) + (m \u2212 5)/6.
Multiply by 6(m \u2212 4): 12(m \u2212 4) = 30 + (m \u2212 5)(m \u2212 4), so m\xB2 \u2212 21m + 98 = 0, giving m = 7 or m = 14.
m = 14 means n = 10, which is excluded. So m = 7 and n = 3.
The largest coefficient in (1 + x)\u2077 is the middle one, \u2077C\u2083 = 35, option (a).`},{pn:109,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"num",st:"Counting terms",q:"The least value of n for which the number of integral terms in the Binomial expansion of (\u221B7 + \xB9\xB2\u221A11)\u207F is 183, is :",o:["2148","2184","2196","2172"],c:1,s:`The general term is T\u1D63\u208A\u2081 = \u207FC\u1D63 \xB7 7^((n\u2212r)/3) \xB7 11^(r/12).
This term is an integer only when (n \u2212 r)/3 and r/12 are both whole numbers, so r must be a multiple of 12 and n \u2212 r a multiple of 3.
Since r is a multiple of 12, it is a multiple of 3, so n must be a multiple of 3 as well.
For such n, r = 0, 12, 24, \u2026 up to n, which gives [n/12] + 1 integral terms.
We need [n/12] + 1 = 183, so [n/12] = 182 and n \u2265 182 \xD7 12 = 2184.
2184 = 3 \xD7 728 is a multiple of 3, so it works (a computer loop over n also gives 2184 as the first n that fits).
So the least value of n is 2184, which is option (b).`},{pn:110,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"General term",q:"The sum of all rational terms in the expansion of (2^(1/5) + 5^(1/3))\xB9\u2075 is equal to",o:["3133","633","931","6131"],c:0,s:`The general term is T\u1D63\u208A\u2081 = \xB9\u2075C\u1D63 \xB7 2^((15\u2212r)/5) \xB7 5^(r/3).
The term is rational only when (15 \u2212 r)/5 and r/3 are whole numbers, so r is a multiple of 3 and also of 5.
In the range 0 \u2264 r \u2264 15 only r = 0 and r = 15 qualify.
For r = 0 the term is 2\xB3 = 8, and for r = 15 the term is 5\u2075 = 3125.
The sum of the rational terms is 8 + 3125 = 3133, which is option (a).`},{pn:111,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"If the constant term in the expansion of (\u2075\u221A3/x + 2x/\xB3\u221A5)\xB9\xB2, x \u2260 0, is \u03B1 \xD7 2\u2078 \xD7 \u2075\u221A3, then 25\u03B1 is equal to :",o:["639","742","724","693"],c:3,s:`The general term is \xB9\xB2C\u1D63 (3^(1/5)/x)^(12\u2212r) (2x/5^(1/3))^r, and the power of x is r \u2212 (12 \u2212 r) = 2r \u2212 12.
For the constant term 2r \u2212 12 = 0, so r = 6.
The constant term is \xB9\xB2C\u2086 \xB7 3^(6/5) \xB7 2\u2076 / 5\xB2 = 924 \xD7 64 \xD7 3 \xB7 3^(1/5) / 25, because 3^(6/5) = 3 \xB7 3^(1/5).
Compare with \u03B1 \xD7 2\u2078 \xD7 3^(1/5): \u03B1 = 924 \xD7 64 \xD7 3 / (25 \xD7 256) = 924 \xD7 3 / 100.
So 25\u03B1 = 924 \xD7 3 / 4 = 693, which is option (d) (checked by computer: 924\xD764\xD73/25/256\xD725 = 693).`},{pn:112,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"If the term independent of x in the expansion of (\u221Aa \xB7 x\xB2 + 1/(2x\xB3))\xB9\u2070 is 105, then a\xB2 is equal to :",o:["2","6","4","9"],c:2,s:`The general term is \xB9\u2070C\u1D63 (\u221Aa x\xB2)^(10\u2212r) (1/(2x\xB3))\u02B3, and its power of x is 2(10 \u2212 r) \u2212 3r = 20 \u2212 5r.
For the term independent of x, 20 \u2212 5r = 0, so r = 4.
That term is \xB9\u2070C\u2084 \xB7 a\xB3 / 2\u2074 = 210a\xB3/16.
Set it equal to 105: a\xB3 = 105 \xD7 16 / 210 = 8, so a = 2.
So a\xB2 = 4, which is option (c).`},{pn:113,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"exp",st:"General term",q:"The sum of the coefficient of x^(2/3) and x^(\u22122/5) in the binomial expansion of (x^(2/3) + 1/2 \xB7 x^(\u22122/5))\u2079 is",o:["69/16","63/16","21/4","19/4"],c:2,s:`The general term is \u2079C\u1D63 (x^(2/3))^(9\u2212r) (x^(\u22122/5)/2)\u02B3 and its power of x is 6 \u2212 2r/3 \u2212 2r/5 = 6 \u2212 16r/15.
For x^(2/3): 6 \u2212 16r/15 = 2/3, so 16r/15 = 16/3 and r = 5. Its coefficient is \u2079C\u2085/2\u2075 = 126/32 = 63/16.
For x^(\u22122/5): 6 \u2212 16r/15 = \u22122/5, so 16r/15 = 32/5 and r = 6. Its coefficient is \u2079C\u2086/2\u2076 = 84/64 = 21/16.
Add the two coefficients: 63/16 + 21/16 = 84/16 = 21/4.
So the sum is 21/4, which is option (c).`},{pn:114,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"exp",st:"General term",q:"Let m and n be the coefficients of seventh and thirteenth terms respectively in the expansion of ((1/3)x^(1/3) + 1/(2x^(2/3)))\xB9\u2078. Then (n/m)^(1/3) is :",o:["1/9","1/4","4/9","9/4"],c:3,s:`The general term is \xB9\u2078C\u1D63 (1/3)^(18\u2212r) (1/2)\u02B3 times a power of x, so the coefficient of T\u1D63\u208A\u2081 is \xB9\u2078C\u1D63 / (3^(18\u2212r) \xB7 2\u02B3).
The seventh term has r = 6: m = \xB9\u2078C\u2086 / (3\xB9\xB2 \xB7 2\u2076).
The thirteenth term has r = 12: n = \xB9\u2078C\u2081\u2082 / (3\u2076 \xB7 2\xB9\xB2).
Since \xB9\u2078C\u2086 = \xB9\u2078C\u2081\u2082, the binomial coefficients cancel in the ratio.
n/m = (3\xB9\xB2 \xB7 2\u2076)/(3\u2076 \xB7 2\xB9\xB2) = 3\u2076/2\u2076, so (n/m)^(1/3) = 3\xB2/2\xB2 = 9/4.
This is option (d).`},{pn:115,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"Suppose 2 \u2212 p, p, 2 \u2212 \u03B1, \u03B1 are the coefficients of four consecutive terms in the expansion of (1 + x)\u207F. Then the value of p\xB2 \u2212 \u03B1\xB2 + 6\u03B1 + 2p equals",o:["8","4","6","10"],c:0,s:`Call the four consecutive coefficients \u207FC\u1D63, \u207FC\u1D63\u208A\u2081, \u207FC\u1D63\u208A\u2082, \u207FC\u1D63\u208A\u2083. Two neighbours satisfy \u207FC\u1D63\u208A\u2081/\u207FC\u1D63 = (n \u2212 r)/(r + 1).
Let x = n \u2212 r. From the first pair, p/(2 \u2212 p) = x/(r + 1), which gives p = 2x/(n + 1).
From the last pair, \u03B1/(2 \u2212 \u03B1) = (x \u2212 2)/(r + 3), which gives \u03B1 = 2(x \u2212 2)/(n + 1) and 2 \u2212 \u03B1 = 2(r + 3)/(n + 1).
The middle ratio (2 \u2212 \u03B1)/p = (x \u2212 1)/(r + 2) then gives (r + 3)(r + 2) = x(x \u2212 1), that is (x \u2212 r \u2212 3)(x + r + 2) = 0, so x = r + 3 and n = 2r + 3.
Now p = (r + 3)/(r + 2) and \u03B1 = (r + 1)/(r + 2), so p + \u03B1 = 2 and p \u2212 \u03B1 = 2/(r + 2).
p\xB2 \u2212 \u03B1\xB2 + 6\u03B1 + 2p = (p \u2212 \u03B1)(p + \u03B1) + 6\u03B1 + 2p = [4 + 6(r + 1) + 2(r + 3)]/(r + 2) = (8r + 16)/(r + 2) = 8.
So the value is 8, which is option (a).`},{pn:116,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"General term",q:"If the ratio of the fifth term from the beginning to the fifth term from the end in the expansion of (\u2074\u221A2 + 1/\u2074\u221A3)\u207F is \u221A6 : 1, then the third term from the beginning is",o:["30\u221A2","60\u221A3","60\u221A2","30\u221A3"],c:1,s:`The fifth term from the beginning is T\u2085 = \u207FC\u2084 (2^(1/4))^(n\u22124) (3^(\u22121/4))\u2074.
The fifth term from the end is the fifth term of the expansion written in reverse order: \u207FC\u2084 (3^(\u22121/4))^(n\u22124) (2^(1/4))\u2074.
Dividing, the binomial coefficients cancel and the powers of 2 combine to 2^((n\u22128)/4) and the powers of 3 to 3^((n\u22128)/4), so the ratio is 6^((n\u22128)/4).
Set 6^((n\u22128)/4) = 6^(1/2), so (n \u2212 8)/4 = 1/2 and n = 10.
The third term is T\u2083 = \xB9\u2070C\u2082 (2^(1/4))\u2078 (3^(\u22121/4))\xB2 = 45 \xD7 4 \xD7 1/\u221A3 = 180/\u221A3 = 60\u221A3.
This is option (b).`},{pn:117,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"If the coefficients of x\u2077 in (ax\xB2 + 1/(2bx))\xB9\xB9 and x\u207B\u2077 in (ax \u2212 1/(3bx\xB2))\xB9\xB9 are equal, then",o:["64ab = 243","729ab = 32","32ab = 729","243ab = 64"],c:1,s:`In the first expansion the power of x in T\u1D63\u208A\u2081 is 2(11 \u2212 r) \u2212 r = 22 \u2212 3r. For x\u2077, 22 \u2212 3r = 7, so r = 5.
The coefficient is \xB9\xB9C\u2085 a\u2076 / (2b)\u2075 = \xB9\xB9C\u2085 a\u2076/(32b\u2075).
In the second expansion the power is (11 \u2212 r) \u2212 2r = 11 \u2212 3r. For x\u207B\u2077, 11 \u2212 3r = \u22127, so r = 6.
The coefficient is \xB9\xB9C\u2086 a\u2075 (\u22121/(3b))\u2076 = \xB9\xB9C\u2086 a\u2075/(729b\u2076), the sign is + because 6 is even.
Since \xB9\xB9C\u2085 = \xB9\xB9C\u2086, equal coefficients give a\u2076/(32b\u2075) = a\u2075/(729b\u2076).
Multiply both sides by 729\xB732\xB7b\u2076/a\u2075: 729ab = 32, which is option (b).`},{pn:118,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"If the coefficients of three consecutive terms in the expansion of (1 + x)\u207F are in the ratio 1 : 5 : 20, then the coefficient of the fourth term is",o:["1827","5481","2436","3654"],c:3,s:`Let the three coefficients be \u207FC\u1D63, \u207FC\u1D63\u208A\u2081, \u207FC\u1D63\u208A\u2082. Use \u207FC\u1D63\u208A\u2081/\u207FC\u1D63 = (n \u2212 r)/(r + 1).
First ratio: (n \u2212 r)/(r + 1) = 5, so n \u2212 r = 5r + 5.
Second ratio: (n \u2212 r \u2212 1)/(r + 2) = 20/5 = 4, so n \u2212 r \u2212 1 = 4r + 8.
Subtract: from the first, n \u2212 r \u2212 1 = 5r + 4, so 5r + 4 = 4r + 8 and r = 4.
Then n = 5r + 5 + r = 29. The three terms are \xB2\u2079C\u2084, \xB2\u2079C\u2085, \xB2\u2079C\u2086 (check: ratio 1 : 5 : 20).
The fourth term of (1 + x)\xB2\u2079 has coefficient \xB2\u2079C\u2083 = 29 \xD7 28 \xD7 27 / 6 = 3654, which is option (d).`},{pn:119,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"The absolute difference of the coefficients of x\xB9\u2070 and x\u2077 in the expansion of (2x\xB2 + 1/(2x))\xB9\xB9 is equal to",o:["10\xB3 \u2212 10","12\xB3 \u2212 12","13\xB3 \u2212 13","11\xB3 \u2212 11"],c:1,s:`The power of x in T\u1D63\u208A\u2081 is 2(11 \u2212 r) \u2212 r = 22 \u2212 3r.
For x\xB9\u2070: r = 4, coefficient \xB9\xB9C\u2084 \xB7 2\u2077 \xB7 (1/2)\u2074 = 330 \xD7 8 = 2640.
For x\u2077: r = 5, coefficient \xB9\xB9C\u2085 \xB7 2\u2076 \xB7 (1/2)\u2075 = 462 \xD7 2 = 924.
The absolute difference is 2640 \u2212 924 = 1716.
Now 12\xB3 \u2212 12 = 1728 \u2212 12 = 1716, so the answer is option (b).`},{pn:120,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"If the coefficient of x\u2077 in (ax \u2212 1/(bx\xB2))\xB9\xB3 and the coefficient of x\u207B\u2075 in (ax + 1/(bx\xB2))\xB9\xB3 are equal, then a\u2074b\u2074 is equal to",o:["11","44","22","33"],c:2,s:`The power of x in T\u1D63\u208A\u2081 of either expansion is (13 \u2212 r) \u2212 2r = 13 \u2212 3r.
For x\u2077: 13 \u2212 3r = 7, so r = 2. The coefficient is \xB9\xB3C\u2082 a\xB9\xB9 (\u22121/b)\xB2 = 78 a\xB9\xB9/b\xB2.
For x\u207B\u2075: 13 \u2212 3r = \u22125, so r = 6. The coefficient is \xB9\xB3C\u2086 a\u2077 (1/b)\u2076 = 1716 a\u2077/b\u2076.
Equal coefficients: 78 a\xB9\xB9/b\xB2 = 1716 a\u2077/b\u2076.
Multiply by b\u2076/(78 a\u2077): a\u2074b\u2074 = 1716/78 = 22, which is option (c).`},{pn:121,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Product of expansions",q:"If the coefficients of x and x\xB2 in (1 + x)\u1D56 (1 \u2212 x)\u1D60 are 4 and \u22125 respectively, then 2p + 3q is equal to",o:["66","60","63","69"],c:2,s:`Expand to the first terms: (1 + x)\u1D56 = 1 + px + \u1D56C\u2082x\xB2 + \u2026 and (1 \u2212 x)\u1D60 = 1 \u2212 qx + \u1D60C\u2082x\xB2 \u2212 \u2026.
The coefficient of x is p \u2212 q = 4.
The coefficient of x\xB2 is \u1D56C\u2082 + \u1D60C\u2082 \u2212 pq = \u22125.
Write \u1D56C\u2082 + \u1D60C\u2082 \u2212 pq = [p\xB2 \u2212 p + q\xB2 \u2212 q \u2212 2pq]/2 = [(p \u2212 q)\xB2 \u2212 (p + q)]/2.
So (16 \u2212 (p + q))/2 = \u22125, which gives p + q = 26.
With p \u2212 q = 4 we get p = 15 and q = 11, so 2p + 3q = 30 + 33 = 63, which is option (c).`},{pn:122,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"The sum of the coefficients of three consecutive terms in the binomial expansion of (1 + x)\u207F\u207A\xB2, which are in the ratio 1 : 3 : 5, is equal to",o:["41","92","25","63"],c:3,s:`Let N = n + 2 and let the coefficients be \u1D3AC\u1D63, \u1D3AC\u1D63\u208A\u2081, \u1D3AC\u1D63\u208A\u2082 in the ratio 1 : 3 : 5.
First ratio: (N \u2212 r)/(r + 1) = 3, so N \u2212 r = 3r + 3.
Second ratio: (N \u2212 r \u2212 1)/(r + 2) = 5/3, so 3(N \u2212 r \u2212 1) = 5(r + 2), that is 3N \u2212 3r \u2212 3 = 5r + 10.
From the first, N = 4r + 3; put in the second: 12r + 9 \u2212 3r \u2212 3 = 5r + 10, so 4r = 4 and r = 1, N = 7.
The coefficients are \u2077C\u2081, \u2077C\u2082, \u2077C\u2083 = 7, 21, 35 (ratio 1 : 3 : 5, as needed; a computer search also finds only N = 7, r = 1).
Their sum is 7 + 21 + 35 = 63, which is option (d).`},{pn:123,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"exp",st:"General term",q:"If the 1011\u1D57\u02B0 term from the end in the binomial expansion of (4x/5 \u2212 5/(2x))\xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2 is 1024 times 1011\u1D57\u02B0 term from the beginning, then |x| is equal to",o:["15","8","12","10"],c:3,s:`Note: no key is printed. Reading the question exactly as printed, the working below gives |x| = 5/16, which is not among the options; the option that fits is (d) when the ratio is taken the other way round (the 1011\u1D57\u02B0 term from the beginning is 1024 times the 1011\u1D57\u02B0 from the end).
With A = 4x/5 and B = \u22125/(2x), the 1011\u1D57\u02B0 term from the beginning is T\u2081\u2080\u2081\u2081 = \xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2C\u2081\u2080\u2081\u2080 A\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB2 B\xB9\u2070\xB9\u2070.
The expansion has 2023 terms, so the 1011\u1D57\u02B0 from the end is T\u2081\u2080\u2081\u2083 = \xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2C\u2081\u2080\u2081\u2082 A\xB9\u2070\xB9\u2070 B\xB9\u2070\xB9\xB2, and \xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2C\u2081\u2080\u2081\u2082 = \xB2\u2070\xB2\xB2C\u2081\u2080\u2081\u2080 because 1012 + 1010 = 2022.
So T\u2081\u2080\u2081\u2083/T\u2081\u2080\u2081\u2081 = (B/A)\xB2 = (25/(8x\xB2))\xB2 = 625/(64x\u2074), which as printed would give x\u2074 = 625/65536.
Taken the other way, T\u2081\u2080\u2081\u2081/T\u2081\u2080\u2081\u2083 = (A/B)\xB2 = (8x\xB2/25)\xB2 = 1024, so 8x\xB2/25 = 32 (sign of x\xB2 is positive) and x\xB2 = 100.
So |x| = 10, which is option (d).`},{pn:124,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"The coefficient of x\u2075 in the expansion of (2x\xB3 \u2212 1/(3x\xB2))\u2075 is",o:["26/3","9","8","80/9"],c:3,s:`The power of x in T\u1D63\u208A\u2081 is 3(5 \u2212 r) \u2212 2r = 15 \u2212 5r.
For x\u2075: 15 \u2212 5r = 5, so r = 2.
The coefficient is \u2075C\u2082 \xB7 2\xB3 \xB7 (\u22121/3)\xB2 = 10 \xD7 8 \xD7 1/9.
That equals 80/9, which is option (d).`},{pn:125,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"If a\u1D63 is the coefficient of x\xB9\u2070\u207B\u02B3 in the Binomial expansion of (1 + x)\xB9\u2070, then \u03A3 (r=1 to 10) r\xB3 (a\u1D63/a\u1D63\u208B\u2081)\xB2 is equal to",o:["3025","1210","5445","4895"],c:1,s:`The coefficient of x\xB9\u2070\u207B\u02B3 is \xB9\u2070C\u2081\u2080\u208B\u1D63 = \xB9\u2070C\u1D63, so a\u1D63 = \xB9\u2070C\u1D63.
Then a\u1D63/a\u1D63\u208B\u2081 = \xB9\u2070C\u1D63/\xB9\u2070C\u1D63\u208B\u2081 = (11 \u2212 r)/r.
So r\xB3 (a\u1D63/a\u1D63\u208B\u2081)\xB2 = r\xB3 (11 \u2212 r)\xB2/r\xB2 = r(11 \u2212 r)\xB2.
Add for r = 1 to 10: 1\xB7100 + 2\xB781 + 3\xB764 + 4\xB749 + 5\xB736 + 6\xB725 + 7\xB716 + 8\xB79 + 9\xB74 + 10\xB71 = 100 + 162 + 192 + 196 + 180 + 150 + 112 + 72 + 36 + 10.
The total is 1210 (also confirmed by a quick computer sum), which is option (b).`},{pn:126,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sum",st:"Sums of binomial coefficients",q:"Let K be the sum of the coefficients of the odd powers of x in the expansion of (1 + x)\u2079\u2079. Let a be the middle term in the expansion of (2 + 1/\u221A2)\xB2\u2070\u2070. If \xB2\u2070\u2070C\u2089\u2089K/a = 2\u02E1 m/n, where m and n are odd numbers, then the ordered pair (l, n) is equal to",o:["(50, 101)","(51, 101)","(51, 99)","(50, 51)"],c:0,s:`The sum of coefficients of odd powers in (1 + x)\u2079\u2079 is half of 2\u2079\u2079 (even and odd parts are equal), so K = 2\u2079\u2078.
The expansion of (2 + 1/\u221A2)\xB2\u2070\u2070 has 201 terms, so the middle term is T\u2081\u2080\u2081 = \xB2\u2070\u2070C\u2081\u2080\u2080 \xB7 2\xB9\u2070\u2070 \xB7 (1/\u221A2)\xB9\u2070\u2070 = \xB2\u2070\u2070C\u2081\u2080\u2080 \xB7 2\xB9\u2070\u2070/2\u2075\u2070 = \xB2\u2070\u2070C\u2081\u2080\u2080 \xB7 2\u2075\u2070.
Then \xB2\u2070\u2070C\u2089\u2089K/a = (\xB2\u2070\u2070C\u2089\u2089/\xB2\u2070\u2070C\u2081\u2080\u2080) \xB7 2\u2079\u2078/2\u2075\u2070.
Note: \u207FC\u1D63\u208A\u2081/\u207FC\u1D63 = (n \u2212 r)/(r + 1), so \xB2\u2070\u2070C\u2089\u2089/\xB2\u2070\u2070C\u2081\u2080\u2080 = 100/101.
So the expression is (100/101) \xB7 2\u2074\u2078 = (4 \xD7 25)/101 \xB7 2\u2074\u2078 = 2\u2075\u2070 \xB7 25/101.
Here 25 and 101 are odd, so l = 50, m = 25, n = 101 and (l, n) = (50, 101), which is option (a).`},{pn:127,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"The coefficient of x\xB3\u2070\xB9 in (1 + x)\u2075\u2070\u2070 + x(1 + x)\u2074\u2079\u2079 + x\xB2(1 + x)\u2074\u2079\u2078 + \u2026 + x\u2075\u2070\u2070 is",o:["\u2075\u2070\u2070C\u2083\u2080\u2081","\u2075\u2070\xB9C\u2082\u2080\u2080","\u2075\u2070\u2070C\u2083\u2080\u2080","\u2075\u2070\xB9C\u2083\u2080\u2082"],c:1,s:`The sum is a geometric series with first term (1 + x)\u2075\u2070\u2070, common ratio x/(1 + x) and 501 terms.
Its sum is (1 + x)\u2075\u2070\u2070 \xB7 [1 \u2212 (x/(1 + x))\u2075\u2070\xB9] / [1 \u2212 x/(1 + x)] = [(1 + x)\u2075\u2070\xB9 \u2212 x\u2075\u2070\xB9]/[(1 + x) \u2212 x] = (1 + x)\u2075\u2070\xB9 \u2212 x\u2075\u2070\xB9.
Note: the denominator 1 \u2212 x/(1 + x) equals 1/(1 + x), which is why the sum simplifies so neatly.
The coefficient of x\xB3\u2070\xB9 in (1 + x)\u2075\u2070\xB9 \u2212 x\u2075\u2070\xB9 is \u2075\u2070\xB9C\u2083\u2080\u2081 (the x\u2075\u2070\xB9 term does not affect x\xB3\u2070\xB9).
Since \u2075\u2070\xB9C\u2083\u2080\u2081 = \u2075\u2070\xB9C\u2082\u2080\u2080, the answer is option (b).`},{pn:128,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"If the coefficient of x\xB9\u2075 in the expansion of (ax\xB3 + 1/(bx^(1/3)))\xB9\u2075 is equal to the coefficient of x\u207B\xB9\u2075 in the expansion of (ax^(1/3) \u2212 1/(bx\xB3))\xB9\u2075, where a and b are positive real numbers, then for each such ordered pair (a, b)",o:["a = 3b","a = b","ab = 1","ab = 3"],c:2,s:`In the first expansion the power of x in T\u1D63\u208A\u2081 is 3(15 \u2212 r) \u2212 r/3. Set it to 15: 45 \u2212 3r \u2212 r/3 = 15, so 10r/3 = 30 and r = 9.
The coefficient is \xB9\u2075C\u2089 a\u2076 / b\u2079.
In the second expansion the power is (15 \u2212 r)/3 \u2212 3r. Set it to \u221215: 15 \u2212 r \u2212 9r = \u221245, so r = 6.
The coefficient is \xB9\u2075C\u2086 a\u2079 (\u22121/b)\u2076 = \xB9\u2075C\u2086 a\u2079/b\u2076.
Since \xB9\u2075C\u2089 = \xB9\u2075C\u2086, equal coefficients give a\u2076/b\u2079 = a\u2079/b\u2076, so 1 = a\xB3b\xB3.
Taking the cube root of positive numbers, ab = 1, which is option (c).`},{pn:129,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"The term independent of x in the expansion of (1 \u2212 x\xB2 + 3x\xB3)((5/2)x\xB3 \u2212 1/(5x\xB2))\xB9\xB9, x \u2260 0 is",o:["7/40","33/200","39/200","11/50"],c:1,s:`In ((5/2)x\xB3 \u2212 1/(5x\xB2))\xB9\xB9 the power of x in T\u1D63\u208A\u2081 is 3(11 \u2212 r) \u2212 2r = 33 \u2212 5r, which is always 3 more than a multiple of 5 (33, 28, \u2026, 3, \u22122, \u22127, \u2026).
To get a constant term we need the power from the first bracket (0, 2 or 3) plus 33 \u2212 5r to be 0.
For 1 (power 0): 33 \u2212 5r = 0 has no whole number r. For 3x\xB3: 33 \u2212 5r = \u22123 gives r = 7.2, not possible.
For \u2212x\xB2: 33 \u2212 5r = \u22122 gives r = 7, which works.
That product is (\u22121) \xD7 \xB9\xB9C\u2087 (5/2)\u2074 (\u22121/5)\u2077 = \xB9\xB9C\u2087 \xD7 625/16 \xD7 1/78125 = 330/(16 \xD7 125) = 33/200.
So the constant term is 33/200, which is option (b).`},{pn:130,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"gen",st:"Multinomial and other",q:"If the constant term in the expansion of (3x\xB3 \u2212 2x\xB2 + 5/x\u2075)\xB9\u2070 is 2\u1D4F \xB7 l, where l is an odd integer, then the value of k is equal to",o:["6","7","8","9"],c:3,s:`Choose a terms 3x\xB3, b terms \u22122x\xB2, c terms 5/x\u2075 with a + b + c = 10. The term is 10!/(a! b! c!) (3x\xB3)\u1D43 (\u22122x\xB2)\u1D47 (5x\u207B\u2075)\u1D9C.
For a constant term the power of x must be 0: 3a + 2b \u2212 5c = 0.
Substituting a = 10 \u2212 b \u2212 c gives 30 \u2212 b \u2212 8c = 0, so b = 30 \u2212 8c; with b \u2265 0 and b + c \u2264 10 the only whole-number choice is c = 3, b = 6, a = 1 (a computer check of all (a, b, c) confirms this is the only one).
The constant term is 10!/(1! 6! 3!) \xD7 3 \xD7 (\u22122)\u2076 \xD7 5\xB3 = 840 \xD7 3 \xD7 64 \xD7 125.
Write 840 = 2\xB3 \xD7 105, 64 = 2\u2076, so the power of 2 is 2\u2079 and the rest 105 \xD7 3 \xD7 125 is odd (it equals 39375).
So the constant term is 20160000 = 2\u2079 \xD7 39375, hence k = 9, which is option (d).`},{pn:131,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"exp",st:"General term",q:"The sum of all those terms which are rational numbers in the expansion of (2^(1/3) + 3^(1/4))\xB9\xB2 is",o:["27","89","35","43"],c:3,s:`The general term is T\u1D63\u208A\u2081 = \xB9\xB2C\u1D63 2^((12\u2212r)/3) 3^(r/4).
It is rational only if (12 \u2212 r)/3 and r/4 are whole numbers: r must be a multiple of 4, and 12 \u2212 r a multiple of 3, so r a multiple of 3.
So r is a multiple of 12, and in 0 \u2264 r \u2264 12 only r = 0 and r = 12 work.
r = 0 gives 2\u2074 = 16 and r = 12 gives 3\xB3 = 27.
The sum is 16 + 27 = 43, which is option (d).`},{pn:132,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"If the greatest value of the term independent of x in the expansion of (x sin\u03B1 + a cos\u03B1/x)\xB9\u2070 is 10!/(5!)\xB2, then the value of a is equal to",o:["\u22122","\u22121","2","1"],c:2,s:`The general term is \xB9\u2070C\u1D63 (x sin\u03B1)\xB9\u2070\u207B\u02B3 (a cos\u03B1/x)\u02B3, with power of x equal to 10 \u2212 2r. For a constant term r = 5.
The term is \xB9\u2070C\u2085 (sin\u03B1 cos\u03B1)\u2075 a\u2075 = 252 \xB7 a\u2075 (sin 2\u03B1 / 2)\u2075.
Its greatest value is when sin 2\u03B1 = 1, giving 252 a\u2075/32.
Note: 10!/(5!)\xB2 = \xB9\u2070C\u2085 = 252, so we need 252 a\u2075/32 = 252.
So a\u2075 = 32 and a = 2, which is option (c).`},{pn:133,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"If the coefficients of x\u2077 in (x\xB2 + 1/(bx))\xB9\xB9 and x\u207B\u2077 in (x \u2212 1/(bx\xB2))\xB9\xB9 are equal, then the value of b is equal to",o:["2","\u22121","1","\u22122"],c:2,s:`In the first expansion the power of x in T\u1D63\u208A\u2081 is 2(11 \u2212 r) \u2212 r = 22 \u2212 3r. For x\u2077, r = 5, coefficient \xB9\xB9C\u2085/b\u2075.
In the second the power is (11 \u2212 r) \u2212 2r = 11 \u2212 3r. For x\u207B\u2077, r = 6, coefficient \xB9\xB9C\u2086 (\u22121/b)\u2076 = \xB9\xB9C\u2086/b\u2076.
Since \xB9\xB9C\u2085 = \xB9\xB9C\u2086, equal coefficients give 1/b\u2075 = 1/b\u2076.
Multiply by b\u2076: b = 1, which is option (c).`},{pn:134,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"exp",st:"General term",q:"A possible value of x, for which the ninth term in the expansion of \uFF5B3^(log\u2083\u221A(25^(x\u22121)+7)) + 3^(\u2212(1/8)log\u2083(5^(x\u22121)+1))\uFF5D\xB9\u2070 in the increasing powers of 3^(\u2212(1/8)log\u2083(5^(x\u22121)+1)) is equal to 180, is",o:["\u22121","0","1","2"],c:2,s:`Let A = 3^(log\u2083\u221A(25^(x\u22121)+7)) = \u221A(25^(x\u22121) + 7) and B = 3^(\u2212(1/8)log\u2083(5^(x\u22121)+1)) = (5^(x\u22121) + 1)^(\u22121/8).
In increasing powers of B, the ninth term is T\u2089 = \xB9\u2070C\u2088 A\xB2 B\u2078.
A\xB2 = 25^(x\u22121) + 7 and B\u2078 = 1/(5^(x\u22121) + 1), so T\u2089 = 45 (25^(x\u22121) + 7)/(5^(x\u22121) + 1).
Let t = 5^(x\u22121). Then 45(t\xB2 + 7)/(t + 1) = 180, so t\xB2 + 7 = 4t + 4, that is t\xB2 \u2212 4t + 3 = 0.
t = 1 or t = 3. t = 1 gives 5^(x\u22121) = 1, so x = 1; t = 3 gives x = 1 + log\u20853, which is not an option.
So the possible value is x = 1, which is option (c).`},{pn:135,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"num",st:"Counting terms",q:"If n is the number of irrational terms in the expansion of (3^(1/4) + 5^(1/8))\u2076\u2070, then (n \u2212 1) is divisible by",o:["26","7","8","30"],c:0,s:`The expansion has 61 terms; the general term is \u2076\u2070C\u1D63 3^((60\u2212r)/4) 5^(r/8).
It is rational only if (60 \u2212 r)/4 and r/8 are whole numbers: r is a multiple of 8 and 60 \u2212 r a multiple of 4, which holds when r is a multiple of 4 (already true for multiples of 8).
So the rational terms have r = 0, 8, 16, 24, 32, 40, 48, 56, which is 8 terms.
The number of irrational terms is n = 61 \u2212 8 = 53.
Then n \u2212 1 = 52 = 2 \xD7 26, which is divisible by 26, option (a) (7, 8 and 30 do not divide 52).`},{pn:136,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"General term",q:"If the fourth term in the expansion of (x + x^(log\u2082x))\u2077 is 4480, then the value of x where x \u2208 N is equal to",o:["3","4","2","1"],c:2,s:`The fourth term is T\u2084 = \u2077C\u2083 x\u2074 (x^(log\u2082x))\xB3 = 35 x\u2074 \xB7 x^(3 log\u2082x).
Set 35 x^(4 + 3 log\u2082x) = 4480, so x^(4 + 3 log\u2082x) = 128.
Try the options for natural x: x = 2 gives 2^(4 + 3) = 2\u2077 = 128, which works.
Others fail: x = 1 gives 1, x = 3 gives 3^(4 + 3 log\u20823) \u2248 3^(8.75) which is far more than 128, and x = 4 gives 4^(4 + 6) = 4\xB9\u2070 = 1048576.
So x = 2, which is option (c).`},{pn:137,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"The maximum value of the term independent of t in the expansion of (t x^(1/5) + (1 \u2212 x)^(1/10)/t)\xB9\u2070, where x \u2208 (0, 1) is",o:["2\xB710!/(3(5!)\xB2)","10!/(\u221A3 (5!)\xB2)","2\xB710!/(3\u221A3 (5!)\xB2)","10!/(3(5!)\xB2)"],c:2,s:`The power of t in T\u1D63\u208A\u2081 is (10 \u2212 r) \u2212 r = 10 \u2212 2r, so the term independent of t has r = 5.
It is \xB9\u2070C\u2085 (x^(1/5))\u2075 ((1 \u2212 x)^(1/10))\u2075 = \xB9\u2070C\u2085 \xB7 x \xB7 \u221A(1 \u2212 x), where \xB9\u2070C\u2085 = 10!/(5!)\xB2.
Maximise f(x) = x\u221A(1 \u2212 x) on (0, 1). Its derivative is \u221A(1 \u2212 x) \u2212 x/(2\u221A(1 \u2212 x)) = (2 \u2212 3x)/(2\u221A(1 \u2212 x)).
The derivative is zero at x = 2/3, positive before it and negative after it, so this is the maximum.
f(2/3) = (2/3) \xD7 \u221A(1/3) = 2/(3\u221A3).
So the maximum value is 2 \xB7 10!/(3\u221A3 (5!)\xB2), which is option (c).`},{pn:138,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"Let \u03B1 > 0, \u03B2 > 0 be such that \u03B1\xB3 + \u03B2\xB2 = 4. If the maximum value of the term independent of x in the binomial expansion of (\u03B1x^(1/9) + \u03B2x^(\u22121/6))\xB9\u2070 is 10k, then k is equal to",o:["336","352","84","176"],c:0,s:`The power of x in T\u1D63\u208A\u2081 is (10 \u2212 r)/9 \u2212 r/6. Setting it to 0 gives 2(10 \u2212 r) = 3r, so r = 4.
The independent term is \xB9\u2070C\u2084 \u03B1\u2076 \u03B2\u2074 = 210 \u03B1\u2076 \u03B2\u2074 = 210 (\u03B1\xB3)\xB2 (\u03B2\xB2)\xB2.
Let u = \u03B1\xB3 and v = \u03B2\xB2, so u + v = 4 and we maximise (uv)\xB2. By AM\u2013GM, uv \u2264 ((u + v)/2)\xB2 = 4, with equality at u = v = 2.
Note: AM\u2013GM says the product of two positive numbers with a fixed sum is largest when they are equal.
The maximum is 210 \xD7 4\xB2 = 3360.
Then 10k = 3360 gives k = 336, which is option (a).`},{pn:139,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"num",st:"Counting terms",q:"If the number of integral terms in the expansion of (3^(1/2) + 5^(1/8))\u207F is exactly 33, then the least value of n is",o:["264","128","256","248"],c:2,s:`The general term is \u207FC\u1D63 3^((n\u2212r)/2) 5^(r/8).
It is an integer only if r is a multiple of 8 and n \u2212 r is even. Since r is then even, n must be even.
For even n the integral terms are r = 0, 8, 16, \u2026 up to n, so their number is [n/8] + 1.
We need [n/8] + 1 = 33, so [n/8] = 32 and n \u2265 256.
256 is even, so it works, and the least n is 256 (option (c)).`},{pn:140,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"If the term independent of x in the expansion of ((3/2)x\xB2 \u2212 1/(3x))\u2079 is k, then 18k is equal to",o:["5","9","7","11"],c:2,s:`The power of x in T\u1D63\u208A\u2081 is 2(9 \u2212 r) \u2212 r = 18 \u2212 3r. For the independent term r = 6.
The term is \u2079C\u2086 (3/2)\xB3 (\u22121/3)\u2076 = 84 \xD7 27/8 \xD7 1/729.
Simplify: 84 \xD7 27/(8 \xD7 729) = 84/(8 \xD7 27) = 84/216 = 7/18.
So k = 7/18 and 18k = 7, which is option (c).`},{pn:141,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"If for some positive integer n, the coefficients of three consecutive terms in the binomial expansion of (1 + x)\u207F\u207A\u2075 are in the ratio 5 : 10 : 14, then the largest coefficient in this expansion is",o:["330","252","792","462"],c:3,s:`Let N = n + 5 and the three coefficients be \u1D3AC\u1D63, \u1D3AC\u1D63\u208A\u2081, \u1D3AC\u1D63\u208A\u2082. Use \u1D3AC\u1D63\u208A\u2081/\u1D3AC\u1D63 = (N \u2212 r)/(r + 1).
First ratio: (N \u2212 r)/(r + 1) = 10/5 = 2, so N \u2212 r = 2r + 2.
Second ratio: (N \u2212 r \u2212 1)/(r + 2) = 14/10 = 7/5, so 5(N \u2212 r \u2212 1) = 7(r + 2).
Put N \u2212 r = 2r + 2: 5(2r + 1) = 7r + 14, so 3r = 9 and r = 3, then N = 11 and n = 6.
In (1 + x)\xB9\xB9 the largest coefficient is the middle one, \xB9\xB9C\u2085 = \xB9\xB9C\u2086 = 462 (check: the consecutive terms \xB9\xB9C\u2083, \xB9\xB9C\u2084, \xB9\xB9C\u2085 are 165, 330, 462, which are in the ratio 5 : 10 : 14).
So the largest coefficient is 462, which is option (d).`},{pn:142,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"If the constant term in the binomial expansion of (\u221Ax \u2212 k/x\xB2)\xB9\u2070 is 405, then |k| equals",o:["9","1","3","2"],c:2,s:`The power of x in T\u1D63\u208A\u2081 is (10 \u2212 r)/2 \u2212 2r. Setting it to 0 gives 10 \u2212 r = 4r, so r = 2.
The constant term is \xB9\u2070C\u2082 (\u2212k)\xB2 = 45k\xB2.
Set 45k\xB2 = 405, so k\xB2 = 9.
Hence |k| = 3, which is option (c).`},{pn:143,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"The coefficient of x\u2077 in the expression (1 + x)\xB9\u2070 + x(1 + x)\u2079 + x\xB2(1 + x)\u2078 + \u2026 + x\xB9\u2070 is",o:["420","330","120","210"],c:1,s:`The terms form a geometric series with first term (1 + x)\xB9\u2070, ratio x/(1 + x) and 11 terms.
The sum is [(1 + x)\xB9\xB9 \u2212 x\xB9\xB9]/[(1 + x) \u2212 x] = (1 + x)\xB9\xB9 \u2212 x\xB9\xB9.
Note: this works because (1 + x)\xB9\u2070 \xD7 [1 \u2212 (x/(1 + x))\xB9\xB9] / [1 \u2212 x/(1 + x)] simplifies when 1 \u2212 x/(1 + x) = 1/(1 + x).
The coefficient of x\u2077 is \xB9\xB9C\u2087 = \xB9\xB9C\u2084 = 330 (the x\xB9\xB9 term has no effect).
So the answer is 330, option (b).`},{pn:144,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"coef",st:"Middle and greatest term",q:"If a, b and c are the greatest values of \xB9\u2079C\u209A, \xB2\u2070C_q and \xB2\xB9C\u1D63 respectively, then",o:["a/11 = b/22 = c/42","a/10 = b/11 = c/42","a/11 = b/22 = c/21","a/10 = b/11 = c/21"],c:0,s:`The greatest value of \u207FC\u2096 is the middle one: for n = 19 it is \xB9\u2079C\u2089 = \xB9\u2079C\u2081\u2080, for n = 20 it is \xB2\u2070C\u2081\u2080 and for n = 21 it is \xB2\xB9C\u2081\u2080 = \xB2\xB9C\u2081\u2081.
Compute them: a = \xB9\u2079C\u2089 = 92378, b = \xB2\u2070C\u2081\u2080 = 184756, c = \xB2\xB9C\u2081\u2080 = 352716.
Divide all three by 8398: a = 11 \xD7 8398, b = 22 \xD7 8398, c = 42 \xD7 8398.
So a : b : c = 11 : 22 : 42, that is a/11 = b/22 = c/42, which is option (a).
Note: the key printed in the scan is unclear (it reads 2); working gives option (a), and the three numbers above can be checked directly.`},{pn:145,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"If \u03B1 and \u03B2 are the coefficients of x\u2074 and x\xB2 respectively in the expansion of (x + \u221A(x\xB2\u22121))\u2076 + (x \u2212 \u221A(x\xB2\u22121))\u2076, then",o:["\u03B1 + \u03B2 = \u221230","\u03B1 \u2212 \u03B2 = \u2212132","\u03B1 + \u03B2 = 60","\u03B1 \u2212 \u03B2 = 60"],c:1,s:`In (a + b)\u2076 + (a \u2212 b)\u2076 the odd powers of b cancel and the even terms double, so the sum is 2[x\u2076 + C(6,2)x\u2074(x\xB2\u22121) + C(6,4)x\xB2(x\xB2\u22121)\xB2 + (x\xB2\u22121)\xB3].
Expand each piece: x\u2076 + 15x\u2074(x\xB2\u22121) + 15x\xB2(x\xB2\u22121)\xB2 + (x\xB2\u22121)\xB3 = x\u2076 + 15x\u2076 \u2212 15x\u2074 + 15x\u2076 \u2212 30x\u2074 + 15x\xB2 + x\u2076 \u2212 3x\u2074 + 3x\xB2 \u2212 1.
Collect like terms: 32x\u2076 \u2212 48x\u2074 + 18x\xB2 \u2212 1. Multiplying by 2 gives 64x\u2076 \u2212 96x\u2074 + 36x\xB2 \u2212 2.
So \u03B1 (coefficient of x\u2074) = \u221296 and \u03B2 (coefficient of x\xB2) = 36.
Then \u03B1 \u2212 \u03B2 = \u221296 \u2212 36 = \u2212132, and \u03B1 + \u03B2 = \u221260 (not in the options).
So the correct statement is \u03B1 \u2212 \u03B2 = \u2212132, which is option (b).`},{pn:146,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"In the expansion of (x/cos\u03B8 + 1/(x sin\u03B8))\xB9\u2076, if l\u2081 is the least value of the term independent of x when \u03C0/8 \u2264 \u03B8 \u2264 \u03C0/4 and l\u2082 is the least value of the term independent of x when \u03C0/16 \u2264 \u03B8 \u2264 \u03C0/8, then the ratio l\u2082 : l\u2081 is equal to",o:["16 : 1","8 : 1","1 : 8","1 : 16"],c:0,s:`The general term is C(16,r) x^(16\u2212r)/cos^(16\u2212r)\u03B8 \xB7 1/(x^r sin^r\u03B8), and x disappears when 16 \u2212 r = r, so r = 8.
The term independent of x is C(16,8)/(sin\u03B8 cos\u03B8)\u2078 = C(16,8)\xB7(2/sin2\u03B8)\u2078.
This is least when sin2\u03B8 is largest, because sin2\u03B8 is in the denominator.
For \u03C0/8 \u2264 \u03B8 \u2264 \u03C0/4, 2\u03B8 runs from \u03C0/4 to \u03C0/2, so the largest sin2\u03B8 is 1 and l\u2081 = C(16,8)\xB72\u2078.
For \u03C0/16 \u2264 \u03B8 \u2264 \u03C0/8, 2\u03B8 runs from \u03C0/8 to \u03C0/4, so the largest sin2\u03B8 is sin(\u03C0/4) = 1/\u221A2 and l\u2082 = C(16,8)\xB7(2\u221A2)\u2078 = C(16,8)\xB72\xB9\xB2.
So l\u2082 : l\u2081 = 2\xB9\xB2 : 2\u2078 = 16 : 1, which is option (a).`},{pn:147,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"The sum of the co-efficients of all even degree terms in x in the expansion of (x + \u221A(x\xB3\u22121))\u2076 + (x \u2212 \u221A(x\xB3\u22121))\u2076, (x > 1) is equal to",o:["26","32","29","24"],c:3,s:`In (a + b)\u2076 + (a \u2212 b)\u2076 the odd powers of b cancel, so the expression is 2[x\u2076 + 15x\u2074(x\xB3\u22121) + 15x\xB2(x\xB3\u22121)\xB2 + (x\xB3\u22121)\xB3].
Expand: x\u2076 + (15x\u2077 \u2212 15x\u2074) + (15x\u2078 \u2212 30x\u2075 + 15x\xB2) + (x\u2079 \u2212 3x\u2076 + 3x\xB3 \u2212 1) and then double everything.
The full polynomial is 2x\u2079 + 30x\u2078 + 30x\u2077 \u2212 4x\u2076 \u2212 60x\u2075 \u2212 30x\u2074 + 6x\xB3 + 30x\xB2 \u2212 2 (x\u2076 terms: 1 \u2212 3 = \u22122, doubled \u22124).
Even-degree terms: 30x\u2078, \u22124x\u2076, \u221230x\u2074, 30x\xB2, and the constant \u22122 (degree 0).
Add their coefficients: 30 \u2212 4 \u2212 30 + 30 \u2212 2 = 24.
So the required sum is 24, which is option (d).`},{pn:148,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"exp",st:"General term",q:"If the fourth term in the binomial expansion of (\u221A(x^(1/(1+log\u2081\u2080x))) + x^(1/12))\u2076 is equal to 200, and x > 1, then the value of x is",o:["100","10","10\xB3","10\u2074"],c:1,s:`Write L = log\u2081\u2080x. The first term is x^(1/(2(1+L))) and the second term is x^(1/12).
The fourth term is T\u2084 = C(6,3)\xB7(first)\xB3\xB7(second)\xB3 = 20\xB7x^(3/(2(1+L)))\xB7x^(1/4).
Setting 20\xB7x^(3/(2(1+L)) + 1/4) = 200 gives x^(3/(2(1+L)) + 1/4) = 10.
Take log\u2081\u2080 of both sides: L\xB7(3/(2(1+L)) + 1/4) = 1.
Check x = 10, so L = 1: 1\xB7(3/4 + 1/4) = 1. It works.
Note: the left side increases with L, so there is only one solution with x > 1 (L > 0), so x = 10, option (b).`},{pn:149,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"General term",q:"If the fourth term in the Binomial expansion of (2/x + x^(log\u2088x))\u2076 (x > 0) is 20 \xD7 8\u2077, then a value of x is",o:["8","8\u207B\xB2","8\xB3","8\xB2"],c:3,s:`The fourth term is T\u2084 = C(6,3)(2/x)\xB3(x^(log\u2088x))\xB3 = 20\xB78\xB7x^(3log\u2088x \u2212 3).
Set this equal to 20\xB78\u2077, so x^(3log\u2088x \u2212 3) = 8\u2076.
Let L = log\u2088x, so x = 8^L and the left side is 8^(L(3L\u22123)).
Equate the powers of 8: 3L\xB2 \u2212 3L = 6, so L\xB2 \u2212 L \u2212 2 = 0.
Factor: (L \u2212 2)(L + 1) = 0, so L = 2 or L = \u22121, giving x = 8\xB2 or x = 8\u207B\xB9.
Only 8\xB2 is in the options, so the answer is option (d).`},{pn:150,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"If some three consecutive coefficients in the binomial expansion of (x + 1)\u207F in powers of x are in the ratio 2 : 15 : 70, then the average of these three coefficients is",o:["625","964","227","232"],c:3,s:`Let the three consecutive coefficients be C(n,r\u22121), C(n,r), C(n,r+1).
The ratio of two neighbours is C(n,r)/C(n,r\u22121) = (n\u2212r+1)/r. From 2 : 15 we get (n\u2212r+1)/r = 15/2.
The next ratio is C(n,r+1)/C(n,r) = (n\u2212r)/(r+1). From 15 : 70 we get (n\u2212r)/(r+1) = 14/3.
First equation: 2n \u2212 2r + 2 = 15r, so 2n = 17r \u2212 2. Second: 3n \u2212 3r = 14r + 14, so 3n = 17r + 14.
Make both equal to 6n: 6n = 51r \u2212 6 and 6n = 34r + 28, so 17r = 34, r = 2 and n = 16.
The coefficients are C(16,1) = 16, C(16,2) = 120, C(16,3) = 560, and 16 : 120 : 560 = 2 : 15 : 70.
Their sum is 696, so the average is 696/3 = 232, which is option (d).`},{pn:151,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"coef",st:"Product of expansions",q:"If the coefficients of x\xB2 and x\xB3 are both zero, in the expansion of the expression (1 + ax + bx\xB2)(1 \u2212 3x)\xB9\u2075 in powers of x, then the ordered pair (a, b) is equal to",o:["(\u221254, 315)","(28, 861)","(\u221221, 714)","(28, 315)"],c:3,s:`The needed coefficients of (1 \u2212 3x)\xB9\u2075 are: x\xB9: \u221245, x\xB2: C(15,2)\xB79 = 945, x\xB3: C(15,3)\xB7(\u221227) = \u221212285.
Coefficient of x\xB2 in the product: 945 + a(\u221245) + b = 0, so b = 45a \u2212 945.
Coefficient of x\xB3 in the product: \u221212285 + a(945) + b(\u221245) = 0.
Substitute b: \u221212285 + 945a \u2212 2025a + 42525 = 0, so \u22121080a + 30240 = 0 and a = 28.
Then b = 45\xB728 \u2212 945 = 1260 \u2212 945 = 315.
So (a, b) = (28, 315), which is option (d).`},{pn:152,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"The smallest natural number n, such that the coefficient of x in the expansion of (x\xB2 + 1/x\xB3)\u207F is \u207FC\u2082\u2083, is",o:["38","23","35","58"],c:0,s:`The general term is C(n,r)(x\xB2)^(n\u2212r)(1/x\xB3)^r = C(n,r)x^(2n\u22125r).
For the power of x to be 1 we need 2n \u2212 5r = 1, so 2n = 5r + 1.
The coefficient C(n,r) must equal C(n,23). That happens when r = 23 or r = n \u2212 23.
Case r = 23: 2n = 116, so n = 58.
Case r = n \u2212 23: 2n = 5n \u2212 115 + 1, so 3n = 114 and n = 38 (then r = 15, and C(38,15) = C(38,23)).
The smaller value is n = 38, which is option (a).`},{pn:153,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"coef",st:"Product of expansions",q:"The coefficient of x\xB9\u2078 in the product (1 + x)(1 \u2212 x)\xB9\u2070(1 + x + x\xB2)\u2079 is",o:["84","\u2212126","126","\u221284"],c:0,s:`Combine (1 + x)(1 \u2212 x) = 1 \u2212 x\xB2, so the product is (1 \u2212 x\xB2)(1 \u2212 x)\u2079(1 + x + x\xB2)\u2079.
Use (1 \u2212 x)(1 + x + x\xB2) = 1 \u2212 x\xB3, so (1 \u2212 x)\u2079(1 + x + x\xB2)\u2079 = (1 \u2212 x\xB3)\u2079.
Now the product is (1 \u2212 x\xB2)(1 \u2212 x\xB3)\u2079.
The terms of (1 \u2212 x\xB3)\u2079 have powers that are multiples of 3: C(9,k)(\u22121)\u1D4Fx^(3k).
From the "1" of (1 \u2212 x\xB2) we need x\xB9\u2078 = x^(3k) with k = 6: C(9,6) = 84.
From the "\u2212x\xB2" we would need x\xB9\u2076, but 16 is not a multiple of 3, so it adds nothing.
So the coefficient is 84, which is option (a).`},{pn:154,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"The term independent of x in the expansion of (1/60 \u2212 x\u2078/81)\xB7(2x\xB2 \u2212 3/x\xB2)\u2076 is equal to",o:["\u2212108","\u221272","\u221236","36"],c:2,s:`The general term of (2x\xB2 \u2212 3/x\xB2)\u2076 is C(6,k)(2x\xB2)^(6\u2212k)(\u22123/x\xB2)\u1D4F, with power of x equal to 12 \u2212 4k.
For the product to have x\u2070 we need either x\u2070 from the bracket (times 1/60) or x\u207B\u2078 from the bracket (times \u2212x\u2078/81).
x\u2070: 12 \u2212 4k = 0 gives k = 3, coefficient C(6,3)\xB72\xB3\xB7(\u22123)\xB3 = 20\xB78\xB7(\u221227) = \u22124320.
x\u207B\u2078: 12 \u2212 4k = \u22128 gives k = 5, coefficient C(6,5)\xB72\xB7(\u22123)\u2075 = 6\xB72\xB7(\u2212243) = \u22122916.
Term independent of x = (1/60)(\u22124320) + (\u22121/81)(\u22122916) = \u221272 + 36.
So the value is \u221236, which is option (c).`},{pn:155,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"The coefficient of t\u2074 in the expansion of ((1 \u2212 t\u2076)/(1 \u2212 t))\xB3 is",o:["10","12","15","14"],c:2,s:`Write the expression as (1 \u2212 t\u2076)\xB3\xB7(1 \u2212 t)\u207B\xB3.
Since we only need t\u2074 and the lowest power in (1 \u2212 t\u2076)\xB3 other than 1 is t\u2076, only the "1" of (1 \u2212 t\u2076)\xB3 matters.
So we need the coefficient of t\u2074 in (1 \u2212 t)\u207B\xB3.
The expansion (1 \u2212 t)\u207B\xB3 = \u03A3 C(r+2, 2) t\u02B3.
For r = 4 the coefficient is C(6,2) = 15.
So the coefficient is 15, which is option (c).`},{pn:156,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"General term",q:"If the third term in the binomial expansion of (1 + x^(log\u2082x))\u2075 equals 2560, then a possible value of x is",o:["1/8","1/4","4\u221A2","2\u221A2"],c:1,s:`The third term is T\u2083 = C(5,2)(x^(log\u2082x))\xB2 = 10\xB7x^(2log\u2082x).
Set 10\xB7x^(2log\u2082x) = 2560, so x^(2log\u2082x) = 256 = 2\u2078.
Let t = log\u2082x, so x = 2\u1D57 and x^(2log\u2082x) = 2^(2t\xB2).
Then 2t\xB2 = 8, so t\xB2 = 4 and t = 2 or t = \u22122.
So x = 2\xB2 = 4 or x = 2\u207B\xB2 = 1/4.
Only 1/4 is among the options, which is option (b).`},{pn:157,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"The positive value of \u03BB for which the co-efficient of x\xB2 in the expression x\xB2(\u221Ax + \u03BB/x\xB2)\xB9\u2070 is 720, is",o:["4","3","\u221A5","2\u221A2"],c:0,s:`The factor x\xB2 in front means we need the coefficient of x\u2070 in (\u221Ax + \u03BB/x\xB2)\xB9\u2070.
The general term is C(10,r)(x^(1/2))^(10\u2212r)(\u03BBx\u207B\xB2)\u02B3 = C(10,r)\u03BB\u02B3 x^((10\u2212r)/2 \u2212 2r).
For x\u2070: (10 \u2212 r)/2 = 2r, so 10 \u2212 r = 4r and r = 2.
The coefficient is C(10,2)\u03BB\xB2 = 45\u03BB\xB2.
Set 45\u03BB\xB2 = 720, so \u03BB\xB2 = 16 and the positive value is \u03BB = 4.
So the answer is option (a).`},{pn:158,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"Middle and greatest term",q:"The sum of the real values of x for which the middle term in the binomial expansion of (x\xB3/3 + 3/x)\u2078 equals 5670 is",o:["8","6","0","4"],c:2,s:`The power is 8 (even), so there are 9 terms and the middle one is T\u2085.
T\u2085 = C(8,4)(x\xB3/3)\u2074(3/x)\u2074 = 70\xB7(x\xB9\xB2/81)\xB7(81/x\u2074) = 70x\u2078.
Set 70x\u2078 = 5670, so x\u2078 = 81.
Then x\u2074 = 9 (x\u2074 cannot be negative), so x\xB2 = 3 and x = \u221A3 or x = \u2212\u221A3.
The real values are \u221A3 and \u2212\u221A3, and their sum is 0.
So the answer is option (c).`},{pn:159,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"The value of r for which \xB2\u2070C\u1D63\xB7\xB2\u2070C\u2080 + \xB2\u2070C\u1D63\u208B\u2081\xB7\xB2\u2070C\u2081 + \xB2\u2070C\u1D63\u208B\u2082\xB7\xB2\u2070C\u2082 + .... + \xB2\u2070C\u2080\xB7\xB2\u2070C\u1D63 is maximum, is",o:["20","10","15","11"],c:0,s:`Each product has the form \xB2\u2070C(r\u2212k)\xB7\xB2\u2070C\u2096, and the indices add up to r.
By Vandermonde's identity, C(m,r\u2212k)\xB7C(n,k) summed over k equals C(m+n, r).
Note: this identity comes from comparing the coefficient of x\u02B3 in (1 + x)\xB2\u2070(1 + x)\xB2\u2070 = (1 + x)\u2074\u2070.
So the sum equals \u2074\u2070C\u1D63.
The binomial coefficients \u2074\u2070C\u1D63 are largest at the middle, r = 40/2 = 20.
So the value is r = 20, which is option (a).`},{pn:160,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"General term",q:"A ratio of the 5th term from the beginning to the 5th term from the end in the binomial expansion of (2^(1/3) + 1/(2(3)^(1/3)))\xB9\u2070 is",o:["4(36)^(1/3) : 1","1 : 2(6)^(1/3)","2(36)^(1/3) : 1","1 : 4(16)^(1/3)"],c:0,s:`Let a = 2^(1/3) and b = 1/(2\xB73^(1/3)). The expansion has 11 terms.
The 5th term from the beginning is T\u2085 = C(10,4)a\u2076b\u2074.
The 5th term from the end is T\u2087 (since 11 \u2212 5 + 1 = 7) = C(10,6)a\u2074b\u2076.
Since C(10,4) = C(10,6), the ratio is a\u2076b\u2074 / (a\u2074b\u2076) = a\xB2/b\xB2.
a\xB2 = 2^(2/3) and b\xB2 = 1/(4\xB73^(2/3)), so a\xB2/b\xB2 = 4\xB72^(2/3)\xB73^(2/3) = 4\xB76^(2/3) = 4(36)^(1/3).
So the ratio is 4(36)^(1/3) : 1, which is option (a).`},{pn:161,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"num",st:"Counting terms",q:"The total number of irrational terms in the binomial expansion of (7^(1/5) \u2212 3^(1/10))\u2076\u2070 is",o:["55","54","48","49"],c:1,s:`The general term is C(60,r)(7^(1/5))^(60\u2212r)(\u22123^(1/10))\u02B3 = \xB1 C(60,r)\xB77^((60\u2212r)/5)\xB73^(r/10).
The term is rational only if (60 \u2212 r)/5 and r/10 are both integers.
r/10 is an integer when r is a multiple of 10. Then 60 \u2212 r is also a multiple of 10, so (60 \u2212 r)/5 is an integer automatically.
So r = 0, 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, which gives 7 rational terms.
The expansion has 61 terms in all, so the irrational terms number 61 \u2212 7 = 54.
So the answer is 54, which is option (b).`},{pn:162,y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"If n is the degree of the polynomial, [2/(\u221A(5x\xB3+1) \u2212 \u221A(5x\xB3\u22121))]\u2078 + [2/(\u221A(5x\xB3+1) + \u221A(5x\xB3\u22121))]\u2078 and m is the coefficient of x\u207F in it, then the ordered pair (n, m) is equal to",o:["(24, (10)\u2078)","(12, (20)\u2074)","(12, 8(10)\u2074)","(8, 5(10)\u2074)"],c:1,s:`Let p = \u221A(5x\xB3+1) and q = \u221A(5x\xB3\u22121). Then p\xB2 \u2212 q\xB2 = 2.
So 2/(p \u2212 q) = 2(p + q)/(p\xB2 \u2212 q\xB2) = p + q, and similarly 2/(p + q) = p \u2212 q.
The expression becomes (p + q)\u2078 + (p \u2212 q)\u2078 = 2[p\u2078 + C(8,2)p\u2076q\xB2 + C(8,4)p\u2074q\u2074 + C(8,6)p\xB2q\u2076 + q\u2078].
Put t = 5x\xB3: p\xB2 = t + 1 and q\xB2 = t \u2212 1, so each term p^(8\u2212r)q^r is a polynomial in t of degree 4 with leading coefficient 1.
The leading coefficient of t\u2074 is 2(1 + 28 + 70 + 28 + 1) = 256, so the degree in x is 3\xB74 = 12.
Since t\u2074 = 625x\xB9\xB2, the coefficient of x\xB9\xB2 is 256\xB7625 = 160000 = 20\u2074.
So (n, m) = (12, (20)\u2074), which is option (b).`},{pn:163,y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"coef",st:"Product of expansions",q:"The coefficient of x\xB9\u2070 in the expansion of (1 + x)\xB2(1 + x\xB2)\xB3(1 + x\xB3)\u2074 is equal to",o:["50","52","44","56"],c:1,s:`Write A = (1 + x)\xB2 = 1 + 2x + x\xB2, B = (1 + x\xB2)\xB3 = 1 + 3x\xB2 + 3x\u2074 + x\u2076, and C = (1 + x\xB3)\u2074 = 1 + 4x\xB3 + 6x\u2076 + 4x\u2079 + x\xB9\xB2.
First find the coefficients of A\xB7B at the powers we need. A\xB7B has even powers from B and 0, 1, 2 from A.
Coefficient of x\u2077 in AB: only x\xB9 (A, coefficient 2) with x\u2076 (B, 1) gives 2.
Coefficient of x\u2074 in AB: x\u2070\xB7x\u2074 gives 3, and x\xB2\xB7x\xB2 gives 1\xB73 = 3, so 6.
Coefficient of x\xB9 in AB: x\xB9\xB7x\u2070 gives 2. Coefficient of x\xB9\u2070 in AB is 0 (the highest power is 8).
Now pick the power of x from C: x\xB3 (coefficient 4) with x\u2077 of AB: 4\xB72 = 8; x\u2076 (coefficient 6) with x\u2074: 6\xB76 = 36; x\u2079 (coefficient 4) with x\xB9: 4\xB72 = 8; x\u2070 with x\xB9\u2070: 0.
Add: 8 + 36 + 8 = 52, which is option (b).`},{pn:164,y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"coef",st:"Product of expansions",q:"The coefficient of x\xB2 in the expansion of the product (2 \u2212 x\xB2)\xB7((1 + 2x + 3x\xB2)\u2076 + (1 \u2212 4x\xB2)\u2076) is",o:["155","106","108","107"],c:1,s:`First find the constant term and the x\xB2 coefficient of each bracket, then multiply by (2 \u2212 x\xB2).
In (1 + 2x + 3x\xB2)\u2076: the constant is 1. For x\xB2, either one factor gives 3x\xB2 (6 ways, 6\xB73 = 18) or two factors give 2x each (C(6,2) ways, 15\xB74 = 60). Total 78.
In (1 \u2212 4x\xB2)\u2076: the constant is 1, and the x\xB2 coefficient is 6\xB7(\u22124) = \u221224.
So the sum bracket has constant 1 + 1 = 2 and x\xB2 coefficient 78 \u2212 24 = 54.
In (2 \u2212 x\xB2)\xB7(2 + 54x\xB2 + ...), the x\xB2 coefficient is 2\xB754 + (\u22121)\xB72 = 108 \u2212 2 = 106.
So the answer is 106, which is option (b).`},{pn:165,y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"The coefficient of x\u207B\u2075 in the binomial expansion of ((x + 1)/(x^(2/3) \u2212 x^(1/3) + 1) \u2212 (x \u2212 1)/(x \u2212 x^(1/2)))\xB9\u2070, where x \u2260 0, 1, is",o:["1","\u22124","\u22121","4"],c:0,s:`Use a\xB3 + 1 = (a + 1)(a\xB2 \u2212 a + 1) with a = x^(1/3): x + 1 = (x^(1/3) + 1)(x^(2/3) \u2212 x^(1/3) + 1).
So the first fraction simplifies to x^(1/3) + 1.
For the second, x \u2212 1 = (\u221Ax \u2212 1)(\u221Ax + 1) and x \u2212 \u221Ax = \u221Ax(\u221Ax \u2212 1), so the fraction is (\u221Ax + 1)/\u221Ax = 1 + x^(\u22121/2).
The bracket becomes x^(1/3) + 1 \u2212 1 \u2212 x^(\u22121/2) = x^(1/3) \u2212 x^(\u22121/2).
The general term is C(10,r)(x^(1/3))^(10\u2212r)(\u2212x^(\u22121/2))\u02B3 with power (10 \u2212 r)/3 \u2212 r/2.
Set (10 \u2212 r)/3 \u2212 r/2 = \u22125: 20 \u2212 2r \u2212 3r = \u221230, so r = 10.
The coefficient is C(10,10)(\u22121)\xB9\u2070 = 1, which is option (a).`},{pn:166,y:"JEE Main 2016",tp:"num",st:"Counting terms",q:"If the number of terms in the expansion of (1 \u2212 2/x + 4/x\xB2)\u207F, x \u2260 0 is 28, then the sum of the coefficients of all the terms in this expansion is",o:["64","2187","243","729"],c:3,s:`Note: the intended count treats the three parts as separate symbols, so the number of terms of a trinomial power is C(n + 2, 2). (After merging powers of x the count would be odd, 2n + 1, so 28 can only come from this counting.)
Set C(n + 2, 2) = 28, so (n + 2)(n + 1) = 56.
Since 8\xB77 = 56, n + 2 = 8 and n = 6.
The sum of all the coefficients is found by putting x = 1: (1 \u2212 2 + 4)\u2076 = 3\u2076.
3\u2076 = 729.
So the sum is 729, which is option (d).`},{pn:167,y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"For x \u2208 R, x \u2260 \u22121, if (1 + x)\xB2\u2070\xB9\u2076 + x(1 + x)\xB2\u2070\xB9\u2075 + x\xB2(1 + x)\xB2\u2070\xB9\u2074 + .... + x\xB2\u2070\xB9\u2076 = \u03A3 (i = 0 to 2016) a\u1D62x\u2071, then a\u2081\u2087 is equal to",o:["2017!/(17! 2000!)","2016!/(17! 1999!)","2017!/2000!","2016!/16!"],c:0,s:`The sum is a geometric series with first term (1 + x)\xB2\u2070\xB9\u2076, common ratio x/(1 + x) and 2017 terms.
Its sum is [(1 + x)\xB2\u2070\xB9\u2077 \u2212 x\xB2\u2070\xB9\u2077] / [(1 + x) \u2212 x] = (1 + x)\xB2\u2070\xB9\u2077 \u2212 x\xB2\u2070\xB9\u2077.
Note: a geometric sum is (a\u02B3\u207F \u2212 b\u207F)/(a \u2212 b) when the ratio is b/a, and here a \u2212 b = (1 + x) \u2212 x = 1, which makes it simple.
The coefficient of x\xB9\u2077 in (1 + x)\xB2\u2070\xB9\u2077 \u2212 x\xB2\u2070\xB9\u2077 is C(2017, 17), since x\xB2\u2070\xB9\u2077 does not touch x\xB9\u2077.
C(2017, 17) = 2017!/(17!\xB72000!).
So a\u2081\u2087 = 2017!/(17! 2000!), which is option (a).`},{pn:168,y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"If the coefficients of x\u207B\xB2 and x\u207B\u2074 in the expansion of (x^(1/3) + 1/(2x^(1/3)))\xB9\u2078, (x > 0), are m and n respectively, then m/n is equal to",o:["27","182","5/4","4/5"],c:1,s:`The general term is C(18,r)(x^(1/3))^(18\u2212r)(1/(2x^(1/3)))\u02B3 = C(18,r)\xB72\u207B\u02B3\xB7x^((18 \u2212 2r)/3).
For x\u207B\xB2: (18 \u2212 2r)/3 = \u22122, so r = 12. Then m = C(18,12)/2\xB9\xB2 = 18564/2\xB9\xB2.
For x\u207B\u2074: (18 \u2212 2r)/3 = \u22124, so r = 15. Then n = C(18,15)/2\xB9\u2075 = 816/2\xB9\u2075.
So m/n = (18564/816)\xB72\xB3 = 22.75\xB78.
22.75\xB78 = 182.
So m/n = 182, which is option (b).`},{pn:169,y:"JEE Main 2015",tp:"sum",st:"Sums of binomial coefficients",q:"The sum of coefficients of integral powers of x in the binomial expansion of (1 \u2212 2\u221Ax)\u2075\u2070 is",o:["(1/2)(3\u2075\u2070 \u2212 1)","(1/2)(2\u2075\u2070 + 1)","(1/2)(3\u2075\u2070 + 1)","(1/2)(3\u2075\u2070)"],c:2,s:`The general term is C(50,r)(\u22122\u221Ax)\u02B3 = C(50,r)(\u22122)\u02B3 x^(r/2).
The power r/2 is an integer exactly when r is even.
Put y = \u221Ax, so f(y) = (1 \u2212 2y)\u2075\u2070 and we want the sum of coefficients of even powers of y.
f(1) = (1 \u2212 2)\u2075\u2070 = 1 and f(\u22121) = (1 + 2)\u2075\u2070 = 3\u2075\u2070.
The even-power sum is E = [f(1) + f(\u22121)]/2 = (1 + 3\u2075\u2070)/2.
Note: adding f(1) and f(\u22121) cancels odd powers of y and doubles the even ones, so we divide by 2.
So the required sum is (3\u2075\u2070 + 1)/2, which is option (c).`},{pn:170,y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"If the coefficients of the three successive terms in the binomial expansion of (1 + x)\u207F are in the ratio 1 : 7 : 42, then the first of these terms in the expansion is",o:["6th","7th","8th","9th"],c:1,s:`Let the three successive coefficients be C(n,r\u22121), C(n,r), C(n,r+1); the first of these is the rth term.
From 1 : 7: C(n,r)/C(n,r\u22121) = (n \u2212 r + 1)/r = 7, so n + 1 = 8r.
From 7 : 42: C(n,r+1)/C(n,r) = (n \u2212 r)/(r + 1) = 6, so n = 7r + 6.
Substitute n = 8r \u2212 1 into n = 7r + 6: 8r \u2212 1 = 7r + 6, so r = 7 and n = 55.
Check: (55 \u2212 7 + 1)/7 = 7 and (55 \u2212 7)/8 = 6, so the ratios are 1 : 7 : 42.
So the first of these terms is the 7th term, which is option (b).`},{pn:171,y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"The term independent of x in the binomial expansion of (1 \u2212 1/x + 3x\u2075)(2x\xB2 \u2212 1/x)\u2078 is",o:["400","496","\u2212400","\u2212496"],c:0,s:`The general term of (2x\xB2 \u2212 1/x)\u2078 is C(8,k)(2x\xB2)^(8\u2212k)(\u22121/x)\u1D4F, with power of x equal to 16 \u2212 3k.
The constant term of the product comes from three pairs: 1 with x\u2070, (\u22121/x) with x\xB9, and 3x\u2075 with x\u207B\u2075.
x\u2070 needs 16 \u2212 3k = 0, which has no whole-number k, so this pair gives 0.
x\xB9 needs 16 \u2212 3k = 1, so k = 5: coefficient C(8,5)\xB72\xB3\xB7(\u22121)\u2075 = 56\xB78\xB7(\u22121) = \u2212448. Times (\u22121) from (\u22121/x) gives +448.
x\u207B\u2075 needs 16 \u2212 3k = \u22125, so k = 7: coefficient C(8,7)\xB72\xB7(\u22121)\u2077 = \u221216. Times 3 gives \u221248.
Add: 448 \u2212 48 = 400, which is option (a).`},{pn:172,y:"JEE Main 2014",tp:"coef",st:"Product of expansions",q:"If the coefficients of x\xB3 and x\u2074 in the expansion of (1 + ax + bx\xB2)(1 \u2212 2x)\xB9\u2078 in powers of x are both zero, then (a, b) is equal to",o:["(14, 251/3)","(14, 272/3)","(16, 272/3)","(16, 251/3)"],c:2,s:`The coefficients of (1 \u2212 2x)\xB9\u2078 are: x\xB9: \u221236, x\xB2: C(18,2)\xB74 = 612, x\xB3: C(18,3)\xB7(\u22128) = \u22126528, x\u2074: C(18,4)\xB716 = 48960.
Coefficient of x\xB3 in the product: \u22126528 + a(612) + b(\u221236) = 0.
Coefficient of x\u2074 in the product: 48960 + a(\u22126528) + b(612) = 0.
Test option (c), a = 16 and b = 272/3: first equation gives \u22126528 + 9792 \u2212 3264 = 0.
Second equation gives 48960 \u2212 104448 + 55488 = 0.
Both hold, so (a, b) = (16, 272/3), which is option (c).`},{pn:173,y:"JEE Main 2013",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"The term independent of x in expansion of ((x + 1)/(x^(2/3) \u2212 x^(1/3) + 1) \u2212 (x \u2212 1)/(x \u2212 x^(1/2)))\xB9\u2070 is",o:["120","210","310","4"],c:1,s:`Use a\xB3 + 1 = (a + 1)(a\xB2 \u2212 a + 1) with a = x^(1/3): the first fraction becomes x^(1/3) + 1.
For the second fraction, x \u2212 1 = (\u221Ax \u2212 1)(\u221Ax + 1) and x \u2212 \u221Ax = \u221Ax(\u221Ax \u2212 1), so it equals (\u221Ax + 1)/\u221Ax = 1 + x^(\u22121/2).
The bracket is x^(1/3) + 1 \u2212 1 \u2212 x^(\u22121/2) = x^(1/3) \u2212 x^(\u22121/2).
The general term is C(10,r)(x^(1/3))^(10\u2212r)(\u2212x^(\u22121/2))\u02B3, with power (10 \u2212 r)/3 \u2212 r/2.
For the term independent of x: (10 \u2212 r)/3 = r/2, so 20 \u2212 2r = 3r and r = 4.
The term is C(10,4)(\u22121)\u2074 = 210, which is option (b).`},{pn:174,y:"AIEEE 2011",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"The coefficient of x\u2077 in the expansion of (1 \u2212 x \u2212 x\xB2 + x\xB3)\u2076 is",o:["\u2212144","132","144","\u2212132"],c:0,s:`Factor: 1 \u2212 x \u2212 x\xB2 + x\xB3 = (1 \u2212 x) \u2212 x\xB2(1 \u2212 x) = (1 \u2212 x)(1 \u2212 x\xB2).
So the expression is (1 \u2212 x)\u2076(1 \u2212 x\xB2)\u2076.
(1 \u2212 x)\u2076 has terms 1, \u22126x, 15x\xB2, \u221220x\xB3, 15x\u2074, \u22126x\u2075, x\u2076. (1 \u2212 x\xB2)\u2076 has terms 1, \u22126x\xB2, 15x\u2074, \u221220x\u2076, ...
For x\u2077 we need an odd power from the first and an even power from the second: x\xB9\xB7x\u2076, x\xB3\xB7x\u2074, x\u2075\xB7x\xB2.
x\xB9\xB7x\u2076: (\u22126)(\u221220) = 120. x\xB3\xB7x\u2074: (\u221220)(15) = \u2212300. x\u2075\xB7x\xB2: (\u22126)(\u22126) = 36.
Add: 120 \u2212 300 + 36 = \u2212144, which is option (a).`},{pn:175,y:"AIEEE 2007",tp:"exp",st:"General term",q:"In the binomial expansion of (a \u2212 b)\u207F, n \u2265 5, the sum of 5th and 6th terms is zero, then a/b equals",o:["(n \u2212 5)/6","(n \u2212 4)/5","5/(n \u2212 4)","6/(n \u2212 5)"],c:1,s:`The 5th term is T\u2085 = C(n,4)a\u207F\u207B\u2074(\u2212b)\u2074 = C(n,4)a\u207F\u207B\u2074b\u2074.
The 6th term is T\u2086 = C(n,5)a\u207F\u207B\u2075(\u2212b)\u2075 = \u2212C(n,5)a\u207F\u207B\u2075b\u2075.
Their sum is zero, so C(n,4)a\u207F\u207B\u2074b\u2074 = C(n,5)a\u207F\u207B\u2075b\u2075.
Cancel a\u207F\u207B\u2075b\u2074: C(n,4)\xB7a = C(n,5)\xB7b.
So a/b = C(n,5)/C(n,4) = (n \u2212 4)/5.
So a/b = (n \u2212 4)/5, which is option (b).`},{pn:176,y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"If the coefficient of x\u2077 in (ax\xB2 + 1/(bx))\xB9\xB9 equals the coefficient of x\u207B\u2077 in (ax \u2212 1/(bx\xB2))\xB9\xB9, then a and b satisfy the relation",o:["a + b = 1","a \u2212 b = 1","ab = 1","a/b = 1"],c:2,s:`In (ax\xB2 + 1/(bx))\xB9\xB9 the general term is C(11,r)a^(11\u2212r)x^(22\u22122r)\xB7b\u207B\u02B3x\u207B\u02B3, with power 22 \u2212 3r.
For x\u2077: 22 \u2212 3r = 7, so r = 5. The coefficient is C(11,5)a\u2076/b\u2075.
In (ax \u2212 1/(bx\xB2))\xB9\xB9 the general term has power (11 \u2212 r) \u2212 2r = 11 \u2212 3r.
For x\u207B\u2077: 11 \u2212 3r = \u22127, so r = 6. The coefficient is C(11,6)a\u2075(\u22121)\u2076/b\u2076 = C(11,6)a\u2075/b\u2076.
Since C(11,5) = C(11,6) = 462, equate: a\u2076/b\u2075 = a\u2075/b\u2076.
Divide by a\u2075/b\u2076 (a, b nonzero): ab = 1, which is option (c).`},{pn:177,y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"exp",st:"Middle and greatest term",q:"The coefficient of the middle term in the binomial expansion in powers of x of (1 + \u03B1x)\u2074 and of (1 \u2212 \u03B1x)\u2076 is the same if \u03B1 equals",o:["\u22123/10","10/3","\u22125/3","3/5"],c:0,s:`(1 + \u03B1x)\u2074 has 5 terms, so the middle term is the 3rd: C(4,2)\u03B1\xB2x\xB2, with coefficient 6\u03B1\xB2.
(1 \u2212 \u03B1x)\u2076 has 7 terms, so the middle term is the 4th: C(6,3)(\u2212\u03B1)\xB3x\xB3, with coefficient \u221220\u03B1\xB3.
Set 6\u03B1\xB2 = \u221220\u03B1\xB3.
Divide by \u03B1\xB2 (\u03B1 \u2260 0): 6 = \u221220\u03B1.
So \u03B1 = \u22126/20 = \u22123/10.
So the answer is option (a).`},{pn:178,y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"coef",st:"Product of expansions",q:"The coefficient of x\u207F in expansion of (1 + x)(1 \u2212 x)\u207F is",o:["(\u22121)\u207F\u207B\xB9(n \u2212 1)\xB2","(\u22121)\u207F(1 \u2212 n)","(n \u2212 1)","(\u22121)\u207F\u207B\xB9n"],c:1,s:`Multiply out: (1 + x)(1 \u2212 x)\u207F = (1 \u2212 x)\u207F + x(1 \u2212 x)\u207F.
The coefficient of x\u207F in (1 \u2212 x)\u207F is C(n,n)(\u22121)\u207F = (\u22121)\u207F.
The coefficient of x\u207F in x(1 \u2212 x)\u207F is the coefficient of x\u207F\u207B\xB9 in (1 \u2212 x)\u207F, which is C(n,n\u22121)(\u22121)\u207F\u207B\xB9 = n(\u22121)\u207F\u207B\xB9.
Add: (\u22121)\u207F + n(\u22121)\u207F\u207B\xB9 = (\u22121)\u207F \u2212 n(\u22121)\u207F.
Take (\u22121)\u207F common: (\u22121)\u207F(1 \u2212 n).
So the coefficient is (\u22121)\u207F(1 \u2212 n), which is option (b).`},{pn:179,y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"gen",st:"Index not a positive integer",q:"If x is positive, the first negative term in the expansion of (1 + x)^(27/5) is",o:["5th term","8th term","6th term","7th term"],c:1,s:`For an index that is not a whole number, the (r + 1)th term is [n(n\u22121)...(n\u2212r+1)/r!]x\u02B3 with n = 27/5.
Since x is positive, the sign is decided by the product n(n \u2212 1)...(n \u2212 r + 1).
The factors are 27/5, 22/5, 17/5, 12/5, 7/5, 2/5, \u22123/5, ... (each is 5 less in the numerator).
The first six factors (r up to 6) are all positive, so the terms up to T\u2087 (r = 6) are positive.
The seventh factor is \u22123/5, so the term with r = 7 is the first negative one.
That is the (r + 1) = 8th term, which is option (b).`},{pn:180,y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"num",st:"Counting terms",q:"The number of integral terms in the expansion of (\u221A3 + \u2078\u221A5)\xB2\u2075\u2076 is",o:["33","34","35","32"],c:0,s:`The general term is C(256,r)(\u221A3)^(256\u2212r)(5^(1/8))\u02B3 = C(256,r)\xB73^((256\u2212r)/2)\xB75^(r/8).
The term is an integer when (256 \u2212 r)/2 and r/8 are both whole numbers.
r/8 is whole when r is a multiple of 8. Then 256 \u2212 r is also a multiple of 8, so (256 \u2212 r)/2 is whole automatically.
So r = 0, 8, 16, ..., 256, which is r = 8k with k = 0, 1, ..., 32.
That is 33 values.
So there are 33 integral terms, which is option (a).`},{pn:181,y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"r and n are positive integers, r > 1, n > 2 and the coefficient of the (r + 2)th term and the 3rth term in the expansion of (1 + x)\xB2\u207F are equal, then n equals",o:["3r","3r + 1","2r","2r + 1"],c:2,s:`The general term of (1 + x)\xB2\u207F is T(k+1) = C(2n, k)\xB7x\u1D4F, so its coefficient is C(2n, k).
The (r + 2)th term has k = r + 1, so its coefficient is C(2n, r + 1).
The 3rth term has k = 3r \u2212 1, so its coefficient is C(2n, 3r \u2212 1).
Note: C(2n, a) = C(2n, b) holds when a = b or when a + b = 2n, because C(2n, k) = C(2n, 2n \u2212 k).
The case r + 1 = 3r \u2212 1 gives r = 1, which is not allowed because r > 1.
So (r + 1) + (3r \u2212 1) = 2n, which gives 4r = 2n and n = 2r.
Check with r = 2, n = 4: C(8, 3) = C(8, 5) = 56, true. So n = 2r, option (c).`},{pn:182,y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"The coefficients of x\u1D56 and x\u1D60 in the expansion of (1 + x)\u1D56\u207A\u1D60 are",o:["equal","equal with opposite signs","reciprocals of each other","none of these"],c:0,s:`In (1 + x)\u1D56\u207A\u1D60 the coefficient of x\u1D56 is C(p + q, p).
The coefficient of x\u1D60 is C(p + q, q).
Note: C(n, r) = C(n, n \u2212 r), because choosing r objects out of n is the same as leaving out n \u2212 r objects.
Here n = p + q, so C(p + q, p) = C(p + q, q).
Both coefficients have the same sign (all are positive), so they are equal, which is option (a).`},{pn:183,y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"exp",st:"Middle and greatest term",q:"If the sum of the coefficients in the expansion of (a + b)\u207F is 4096, then the greatest coefficient in the expansion is",o:["1594","792","924","2924"],c:2,s:`The sum of the coefficients is found by putting a = b = 1, which gives 2\u207F.
So 2\u207F = 4096 = 2\xB9\xB2, which gives n = 12.
The coefficients are C(12, r). For an even n the greatest one is the middle coefficient, C(12, 6).
Note: C(n, r) increases up to r = n/2 and then decreases, because C(n, r + 1)/C(n, r) = (n \u2212 r)/(r + 1) is more than 1 only while r < (n \u2212 1)/2.
C(12, 6) = 12!/(6!\xB76!) = 665280/720 = 924.
So the greatest coefficient is 924, which is option (c).`},{pn:184,y:"IIT-JEE 1999",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"If in the expression of (1 + x)\u1D50(1 \u2212 x)\u207F, the coefficient of x and x\xB2 are 3 and \u22126 respectively, then m is",o:["6","9","12","24"],c:2,s:`Expand each factor: (1 + x)\u1D50 = 1 + mx + C(m, 2)x\xB2 + ..., and (1 \u2212 x)\u207F = 1 \u2212 nx + C(n, 2)x\xB2 \u2212 ...
The coefficient of x in the product is m \u2212 n, so m \u2212 n = 3.
The coefficient of x\xB2 is C(m, 2) + C(n, 2) \u2212 mn. Writing it out: m(m \u2212 1)/2 + n(n \u2212 1)/2 \u2212 mn = \u22126.
Multiply by 2: m\xB2 \u2212 m + n\xB2 \u2212 n \u2212 2mn = \u221212, that is (m \u2212 n)\xB2 \u2212 (m + n) = \u221212.
Put m \u2212 n = 3: 9 \u2212 (m + n) = \u221212, so m + n = 21.
Solving m \u2212 n = 3 and m + n = 21 gives m = 12 and n = 9. (Checked by computer: C(12,2) + C(9,2) \u2212 108 = 66 + 36 \u2212 108 = \u22126.)
So m = 12, which is option (c).`},{pn:185,y:"IIT-JEE 1983",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"The coefficient of x\u2074 in (x/2 \u2212 3/x\xB2)\xB9\u2070 is",o:["405/256","504/259","450/263","none of these"],c:0,s:`The general term is T(r+1) = C(10, r)\xB7(x/2)\xB9\u2070\u207B\u02B3\xB7(\u22123/x\xB2)\u02B3.
The power of x is (10 \u2212 r) \u2212 2r = 10 \u2212 3r.
We need 10 \u2212 3r = 4, so r = 2.
The coefficient is C(10, 2)\xB7(1/2)\u2078\xB7(\u22123)\xB2 = 45 \xB7 (1/256) \xB7 9.
45 \xD7 9 = 405, so the coefficient is 405/256 (confirmed by exact computer expansion).
This is option (a).`},{pn:186,y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"coef",st:"Product of expansions",q:"Coefficient of x\xB9\xB9 in the expansion of (1 + x\xB2)\u2074(1 + x\xB3)\u2077(1 + x\u2074)\xB9\xB2 is",o:["1051","1106","1113","1120"],c:2,s:`Pick a power from each bracket: x\xB2\u1D43 from (1 + x\xB2)\u2074, x\xB3\u1D47 from (1 + x\xB3)\u2077, x\u2074\u1D9C from (1 + x\u2074)\xB9\xB2.
We need 2a + 3b + 4c = 11 with a \u2264 4, b \u2264 7, c \u2264 12.
Since 11 is odd, b must be odd. Take b = 1: 2a + 4c = 8, so (a, c) = (4, 0), (2, 1), (0, 2).
Take b = 3: 2a + 4c = 2, so (a, c) = (1, 0).
b = 5 would need 3b = 15 > 11, so it is not possible.
The coefficient of each choice is C(4, a)\xB7C(7, b)\xB7C(12, c): b = 1 gives 7\xB7(1\xB71 + 6\xB712 + 1\xB766) = 7\xB7139 = 973; b = 3 gives 35\xB7(4\xB71) = 140.
Total = 973 + 140 = 1113 (confirmed by computer), which is option (c).`},{pn:187,y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"For r = 0, 1, ..., 10, let A\u1D63, B\u1D63 and C\u1D63 denote respectively the coefficient of x\u02B3 in the expansions of (1 + x)\xB9\u2070, (1 + x)\xB2\u2070 and (1 + x)\xB3\u2070. Then \u03A3 (r=1 to 10) A\u1D63(B\u2081\u2080B\u1D63 \u2212 C\u2081\u2080A\u1D63) is equal to",o:["B\u2081\u2080 \u2212 C\u2081\u2080","A\u2081\u2080(B\u2081\u2080\xB2 \u2212 C\u2081\u2080A\u2081\u2080)","0","C\u2081\u2080 \u2212 B\u2081\u2080"],c:3,s:`Split the sum: B\u2081\u2080\xB7\u03A3 A\u1D63B\u1D63 \u2212 C\u2081\u2080\xB7\u03A3 A\u1D63\xB2, with r from 1 to 10.
\u03A3 A\u1D63B\u1D63 = \u03A3 C(10, r)\xB7C(20, r) = \u03A3 C(10, r)\xB7C(20, 20 \u2212 r). By Vandermonde (choose 20 from 10 + 20 objects) the sum over r = 0 to 10 is C(30, 20) = C\u2081\u2080.
Removing the r = 0 term (which is 1) gives \u03A3 (r=1 to 10) A\u1D63B\u1D63 = C\u2081\u2080 \u2212 1.
\u03A3 A\u1D63\xB2 = \u03A3 C(10, r)\xB7C(10, 10 \u2212 r) over r = 0 to 10 is C(20, 10) = B\u2081\u2080, so from r = 1 it is B\u2081\u2080 \u2212 1.
Note: Vandermonde says \u03A3 C(m, r)\xB7C(n, k \u2212 r) = C(m + n, k), the coefficient of x\u1D4F in (1 + x)\u1D50(1 + x)\u207F.
The value is B\u2081\u2080(C\u2081\u2080 \u2212 1) \u2212 C\u2081\u2080(B\u2081\u2080 \u2212 1) = \u2212B\u2081\u2080 + C\u2081\u2080 = C\u2081\u2080 \u2212 B\u2081\u2080, which is option (d).`},{pn:188,y:"IIT-JEE 2003",tp:"coef",st:"Product of expansions",q:"Coefficient of t\xB2\u2074 in (1 + t\xB2)\xB9\xB2(1 + t\xB9\xB2)(1 + t\xB2\u2074) is",o:["\xB9\xB2C\u2086 + 3","\xB9\xB2C\u2086 + 1","\xB9\xB2C\u2086","\xB9\xB2C\u2086 + 2"],c:3,s:`Choose a term from each bracket so the powers of t add to 24.
The second bracket gives 1 or t\xB9\xB2, the third gives 1 or t\xB2\u2074.
Case 1: take 1 from both. We need t\xB2\u2074 from (1 + t\xB2)\xB9\xB2, i.e. (t\xB2)\xB9\xB2, with coefficient C(12, 12) = 1.
Case 2: take t\xB9\xB2 from the second bracket and 1 from the third. We need t\xB9\xB2 from (1 + t\xB2)\xB9\xB2, i.e. (t\xB2)\u2076, with coefficient C(12, 6).
Case 3: take t\xB2\u2074 from the third bracket and 1 from the second. We need t\u2070 from the first, coefficient 1.
Taking t\xB9\xB2 and t\xB2\u2074 together gives t\xB3\u2076, too large.
Total = 1 + C(12, 6) + 1 = \xB9\xB2C\u2086 + 2, which is option (d).`},{pn:189,y:"IIT-JEE 2002",tp:"sum",st:"Sums of binomial coefficients",q:"The sum \u03A3 (i=0 to m) C(10, i)\xB7C(20, m \u2212 i) is maximum when m is",o:["5","10","15","20"],c:2,s:`The sum is the coefficient of x\u1D50 in (1 + x)\xB9\u2070(1 + x)\xB2\u2070, which is the same as in (1 + x)\xB3\u2070.
Note: this is Vandermonde's identity, because multiplying the two expansions and collecting x\u1D50 gives exactly this sum.
So the sum equals C(30, m).
C(30, m) is largest at the middle, m = 30/2 = 15, since the row of Pascal's triangle rises to the centre and falls after it.
A computer check of C(30, m) for m = 0 to 30 gives the maximum 155117520 at m = 15.
So m = 15, which is option (c).`},{pn:190,y:"JEE Advanced 2025",tp:"exp",st:"Middle and greatest term",q:"Let a\u2080, a\u2081, ..., a\u2082\u2083 be real numbers such that (1 + 2x/5)\xB2\xB3 = \u03A3 (i=0 to 23) a\u1D62x\u2071 for every real number x. Let a\u1D63 be the largest among the numbers a\u2C7C for 0 \u2264 j \u2264 23. Then the value of r is",n:6,s:`The coefficient is a\u2C7C = C(23, j)\xB7(2/5)\u02B2, all positive.
Compare neighbours: a(j+1)/a\u2C7C = [(23 \u2212 j)/(j + 1)]\xB7(2/5).
Note: the coefficients keep rising while this ratio is more than 1, and start falling once it is less than 1.
The ratio is at least 1 when 2(23 \u2212 j) \u2265 5(j + 1), i.e. 46 \u2212 2j \u2265 5j + 5, i.e. j \u2264 41/7 \u2248 5.86.
So a\u2080 < a\u2081 < ... < a\u2086 (the last increase is from j = 5 to 6) and then a\u2087 < a\u2086.
So the largest is a\u2086, and r = 6.`},{pn:191,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"If the constant term in the expansion of (1 + 2x \u2212 3x\xB3)((3/2)x\xB2 \u2212 1/(3x))\u2079 is p, then 108p is equal to",n:54,s:`Let the second factor be (3x\xB2/2 \u2212 1/(3x))\u2079. Its general term is C(9, r)(3x\xB2/2)\u2079\u207B\u02B3(\u22121/(3x))\u02B3, with power of x equal to 18 \u2212 2r \u2212 r = 18 \u2212 3r.
The constant term of the product comes from 1\xB7(x\u2070 term) + 2x\xB7(x\u207B\xB9 term) \u2212 3x\xB3\xB7(x\u207B\xB3 term).
x\u2070: 18 \u2212 3r = 0, r = 6: C(9, 6)(3/2)\xB3(\u22121/3)\u2076 = 84\xB7(27/8)\xB7(1/729) = 7/18.
x\u207B\xB9: 18 \u2212 3r = \u22121 gives no whole r, so this term is absent.
x\u207B\xB3: 18 \u2212 3r = \u22123, r = 7: C(9, 7)(3/2)\xB2(\u22121/3)\u2077 = 36\xB7(9/4)\xB7(\u22121/2187) = \u22121/27.
So p = 7/18 + 0 \u2212 3\xB7(\u22121/27) = 7/18 + 1/9 = 9/18 = 1/2 (exact fractions confirmed by computer).
Then 108p = 108 \xD7 1/2 = 54.`},{pn:192,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"General term",q:"If the second, third and fourth terms in the expansion of (x + y)\u207F are 135, 30 and 10/3, respectively, then 6(n\xB3 + x\xB2 + y) is equal to",n:806,s:`The terms are T\u2082 = C(n,1)x\u207F\u207B\xB9y, T\u2083 = C(n,2)x\u207F\u207B\xB2y\xB2, T\u2084 = C(n,3)x\u207F\u207B\xB3y\xB3.
Divide: T\u2083/T\u2082 = ((n \u2212 1)/2)(y/x) = 30/135 = 2/9, and T\u2084/T\u2083 = ((n \u2212 2)/3)(y/x) = (10/3)/30 = 1/9.
Divide the first ratio by the second: 3(n \u2212 1)/(2(n \u2212 2)) = 2, so 3n \u2212 3 = 4n \u2212 8 and n = 5.
Then (4/2)(y/x) = 2/9 gives y/x = 1/9, that is y = x/9.
T\u2082 = 5x\u2074y = 5x\u2075/9 = 135, so x\u2075 = 243 and x = 3. Hence y = 1/3.
6(n\xB3 + x\xB2 + y) = 6(125 + 9 + 1/3) = 750 + 54 + 2 = 806.`},{pn:193,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Product of expansions",q:"The coefficient of x\xB2\u2070\xB9\xB2 in the expansion of (1 \u2212 x)\xB2\u2070\u2070\u2078(1 + x + x\xB2)\xB2\u2070\u2070\u2077 is equal to",n:0,s:`Use 1 \u2212 x\xB3 = (1 \u2212 x)(1 + x + x\xB2), so (1 + x + x\xB2)\xB2\u2070\u2070\u2077 = (1 \u2212 x\xB3)\xB2\u2070\u2070\u2077/(1 \u2212 x)\xB2\u2070\u2070\u2077.
The product becomes (1 \u2212 x)\xB7(1 \u2212 x\xB3)\xB2\u2070\u2070\u2077.
Note: this trick merges the two brackets so that only powers of x\xB3 appear inside the big bracket.
(1 \u2212 x\xB3)\xB2\u2070\u2070\u2077 has only powers x\u2070, x\xB3, x\u2076, ... that are multiples of 3.
The product with 1 gives multiples of 3, and with \u2212x gives powers that are 1 more than a multiple of 3.
2012 = 3 \xD7 670 + 2 leaves remainder 2, so it is neither a multiple of 3 nor 1 more than one.
So the coefficient is 0 (also confirmed by exact computer expansion).`},{pn:194,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"num",st:"Counting terms",q:"Number of integral terms in the expansion of \uFF5B7^(1/2) + 11^(1/6)\uFF5D\u2078\xB2\u2074 is equal to",n:138,s:`The general term is C(824, r)\xB77^((824 \u2212 r)/2)\xB711^(r/6).
The term is an integer when (824 \u2212 r)/2 and r/6 are both whole numbers.
r/6 whole means r is a multiple of 6, so r is even, and then 824 \u2212 r is also even automatically.
So r = 0, 6, 12, ..., up to 824. The last multiple of 6 not above 824 is 822 = 6 \xD7 137.
The values 6 \xD7 0 to 6 \xD7 137 are 138 numbers (a computer count gives the same 138).
So the number of integral terms is 138.`},{pn:195,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"coef",st:"Product of expansions",q:"In the expansion of (1 + x)(1 \u2212 x\xB2)(1 + 3/x + 3/x\xB2 + 1/x\xB3)\u2075, x \u2260 0, the sum of the coefficients of x\xB3 and x\u207B\xB9\xB3 is equal to",n:118,s:`Note: 1 + 3/x + 3/x\xB2 + 1/x\xB3 = (1 + 1/x)\xB3, so the last factor is (1 + 1/x)\xB9\u2075.
Also (1 + x)(1 \u2212 x\xB2) = 1 + x \u2212 x\xB2 \u2212 x\xB3.
The coefficient of x\u1D4F in (1 + 1/x)\xB9\u2075 is C(15, \u2212k) for k from \u221215 to 0, since the general term is C(15, j)x\u207B\u02B2.
Coefficient of x\xB3: the second factor only has powers x\u2070 and below, so the x\xB3 term must come entirely from the first factor, which has \u2212x\xB3 with x\u2070 term 1. The other terms 1, x, \u2212x\xB2 would need x\xB3, x\xB2 and x\xB9 from the second factor, which are positive powers, which are absent, so the coefficient is \u22121.
Coefficient of x\u207B\xB9\xB3: take x\u207B\xB9\xB3 from 1, x\u207B\xB9\u2074 from x, x\u207B\xB9\u2075 from \u2212x\xB2: C(15, 13) + C(15, 14) \u2212 C(15, 15) = 105 + 15 \u2212 1 = 119. The \u2212x\xB3 term would need x\u207B\xB9\u2076, which is absent.
The sum is \u22121 + 119 = 118 (exact computer expansion gives \u22121 and 119).`},{pn:196,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"The coefficient of x\xB9\u2078 in the expansion of (x\u2074 \u2212 1/x\xB3)\xB9\u2075 is",n:5005,s:`The general term is C(15, r)(x\u2074)\xB9\u2075\u207B\u02B3(\u22121/x\xB3)\u02B3 = C(15, r)(\u22121)\u02B3 x\u2076\u2070\u207B\u2077\u02B3.
We need 60 \u2212 7r = 18, so 7r = 42 and r = 6.
The coefficient is C(15, 6)(\u22121)\u2076 = C(15, 6).
C(15, 6) = 15\xB714\xB713\xB712\xB711\xB710/720 = 3603600/720 = 5005.
So the coefficient is 5005.`},{pn:197,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"If the constant term in the expansion of (3x\xB2 \u2212 1/(2x\u2075))\u2077 is \u03B1, then [\u03B1] is equal to",n:1275,s:`The general term is C(7, r)(3x\xB2)\u2077\u207B\u02B3(\u22121/(2x\u2075))\u02B3, with power of x equal to 2(7 \u2212 r) \u2212 5r = 14 \u2212 7r.
The constant term needs 14 \u2212 7r = 0, so r = 2.
\u03B1 = C(7, 2)\xB73\u2075\xB7(\u22121/2)\xB2 = 21 \xD7 243 \xD7 1/4 = 5103/4.
5103/4 = 1275.75.
The greatest integer [\u03B1] is 1275 (the bracket [ ] means the greatest integer not above the number).`},{pn:198,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"num",st:"Counting terms",q:"The number of integral terms in the expansion of (3^(1/2) + 5^(1/4))\u2076\u2078\u2070 is equal to",n:171,s:`The general term is C(680, r)\xB73^((680 \u2212 r)/2)\xB75^(r/4).
For an integer term, r/4 must be a whole number, so r is a multiple of 4.
Then 680 \u2212 r is even too, so the power of 3 is whole automatically.
The possible r are 0, 4, 8, ..., 680.
There are 680/4 + 1 = 171 of these (a computer count also gives 171).
So the number of integral terms is 171.`},{pn:199,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"Let \u03B1 be the constant term in the binomial expansion of (\u221Ax \u2212 6/x^(3/2))\u207F, n \u2264 15. If the sum of the coefficients of the remaining terms in the expansion is 649 and the coefficient of x\u207B\u207F is \u03BB\u03B1, then \u03BB is equal to",n:36,s:`The general term is C(n, r)(\u221Ax)\u207F\u207B\u02B3(\u22126x^(\u22123/2))\u02B3, with power of x equal to (n \u2212 r)/2 \u2212 3r/2 = (n \u2212 4r)/2.
A constant term needs n = 4r, so n is a multiple of 4: 4, 8, 12.
Sum of all coefficients is (1 \u2212 6)\u207F = (\u22125)\u207F, so the remaining terms add up to (\u22125)\u207F \u2212 \u03B1 = 649.
Try n = 4: r = 1, \u03B1 = C(4, 1)(\u22126) = \u221224, and 625 \u2212 (\u221224) = 649. This works. (n = 8 gives 389617 and n = 12 gives 244188145, so they do not.)
For n = 4 the coefficient of x\u207B\u2074 needs (4 \u2212 4r)/2 = \u22124, so r = 3: C(4, 3)(\u22126)\xB3 = \u2212864.
\u2212864 = \u03BB \xD7 (\u221224), so \u03BB = 36.`},{pn:200,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"Let the sixth term in the binomial expansion of (\u221A(2^(log\u2082(10 \u2212 3\u02E3))) + \u2075\u221A(2^((x \u2212 2)log\u20823)))\u1D50, in the increasing powers of 2^((x \u2212 2)log\u20823)/5, be 21. If the binomial coefficients of the second, third and fourth terms in the expansion are respectively the first, third and fifth terms of an A.P., then the sum of the squares of all possible values of x is",n:4,s:`Using 2^(log\u2082 a) = a, the first part is \u221A(10 \u2212 3\u02E3) and the second part is (3^(x\u22122))^(1/5).
The coefficients C(m, 1), C(m, 2), C(m, 3) are the 1st, 3rd and 5th terms of an A.P., so the 3rd is the average of the 1st and 5th gap: 2\xB7C(m, 2) = C(m, 1) + C(m, 3).
This gives m(m \u2212 1) = m + m(m \u2212 1)(m \u2212 2)/6, which simplifies to m\xB2 \u2212 9m + 14 = 0, so m = 7 (m = 2 is too small to have a fourth term).
The sixth term of the expansion is C(7, 5)(\u221A(10 \u2212 3\u02E3))\xB2 (3^(x\u22122)) = 21(10 \u2212 3\u02E3)\xB73\u02E3/9.
Setting it equal to 21 gives (10 \u2212 y)y = 9 with y = 3\u02E3, so y\xB2 \u2212 10y + 9 = 0 and y = 1 or 9.
So 3\u02E3 = 1 gives x = 0, and 3\u02E3 = 9 gives x = 2.
The sum of squares is 0 + 4 = 4.`},{pn:201,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"If the term without x in the expansion of (x^(2/3) + \u03B1/x\xB3)\xB2\xB2 is 7315, then |\u03B1| is equal to",n:1,s:`The general term is C(22, r)(x^(2/3))\xB2\xB2\u207B\u02B3(\u03B1/x\xB3)\u02B3 with power of x equal to (2/3)(22 \u2212 r) \u2212 3r.
Set it to 0: 44 \u2212 2r \u2212 9r = 0, so 11r = 44 and r = 4.
The constant term is C(22, 4)\u03B1\u2074.
C(22, 4) = 22\xB721\xB720\xB719/24 = 175560/24 = 7315.
So 7315\u03B1\u2074 = 7315, hence \u03B1\u2074 = 1 and |\u03B1| = 1.`},{pn:202,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"The constant term in the expansion of (2x + 1/x\u2077 + 3x\xB2)\u2075 is",n:1080,s:`Write each term as 2x, x\u207B\u2077, 3x\xB2 with counts a, b, c where a + b + c = 5.
The power of x is a \u2212 7b + 2c, and it must be 0.
Put a = 5 \u2212 b \u2212 c: 5 \u2212 b \u2212 c \u2212 7b + 2c = 0, that is 5 \u2212 8b + c = 0, so c = 8b \u2212 5.
b = 1 gives c = 3 and a = 1 (b = 0 gives negative c, b = 2 gives c = 11, too big).
The term is 5!/(1!\xB71!\xB73!)\xB7(2)\xB9\xB7(1)\xB9\xB7(3)\xB3 = 20 \xD7 2 \xD7 27 = 1080.
Note: the multinomial coefficient 5!/(a!b!c!) counts the ways to pick which brackets give which part. Exact computer expansion also gives 1080.`},{pn:203,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"Let the coefficients of three consecutive terms in the binomial expansion of (1 + 2x)\u207F be in the ratio 2 : 5 : 8. Then the coefficient of the term, which is in the middle of these three terms, is",n:1120,s:`The coefficient of x\u02B3 is a\u1D63 = C(n, r)\xB72\u02B3. Let the three consecutive coefficients be a\u1D63\u208B\u2081, a\u1D63, a\u1D63\u208A\u2081.
a\u1D63/a\u1D63\u208B\u2081 = 2(n \u2212 r + 1)/r = 5/2, so 4(n \u2212 r + 1) = 5r, i.e. 4n + 4 = 9r.
a\u1D63\u208A\u2081/a\u1D63 = 2(n \u2212 r)/(r + 1) = 8/5, so 10(n \u2212 r) = 8(r + 1), i.e. 5n = 9r + 4.
Subtract: 5n \u2212 (4n + 4) = 4, so n = 8, and then 9r = 36, r = 4.
The middle coefficient is a\u2084 = C(8, 4)\xB72\u2074 = 70 \xD7 16 = 1120.
Check: a\u2083 = 56 \xD7 8 = 448, a\u2085 = 56 \xD7 32 = 1792, and 448 : 1120 : 1792 = 2 : 5 : 8.`},{pn:204,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"If the co-efficient of x\u2079 in (\u03B1x\xB3 + 1/(\u03B2x))\xB9\xB9 and the co-efficient of x\u207B\u2079 in (\u03B1x \u2212 1/(\u03B2x\xB3))\xB9\xB9 are equal, then (\u03B1\u03B2)\xB2 is equal to",n:1,s:`First expansion: C(11, r)(\u03B1x\xB3)\xB9\xB9\u207B\u02B3(1/(\u03B2x))\u02B3 has power 33 \u2212 3r \u2212 r = 33 \u2212 4r. For x\u2079, 4r = 24, r = 6.
The coefficient is C(11, 6)\u03B1\u2075/\u03B2\u2076.
Second expansion: C(11, r)(\u03B1x)\xB9\xB9\u207B\u02B3(\u22121/(\u03B2x\xB3))\u02B3 has power 11 \u2212 r \u2212 3r = 11 \u2212 4r. For x\u207B\u2079, 4r = 20, r = 5.
The coefficient is C(11, 5)\u03B1\u2076(\u22121)\u2075/\u03B2\u2075 = \u2212C(11, 5)\u03B1\u2076/\u03B2\u2075.
Since C(11, 6) = C(11, 5), equating gives \u03B1\u2075/\u03B2\u2076 = \u2212\u03B1\u2076/\u03B2\u2075.
Divide by \u03B1\u2075/\u03B2\u2075 (non-zero): 1/\u03B2 = \u2212\u03B1, so \u03B1\u03B2 = \u22121.
So (\u03B1\u03B2)\xB2 = 1.`},{pn:205,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"General term",q:"Let \u03B1 > 0, be the smallest number such that the expansion of (x^(2/3) + 2/x\xB3)\xB3\u2070 has a term \u03B2x\u207B\u03B1, \u03B2 \u2208 \u2115. Then \u03B1 is equal to",n:2,s:`The general term is C(30, r)(x^(2/3))\xB3\u2070\u207B\u02B3(2/x\xB3)\u02B3 with power of x equal to (2/3)(30 \u2212 r) \u2212 3r = 20 \u2212 11r/3.
We want the smallest positive \u03B1, i.e. the negative power closest to 0 among the powers that occur. Powers are 20 \u2212 11r/3 for r = 0 to 30.
Try r = 5: 20 \u2212 55/3 = 5/3 (positive). r = 6: 20 \u2212 22 = \u22122. So the first negative power is \u22122.
The next ones (r = 7: \u221217/3 and so on) are more negative, so no smaller \u03B1 exists. A computer listing of all 31 powers confirms \u22122 is the closest negative one.
At r = 6 the coefficient is C(30, 6)\xB72\u2076 = 38001600, a natural number, so \u03B2 is fine. So \u03B1 = 2.`},{pn:206,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"The coefficient of x\u207B\u2076, in the expansion of (4x/5 + 5/(2x\xB2))\u2079, is",n:5040,s:`The general term is C(9, r)(4x/5)\u2079\u207B\u02B3(5/(2x\xB2))\u02B3 with power of x equal to 9 \u2212 r \u2212 2r = 9 \u2212 3r.
We need 9 \u2212 3r = \u22126, so r = 5.
The coefficient is C(9, 5)(4/5)\u2074(5/2)\u2075.
(4/5)\u2074 = 256/625 and (5/2)\u2075 = 3125/32, so their product is 256\xB73125/(625\xB732) = 8 \xD7 5 = 40.
C(9, 5) = 126, so the coefficient is 126 \xD7 40 = 5040.`},{pn:207,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"If the constant term in the binomial expansion of (x^(5/2)/2 \u2212 4/x\u02E1)\u2079 is \u221284 and the coefficient of x\u207B\xB3\u02E1 is 2\u1D45\u03B2, where \u03B2 < 0 is an odd number, then |\u03B1l \u2212 \u03B2| is equal to",n:98,s:`The general term is C(9, r)(x^(5/2)/2)\u2079\u207B\u02B3(\u22124/x\u02E1)\u02B3 with power of x equal to 5(9 \u2212 r)/2 \u2212 lr.
For the constant term, l = 5(9 \u2212 r)/(2r). The constant term must be \u221284, so try r = 3: l = 5\xB76/6 = 5.
Check: C(9, 3)(1/2)\u2076(\u22124)\xB3 = 84 \xD7 (1/64) \xD7 (\u221264) = \u221284. This works, so l = 5.
Now x\u207B\xB3\u02E1 = x\u207B\xB9\u2075: 5(9 \u2212 r)/2 \u2212 5r = \u221215, so 45 \u2212 5r \u2212 10r = \u221230 and r = 5.
The coefficient is C(9, 5)(1/2)\u2074(\u22124)\u2075 = 126 \xD7 (1/16) \xD7 (\u22121024) = \u22128064.
\u22128064 = \u221263 \xD7 128 = 2\u2077 \xD7 (\u221263), so \u03B1 = 7 and \u03B2 = \u221263 (odd and negative, as required).
|\u03B1l \u2212 \u03B2| = |35 + 63| = 98.`},{pn:208,y:"JEE Advanced 2023",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"Let a and b be two nonzero real numbers. If the coefficient of x\u2075 in the expansion of (ax\xB2 + 70/(27bx))\u2074 is equal to the coefficient of x\u207B\u2075 in the expansion of (ax \u2212 1/(bx\xB2))\u2077, then the value of 2b is",n:3,s:`First expansion: C(4, r)(ax\xB2)\u2074\u207B\u02B3(70/(27bx))\u02B3 has power 8 \u2212 2r \u2212 r = 8 \u2212 3r. For x\u2075, r = 1.
The coefficient is C(4, 1)a\xB3\xB770/(27b) = 280a\xB3/(27b).
Second expansion: C(7, r)(ax)\u2077\u207B\u02B3(\u22121/(bx\xB2))\u02B3 has power 7 \u2212 r \u2212 2r = 7 \u2212 3r. For x\u207B\u2075, r = 4.
The coefficient is C(7, 4)a\xB3(\u22121)\u2074/b\u2074 = 35a\xB3/b\u2074.
Equate and cancel a\xB3 (a is not zero): 280/(27b) = 35/b\u2074, so b\xB3 = 35 \xD7 27/280 = 27/8.
So b = 3/2 and 2b = 3.`},{pn:209,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"Middle and greatest term",q:"If the maximum value of the term independent of t in the expansion of (t\xB2x^(1/5) + (1 \u2212 x)^(1/10)/t)\xB9\u2075, x \u2265 0, is K, then 8K is equal to",n:6006,s:`The general term is C(15, r)(t\xB2x^(1/5))\xB9\u2075\u207B\u02B3((1 \u2212 x)^(1/10)/t)\u02B3 with power of t equal to 2(15 \u2212 r) \u2212 r = 30 \u2212 3r.
Independent of t means 30 \u2212 3r = 0, so r = 10.
The term is C(15, 10)\xB7x^((1/5)\xB75)\xB7(1 \u2212 x)^((1/10)\xB710) = 3003\xB7x(1 \u2212 x).
Note: x(1 \u2212 x) = 1/4 \u2212 (x \u2212 1/2)\xB2, so it is largest when x = 1/2, where it equals 1/4.
So K = 3003/4, and 8K = 2 \xD7 3003 = 6006.`},{pn:210,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"coef",st:"Product of expansions",q:"If the coefficient of x and x\xB2 in the expansion of (1 + x)\u1D56(1 \u2212 x)\u1D60, p, q \u2264 15 are \u22123 and \u22125 respectively, then the coefficient of x\xB3 is equal to",n:23,s:`(1 + x)\u1D56(1 \u2212 x)\u1D60 = (1 + px + C(p,2)x\xB2 + C(p,3)x\xB3)(1 \u2212 qx + C(q,2)x\xB2 \u2212 C(q,3)x\xB3) up to x\xB3.
Coefficient of x: p \u2212 q = \u22123, so q = p + 3.
Coefficient of x\xB2: C(p,2) + C(q,2) \u2212 pq = \u22125, which simplifies to (p \u2212 q)\xB2 \u2212 (p + q) = \u221210.
Put p \u2212 q = \u22123: 9 \u2212 (p + q) = \u221210, so p + q = 19. With q = p + 3 we get p = 8 and q = 11.
Coefficient of x\xB3 = C(8,3) \u2212 C(8,2)\xB711 + 8\xB7C(11,2) \u2212 C(11,3) = 56 \u2212 308 + 440 \u2212 165.
56 \u2212 308 = \u2212252; \u2212252 + 440 = 188; 188 \u2212 165 = 23 (a computer expansion also gives 23).
So the coefficient of x\xB3 is 23.`},{pn:211,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"exp",st:"Middle and greatest term",q:"Let for the 9th term in the binomial expansion of (3 + 6x)\u207F, in the increasing powers of 6x, to be the greatest for x = 3/2, the least value of n is n\u2080. If k is the ratio of the coefficient of x\u2076 to the coefficient of x\xB3, then k + n\u2080 is equal to",n:24,s:`The 9th term is T\u2089 = C(n, 8)\xB73\u207F\u207B\u2078\xB7(6x)\u2078. At x = 3/2, 6x = 9, so the (r + 1)th term is C(n, r)\xB73\u207F\u207B\u02B3\xB79\u02B3.
The ratio of consecutive terms is T(r+1)/T(r) = ((n \u2212 r + 1)/r)(9/3) = 3(n \u2212 r + 1)/r.
T\u2089 \u2265 T\u2088: 3(n \u2212 7)/8 \u2265 1, so n \u2265 7 + 8/3, i.e. n \u2265 10.
T\u2089 \u2265 T\u2081\u2080: 3(n \u2212 8)/9 \u2264 1, so n \u2264 11. So n can be 10 or 11, and the least is n\u2080 = 10 (computer check agrees).
For (3 + 6x)\xB9\u2070: coefficient of x\u2076 is C(10, 6)3\u20746\u2076 and of x\xB3 is C(10, 3)3\u20776\xB3.
k = (210/120) \xD7 (3\u2074/3\u2077) \xD7 6\xB3 = (7/4) \xD7 (1/27) \xD7 216 = 14.
So k + n\u2080 = 14 + 10 = 24.`},{pn:212,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"Let the coefficients of the middle terms in the expansion of (1/\u221A6 + \u03B2x)\u2074, (1 \u2212 3\u03B2x)\xB2 and (1 \u2212 (\u03B2/2)x)\u2076, \u03B2 > 0, respectively form the first three terms of an A.P. If d is the common difference of this A.P., then 50 \u2212 2d/\u03B2\xB2 is equal to",n:57,s:`Middle term of (1/\u221A6 + \u03B2x)\u2074 is the 3rd: C(4, 2)(1/6)\u03B2\xB2x\xB2, coefficient = \u03B2\xB2.
Middle term of (1 \u2212 3\u03B2x)\xB2 is the 2nd: 2\xB7(\u22123\u03B2x), coefficient = \u22126\u03B2.
Middle term of (1 \u2212 (\u03B2/2)x)\u2076 is the 4th: C(6, 3)(\u2212\u03B2/2)\xB3x\xB3, coefficient = \u221220\u03B2\xB3/8 = \u22125\u03B2\xB3/2.
For an A.P. the middle term is the average: 2(\u22126\u03B2) = \u03B2\xB2 \u2212 5\u03B2\xB3/2, so 5\u03B2\xB2 \u2212 2\u03B2 \u2212 24 = 0, whose positive root is \u03B2 = 12/5.
The common difference is d = \u22126\u03B2 \u2212 \u03B2\xB2, so 2d/\u03B2\xB2 = \u221212/\u03B2 \u2212 2.
Then 50 \u2212 2d/\u03B2\xB2 = 50 + 12/\u03B2 + 2 = 52 + 12 \xD7 (5/12) = 52 + 5 = 57.`},{pn:213,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"General term",q:"Let the ratio of the fifth term from the beginning to the fifth term from the end in the binomial expansion of (2^(1/4) + 1/3^(1/4))\u207F, in the increasing powers of 1/3^(1/4), be 6^(1/4) : 1. If the sixth term from the beginning is \u03B1/3^(1/4), then \u03B1 is equal to",n:84,s:`Write a = 2^(1/4), b = 3^(\u22121/4). The fifth term from the beginning is C(n, 4)a\u207F\u207B\u2074b\u2074.
The fifth term from the end is the (n \u2212 3)th from the beginning: C(n, n \u2212 4)a\u2074b\u207F\u207B\u2074 = C(n, 4)a\u2074b\u207F\u207B\u2074.
Their ratio is (a/b)\u207F\u207B\u2078 = (2^(1/4)\xB73^(1/4))\u207F\u207B\u2078 = 6^((n \u2212 8)/4).
Set 6^((n \u2212 8)/4) = 6^(1/4): n \u2212 8 = 1, so n = 9.
The sixth term is C(9, 5)a\u2074b\u2075 = 126 \xD7 2 \xD7 3^(\u22125/4) = 252/(3 \xB7 3^(1/4)) = 84/3^(1/4).
Comparing with \u03B1/3^(1/4) gives \u03B1 = 84.`},{pn:214,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"If the coefficient of x\xB9\u2070 in the binomial expansion of (\u221Ax/5^(1/4) + \u221A5/x^(1/3))\u2076\u2070 is 5\u1D4Fl, where l, k \u2208 \u2115 and l is co-prime to 5, then k is equal to",n:5,s:`The general term is C(60, r)\xB7(x^(1/2)\xB75^(\u22121/4))\u2076\u2070\u207B\u02B3\xB7(5^(1/2)\xB7x^(\u22121/3))\u02B3.
The power of x is (60 \u2212 r)/2 \u2212 r/3 = 10, so 180 \u2212 3r \u2212 2r = 60 and r = 24.
The power of 5 is \u2212(36)/4 + 24/2 = \u22129 + 12 = 3, so the coefficient is C(60, 24)\xB75\xB3.
Now count the factors of 5 in C(60, 24) = 60!/(24!\xB736!): in 60! there are 12 + 2 = 14, in 24! there are 4, in 36! there are 7 + 1 = 8.
So C(60, 24) has 14 \u2212 4 \u2212 8 = 2 factors of 5 (a computer check gives 2).
So the total power of 5 is 3 + 2 = 5, and k = 5.`},{pn:215,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sum",st:"Sums of binomial coefficients",q:"If the sum of the coefficients of all the positive powers of x, in the Binomial expansion of (x\u207F + 2/x\u2075)\u2077 is 939, then the sum of all the possible integral values of n is",n:57,s:`The general term is C(7, r)\xB7x\u207F\u207D\u2077\u207B\u02B3\u207E\xB7(2/x\u2075)\u02B3 = C(7, r)2\u02B3\xB7x^(7n \u2212 nr \u2212 5r) = C(7, r)2\u02B3\xB7x^(7n \u2212 (n + 5)r).
The total of all coefficients is 3\u2077 = 2187, and positive powers must add to 939.
Try values of n and add C(7, r)2\u02B3 for every r with 7n \u2212 (n + 5)r > 0. A computer scan of integers from \u221240 to 40 shows the sum is 939 exactly for n = 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12.
Check n = 7: positive powers need 49 \u2212 12r > 0, so r = 0 to 4 and the coefficients 1 + 14 + 84 + 280 + 560 = 939.
For larger n up to 12 the same five terms (r = 0 to 4) stay positive, while r = 5 turns non-positive, so the sum stays 939.
So the possible n are 7 to 12, and their sum is 7 + 8 + 9 + 10 + 11 + 12 = 57.`},{pn:216,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"The number of positive integers k such that the constant term in the binomial expansion of (2x\xB3 + 3/x\u1D4F)\xB9\xB2, x \u2260 0 is 2\u2078\xB7l, where l is an odd integer, is",n:2,s:`The general term is C(12, r)(2x\xB3)\xB9\xB2\u207B\u02B3(3/x\u1D4F)\u02B3 with power of x equal to 3(12 \u2212 r) \u2212 kr.
Constant term: 36 \u2212 3r \u2212 kr = 0, so r = 36/(3 + k). So 3 + k must divide 36 and r \u2264 12, giving k = 1, 3, 6, 9, 15, 33 with r = 9, 6, 4, 3, 2, 1.
The constant term is C(12, r)\xB72\xB9\xB2\u207B\u02B3\xB73\u02B3. It equals 2\u2078\xB7(odd) when the total power of 2 is exactly 8.
Count the power of 2 in C(12, r) plus 12 \u2212 r: for r = 9: 5; r = 6: 8; r = 4: 8; r = 3: 11; r = 2: 11; r = 1: 13 (computer count of factors of 2).
Note: for example r = 6 gives C(12, 6) = 924 = 4 \xD7 231, which has two factors of 2, and 12 \u2212 6 = 6 more gives 8.
The exact power 8 happens for r = 6 and r = 4, that is k = 3 and k = 6, so the number of such k is 2.`},{pn:217,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"Let the coefficient of x\u207B\xB9 and x\u207B\xB3 in the expansion of (2x^(1/5) \u2212 1/x^(1/5))\xB9\u2075, x > 0, be m and n respectively. If r is a positive integer such that mn\xB2 = \xB9\u2075C\u1D63\xB72\u02B3, then the value of r is equal to ____.",n:5,s:`The general term is T\u2096\u208A\u2081 = \xB9\u2075C\u2096 (2x^(1/5))\xB9\u2075\u207B\u1D4F (\u22121/x^(1/5))\u1D4F = \xB9\u2075C\u2096 2\xB9\u2075\u207B\u1D4F (\u22121)\u1D4F x^((15 \u2212 2k)/5).
For x\u207B\xB9 we need (15 \u2212 2k)/5 = \u22121, so k = 10. Then m = \xB9\u2075C\u2081\u2080 \xB7 2\u2075 \xB7 (+1) = 3003 \xB7 32.
For x\u207B\xB3 we need (15 \u2212 2k)/5 = \u22123, so k = 15. Then n = \xB9\u2075C\u2081\u2085 \xB7 2\u2070 \xB7 (\u22121)\xB9\u2075 = \u22121.
So mn\xB2 = \xB9\u2075C\u2081\u2080 \xB7 2\u2075 \xB7 1 = \xB9\u2075C\u2085 \xB7 2\u2075, because \xB9\u2075C\u2081\u2080 = \xB9\u2075C\u2085.
Comparing with \xB9\u2075C\u1D63\xB72\u02B3 gives r = 5.
Note: \xB9\u2075C\u2081\u2080 = \xB9\u2075C\u2085 = 3003 uses \u207FC\u1D63 = \u207FC\u2099\u208B\u1D63. A quick node check gave \xB9\u2075C\u2085 = 3003 and mn\xB2 = 96096 = 3003 \xB7 32.
So r = 5.`},{pn:218,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 1 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"exp",st:"Middle and greatest term",q:"If the sum of the coefficients in the expansion of (x + y)\u207F is 4096, then the greatest coefficient in the expansion is ____.",n:924,s:`Put x = 1 and y = 1: the sum of all coefficients of (x + y)\u207F is 2\u207F.
So 2\u207F = 4096 = 2\xB9\xB2, which gives n = 12.
The coefficients are \xB9\xB2C\u2080, \xB9\xB2C\u2081, ..., \xB9\xB2C\u2081\u2082 and the largest one is the middle one, because they rise up to the middle and then fall.
The middle term is the 7th, so the greatest coefficient is \xB9\xB2C\u2086 = (12 \xD7 11 \xD7 10 \xD7 9 \xD7 8 \xD7 7)/(6 \xD7 5 \xD7 4 \xD7 3 \xD7 2 \xD7 1) = 924.
Note: for even n the single middle coefficient \u207FC\u2099/\u2082 is the greatest. Node check: \xB9\xB2C\u2086 = 924.
So the greatest coefficient is 924.`},{pn:219,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"If (3\u2076/4\u2074)\u1D4F is the term, independent of x, in the binomial expansion of (x/4 \u2212 12/x\xB2)\xB9\xB2, then k is equal to ____.",n:55,s:`The general term of (x/4 \u2212 12/x\xB2)\xB9\xB2 is \xB9\xB2C_r (x/4)\xB9\xB2\u207B\u02B3 (\u221212/x\xB2)\u02B3.
The power of x in it is (12 \u2212 r) \u2212 2r = 12 \u2212 3r. For the term independent of x we need 12 \u2212 3r = 0, so r = 4.
Put r = 4: the term is \xB9\xB2C\u2084 \xD7 (1/4)\u2078 \xD7 12\u2074 = 495 \xD7 12\u2074/4\u2078.
Note: 12\u2074 = 3\u2074 \xD7 4\u2074, so 12\u2074/4\u2078 = 3\u2074/4\u2074. The 4\u2074 in the denominator cancels neatly.
So the term is 495 \xD7 3\u2074/4\u2074. Now 495 = 55 \xD7 9 = 55 \xD7 3\xB2, giving 55 \xD7 3\u2076/4\u2074.
Comparing with (3\u2076/4\u2074)\u1D4F, we get k = 55.
Note: in the scan the power of 3 in the question is cut off at the top. It is read as 3\u2076, because that is the value for which the printed key 55 is obtained.`},{pn:220,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"num",st:"Counting terms",q:"The number of rational terms in the binomial expansion of (4^(1/4) + 5^(1/6))\xB9\xB2\u2070 is ____.",n:21,s:`The general term is T\u1D63\u208A\u2081 = \xB9\xB2\u2070C\u1D63 \xB7 4^((120 \u2212 r)/4) \xB7 5^(r/6).
Since 4^(1/4) = 2^(1/2), the first factor is 2^((120 \u2212 r)/2). It is an integer only when 120 \u2212 r is even, so r is even.
The second factor 5^(r/6) is an integer only when r is a multiple of 6.
A multiple of 6 is automatically even, so r must be a multiple of 6 with 0 \u2264 r \u2264 120.
These values are r = 0, 6, 12, ..., 120, and their number is 120/6 + 1 = 21.
So there are 21 rational terms.`},{pn:221,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 22 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"If the constant term in the binomial expansion of (2x\u02B3 + 1/x\xB2)\xB9\u2070 is 180, then r is equal to ____.",n:8,s:`The general term is T\u2096\u208A\u2081 = \xB9\u2070C\u2096 (2x\u02B3)\xB9\u2070\u207B\u1D4F (x\u207B\xB2)\u1D4F = \xB9\u2070C\u2096 2\xB9\u2070\u207B\u1D4F x^(r(10 \u2212 k) \u2212 2k).
The term is constant when r(10 \u2212 k) = 2k, that is k = 10r/(r + 2).
The constant term is \xB9\u2070C\u2096 \xB7 2\xB9\u2070\u207B\u1D4F and it must equal 180. Try whole-number k: k = 5 gives 252 \xB7 32 = 8064, k = 6 gives 210 \xB7 16 = 3360, k = 8 gives 45 \xB7 4 = 180.
So k = 8, and then 10r/(r + 2) = 8 gives 10r = 8r + 16, so r = 8.
Note: k must be a whole number from 0 to 10, so only a few values of r work; testing them is quicker than solving \xB9\u2070C\u2096 2\xB9\u2070\u207B\u1D4F = 180 directly. A node loop over r gave 180 only for r = 8.
So r = 8.`},{pn:222,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"Middle and greatest term",q:"The ratio of the coefficient of the middle term in the expansion of (1 + x)\xB2\u2070 and the sum of the coefficients of two middle terms in the expansion of (1 + x)\xB9\u2079 is ____.",n:1,s:`(1 + x)\xB2\u2070 has 21 terms, so the middle term is the 11th and its coefficient is \xB2\u2070C\u2081\u2080.
(1 + x)\xB9\u2079 has 20 terms, so the two middle terms are the 10th and 11th, with coefficients \xB9\u2079C\u2089 and \xB9\u2079C\u2081\u2080.
Their sum is \xB9\u2079C\u2089 + \xB9\u2079C\u2081\u2080.
Pascal's rule says \u207FC\u1D63 + \u207FC\u1D63\u208A\u2081 = \u207F\u207A\xB9C\u1D63\u208A\u2081, so \xB9\u2079C\u2089 + \xB9\u2079C\u2081\u2080 = \xB2\u2070C\u2081\u2080.
Hence the ratio is \xB2\u2070C\u2081\u2080 / \xB2\u2070C\u2081\u2080 = 1.
So the ratio is 1.`},{pn:223,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"exp",st:"Term independent of x",q:"The term independent of x in the expansion of ((x + 1)/(x^(2/3) \u2212 x^(1/3) + 1) \u2212 (x \u2212 1)/(x \u2212 x^(1/2)))\xB9\u2070, where x \u2260 0, 1 is equal to ____.",n:210,s:`Put t = x^(1/3). Then x + 1 = t\xB3 + 1 = (t + 1)(t\xB2 \u2212 t + 1), so the first fraction simplifies to t + 1 = x^(1/3) + 1.
Put u = x^(1/2). Then (x \u2212 1)/(x \u2212 x^(1/2)) = (u \u2212 1)(u + 1)/(u(u \u2212 1)) = (u + 1)/u = 1 + x^(\u22121/2).
The bracket becomes (x^(1/3) + 1) \u2212 (1 + x^(\u22121/2)) = x^(1/3) \u2212 x^(\u22121/2).
The general term of (x^(1/3) \u2212 x^(\u22121/2))\xB9\u2070 has power of x equal to (10 \u2212 k)/3 \u2212 k/2.
This is 0 when 2(10 \u2212 k) = 3k, so k = 4, and the term independent of x is \xB9\u2070C\u2084 (\u22121)\u2074 = 210.
Note: the trick is to cancel the denominators first using a\xB3 + 1 and a\xB2 \u2212 1 factorisations; without it the expression looks impossible.
So the answer is 210.`},{pn:224,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"If the coefficients of x\u2077 and x\u2078 in the expansion of (2 + x/3)\u207F are equal, then the value of n is equal to ____.",n:55,s:`The general term is \u207FC\u1D63 2\u207F\u207B\u02B3 (x/3)\u02B3, so the coefficient of x\u02B3 is \u207FC\u1D63 2\u207F\u207B\u02B3 / 3\u02B3.
Coefficient of x\u2077 = \u207FC\u2087 2\u207F\u207B\u2077 / 3\u2077 and coefficient of x\u2078 = \u207FC\u2088 2\u207F\u207B\u2078 / 3\u2078.
Setting them equal and dividing: \u207FC\u2088 / \u207FC\u2087 = (2 \xB7 3) = 6.
The ratio \u207FC\u2088 / \u207FC\u2087 = (n \u2212 7)/8, so (n \u2212 7)/8 = 6 and n \u2212 7 = 48.
So n = 55. Check: \u2075\u2075C\u2088 / \u2075\u2075C\u2087 = 48/8 = 6.
Thus n = 55.`},{pn:225,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"Let the coefficients of third, fourth and fifth terms in the expansion of (x + a/x\xB2)\u207F, x \u2260 0, be in the ratio 12 : 8 : 3. Then the term independent of x in the expansion is equal to ____.",n:3.75,s:`The 3rd, 4th, 5th terms have coefficients \u207FC\u2082a\xB2, \u207FC\u2083a\xB3, \u207FC\u2084a\u2074.
Ratio of 4th to 3rd: \u207FC\u2083a\xB3 / \u207FC\u2082a\xB2 = (n \u2212 2)a/3 = 8/12, so (n \u2212 2)a = 2.
Ratio of 5th to 4th: \u207FC\u2084a\u2074 / \u207FC\u2083a\xB3 = (n \u2212 3)a/4 = 3/8, so (n \u2212 3)a = 3/2.
Subtract: a = 2 \u2212 3/2 = 1/2, so n \u2212 2 = 4 and n = 6. Check: coefficients 15/4, 5/2, 15/16 are in the ratio 12 : 8 : 3.
The general term is \u2076C\u2096 x\u2076\u207B\u1D4F (a x\u207B\xB2)\u1D4F = \u2076C\u2096 a\u1D4F x\u2076\u207B\xB3\u1D4F, constant when k = 2.
The constant term is \u2076C\u2082 a\xB2 = 15 \xD7 1/4 = 15/4 = 3.75.
Note: the printed key says 4; working gives 15/4 (3.75). The key looks like the rounded value.`},{pn:226,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"For a positive integer n, (1 + 1/x)\u207F is expanded in increasing powers of x. If three consecutive coefficients in this expansion are in the ratio 2 : 5 : 12, then n is equal to ____.",n:118,s:`The coefficient of x\u207B\u02B3 is \u207FC\u1D63, so in increasing powers of x the coefficients run \u207FC\u2099, \u207FC\u2099\u208B\u2081, ..., \u207FC\u2080. Because \u207FC\u2C7C = \u207FC\u2099\u208B\u2C7C, three consecutive coefficients in the ratio 2 : 5 : 12 can be written as \u207FC\u2096 : \u207FC\u2096\u208A\u2081 : \u207FC\u2096\u208A\u2082.
\u207FC\u2096 : \u207FC\u2096\u208A\u2081 = 2 : 5. Since \u207FC\u2096\u208A\u2081/\u207FC\u2096 = (n \u2212 k)/(k + 1), we get (n \u2212 k)/(k + 1) = 5/2, so 2n \u2212 2k = 5k + 5, i.e. 7k = 2n \u2212 5.
\u207FC\u2096\u208A\u2081 : \u207FC\u2096\u208A\u2082 = 5 : 12, so (n \u2212 k \u2212 1)/(k + 2) = 12/5, giving 5n \u2212 5k \u2212 5 = 12k + 24, i.e. 17k = 5n \u2212 29.
Substitute n = (7k + 5)/2 into the second: 17k = (35k + 25)/2 \u2212 29, so 34k = 35k \u2212 33 and k = 33.
Then n = (7 \xD7 33 + 5)/2 = 118. Node check: \xB9\xB9\u2078C\u2083\u2083 : \xB9\xB9\u2078C\u2083\u2084 : \xB9\xB9\u2078C\u2083\u2085 = 2 : 5 : 12.
So n = 118.`},{pn:227,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"The natural number m, for which the coefficient of x in the binomial expansion of (x\u1D50 + 1/x\xB2)\xB2\xB2 is 1540, is ____.",n:13,s:`The general term is \xB2\xB2C\u2096 (x\u1D50)\xB2\xB2\u207B\u1D4F (x\u207B\xB2)\u1D4F = \xB2\xB2C\u2096 x^(m(22 \u2212 k) \u2212 2k).
For the coefficient of x we need m(22 \u2212 k) \u2212 2k = 1, i.e. 22m \u2212 (m + 2)k = 1.
The coefficient is \xB2\xB2C\u2096 = 1540. Since \xB2\xB2C\u2081\u2089 = \xB2\xB2C\u2083 = (22 \xD7 21 \xD7 20)/6 = 1540, take k = 19 (or 3).
With k = 19: 22m \u2212 19m \u2212 38 = 1, so 3m = 39 and m = 13. With k = 3: 22m \u2212 3m \u2212 6 = 1 gives 19m = 7, not a natural number.
Note: a node search over m = 1 to 39 and k = 0 to 22 showed that only m = 13 (k = 19) gives coefficient 1540.
So m = 13.`},{pn:228,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"gen",st:"Multinomial and other",q:"The coefficient of x\u2074 in the expansion of (1 + x + x\xB2 + x\xB3)\u2076 in powers of x, is ____.",n:120,s:`Factorise: 1 + x + x\xB2 + x\xB3 = (1 + x)(1 + x\xB2).
So (1 + x + x\xB2 + x\xB3)\u2076 = (1 + x)\u2076 (1 + x\xB2)\u2076.
x\u2074 arises as x\u2074\xB71, x\xB2\xB7x\xB2, or 1\xB7x\u2074 from the two factors, using (1 + x)\u2076 = \u03A3\u2076C\u2096x\u1D4F and (1 + x\xB2)\u2076 = \u03A3\u2076C\u2C7Cx\xB2\u02B2.
The three cases give \u2076C\u2084\xB7\u2076C\u2080 + \u2076C\u2082\xB7\u2076C\u2081 + \u2076C\u2080\xB7\u2076C\u2082 = 15 + 15 \xD7 6 + 15 = 120.
Note: factoring first turns a four-term expansion into a product of two simple binomials. A node polynomial multiplication also gave 120.
So the coefficient is 120.`},{pn:229,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Sums of binomial coefficients",q:"If the sum of the coefficients of all even powers of x in the product (1 + x + x\xB2 + .... + x\xB2\u207F)(1 \u2212 x + x\xB2 \u2212 x\xB3 + .... + x\xB2\u207F) is 61, then n is equal to ____.",n:30,s:`Let P(x) be the product. The sum of coefficients of even powers is (P(1) + P(\u22121))/2.
P(1) = (2n + 1) \xD7 (1 \u2212 1 + 1 \u2212 ... + 1) = (2n + 1) \xD7 1, since the second factor has 2n + 1 terms alternating from +1 to +1.
P(\u22121) = (1 \u2212 1 + 1 \u2212 ... + 1) \xD7 (1 + 1 + ... + 1) = 1 \xD7 (2n + 1).
So the sum of even-power coefficients is (2n + 1 + 2n + 1)/2 = 2n + 1.
Set 2n + 1 = 61, so n = 30.
Note: putting x = 1 and x = \u22121 adds or cancels odd-power terms, which is the standard way to separate even and odd powers.
So n = 30.`},{pn:230,y:"JEE Advanced 2013",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"The coefficients of three consecutive terms of (1 + x)\u207F\u207A\u2075 are in the ratio 5 : 10 : 14. Then n = ____.",n:6,s:`Let N = n + 5 and let the three consecutive coefficients be \u1D3AC\u1D63, \u1D3AC\u1D63\u208A\u2081, \u1D3AC\u1D63\u208A\u2082.
\u1D3AC\u1D63 : \u1D3AC\u1D63\u208A\u2081 = 5 : 10 = 1 : 2, and \u1D3AC\u1D63\u208A\u2081/\u1D3AC\u1D63 = (N \u2212 r)/(r + 1), so (N \u2212 r)/(r + 1) = 2 and N = 3r + 2.
\u1D3AC\u1D63\u208A\u2081 : \u1D3AC\u1D63\u208A\u2082 = 10 : 14, and \u1D3AC\u1D63\u208A\u2082/\u1D3AC\u1D63\u208A\u2081 = (N \u2212 r \u2212 1)/(r + 2) = 14/10 = 7/5.
Substitute N = 3r + 2: (2r + 1)/(r + 2) = 7/5, so 10r + 5 = 7r + 14 and r = 3.
Then N = 11, so n = N \u2212 5 = 6. Node check: \xB9\xB9C\u2083 : \xB9\xB9C\u2084 : \xB9\xB9C\u2085 = 165 : 330 : 462 = 5 : 10 : 14.
So n = 6.`},{pn:231,y:"AIEEE 2008",tp:"sum",st:"Using differentiation and integration",q:`Statement-1: \u03A3 (r + 1)\xB7\u207FC\u1D63 = (n + 2)2\u207F\u207B\xB9, sum over r = 0 to n.
Statement-2: \u03A3 (r + 1)\xB7\u207FC\u1D63 x\u02B3 = (1 + x)\u207F + nx(1 + x)\u207F\u207B\xB9, sum over r = 0 to n.`,o:["Statement-1 is true, Statement-2 is false","Statement-1 is false, Statement-2 is true","Statement-1 is true, Statement-2 is true; Statement-2 is a correct explanation for Statement-1","Statement-1 is true, Statement-2 is true; Statement-2 is not a correct explanation for Statement-1"],c:2,s:`Start from x(1 + x)\u207F = \u03A3 \u207FC\u1D63 x\u02B3\u207A\xB9 and differentiate both sides with respect to x.
Left side: (1 + x)\u207F + nx(1 + x)\u207F\u207B\xB9. Right side: \u03A3 (r + 1) \u207FC\u1D63 x\u02B3. So Statement-2 is true.
Note: x(1 + x)\u207F is used because differentiating x\u02B3\u207A\xB9 brings down the factor (r + 1) that we need.
Put x = 1 in Statement-2: \u03A3 (r + 1) \u207FC\u1D63 = 2\u207F + n\xB72\u207F\u207B\xB9 = 2\u207F\u207B\xB9(2 + n) = (n + 2)2\u207F\u207B\xB9, which is Statement-1, so Statement-1 is true.
Statement-1 comes directly from Statement-2, so Statement-2 explains it, which is option (c).`},{pn:232,y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"num",st:"Counting terms",q:"The sum of the rational terms in the expansion of (\u221A2 + 3^(1/5))\xB9\u2070 is ......",a:"41",s:`The general term is T\u1D63\u208A\u2081 = \xB9\u2070C\u1D63 \xB7 2^((10 \u2212 r)/2) \xB7 3^(r/5), for r = 0 to 10.
The power of 2 is a whole number only when 10 \u2212 r is even, so r is even.
The power of 3 is a whole number only when r is a multiple of 5, so r = 0, 5 or 10.
Both conditions hold only for r = 0 and r = 10.
For r = 0: \xB9\u2070C\u2080 \xB7 2\u2075 \xB7 3\u2070 = 32. For r = 10: \xB9\u2070C\u2081\u2080 \xB7 2\u2070 \xB7 3\xB2 = 9.
The sum of the rational terms is 32 + 9 = 41.`},{pn:233,y:"IIT-JEE 1994",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"Let n be a positive integer. If the coefficients of 2\u207F\u1D48, 3\u02B3\u1D48, 4\u1D57\u02B0 terms in the expansion of (1 + x)\u207F are in A.P. then n = ......",a:"7",s:`The coefficients of the 2nd, 3rd and 4th terms are \u207FC\u2081 = n, \u207FC\u2082 = n(n \u2212 1)/2 and \u207FC\u2083 = n(n \u2212 1)(n \u2212 2)/6.
Three numbers are in A.P. when twice the middle one equals the sum of the other two: 2\xB7\u207FC\u2082 = \u207FC\u2081 + \u207FC\u2083.
So n(n \u2212 1) = n + n(n \u2212 1)(n \u2212 2)/6. Divide by n: n \u2212 1 = 1 + (n \u2212 1)(n \u2212 2)/6, so 6(n \u2212 2) = (n \u2212 1)(n \u2212 2).
So n \u2212 2 = 0 or n \u2212 1 = 6, giving n = 2 or n = 7.
For n = 2 the expansion has only 3 terms, so there is no 4th term; hence n = 2 is rejected.
Therefore n = 7. Check: 7, 21, 35 are in A.P. with difference 14.`},{pn:234,y:"IIT-JEE 1982",tp:"gen",st:"Multinomial and other",q:"The coefficient of x\u2079\u2079 in the polynomial (x \u2212 1)(x \u2212 2) ... (x \u2212 100) is ......",a:"\u22125050",s:`The polynomial has degree 100, since it is a product of 100 linear factors.
To get x\u2079\u2079 we take x from 99 of the factors and the constant from exactly one factor.
If the constant is taken from the factor (x \u2212 k), it contributes \u2212k, so the coefficient is \u2212(1 + 2 + 3 + ... + 100).
Use 1 + 2 + ... + 100 = 100 \xD7 101/2 = 5050.
So the coefficient of x\u2079\u2079 is \u22125050.`},{pn:235,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Sums of binomial coefficients",q:"If A denotes the sum of all the coefficients in the expansion of (1 \u2212 3x + 10x\xB2)\u207F and B denotes the sum of all the coefficients in the expansion of (1 + x\xB2)\u207F, then",o:["B = A\xB3","3A = B","A = 3B","A = B\xB3"],c:3,s:`The sum of all coefficients of a polynomial is its value at x = 1.
A = (1 \u2212 3 + 10)\u207F = 8\u207F.
B = (1 + 1)\u207F = 2\u207F.
Since 8 = 2\xB3, A = 8\u207F = (2\u207F)\xB3 = B\xB3.
So A = B\xB3, which is option (d).`},{pn:236,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 15 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"coef",st:"Specific coefficient",q:"Let (a + bx + cx\xB2)\xB9\u2070 = \u03A3 p\u1D62x\u2071 (i = 0 to 20), a, b, c \u2208 \u2115. If p\u2081 = 20 and p\u2082 = 210, then 2(a + b + c) is equal to",o:["8","6","12","15"],c:2,s:`p\u2081 is the coefficient of x: only one factor gives bx and the other nine give a, so p\u2081 = 10\xB7a\u2079\xB7b = 20, i.e. a\u2079b = 2.
a and b are natural numbers, so a = 1 and b = 2 (a = 2 would need a\u2079 = 512 > 2).
p\u2082 is the coefficient of x\xB2: either two factors give bx, or one factor gives cx\xB2. So p\u2082 = \xB9\u2070C\u2082 a\u2078b\xB2 + 10a\u2079c.
Substitute a = 1, b = 2: p\u2082 = 45 \xD7 4 + 10c = 180 + 10c = 210, so c = 3.
Then 2(a + b + c) = 2(1 + 2 + 3) = 12, which is option (c).`},{pn:237,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"gen",st:"Multinomial and other",q:"The number of ways to distribute 30 identical candies among four children C\u2081, C\u2082, C\u2083 and C\u2084 so that C\u2082 receives atleast 4 and atmost 7 candies, C\u2083 receives atleast 2 and atmost 6 candies, is equal to :",o:["205","615","510","430"],c:3,s:`Let C\u2082 get 4 + p candies (p = 0 to 3) and C\u2083 get 2 + q candies (q = 0 to 4).
The remaining candies are 30 \u2212 6 \u2212 p \u2212 q = 24 \u2212 p \u2212 q, shared freely among C\u2081 and C\u2084.
Sharing m identical candies between two children can be done in m + 1 ways, so there are 25 \u2212 p \u2212 q ways.
Add over all 4 \xD7 5 = 20 pairs (p, q): 20 \xD7 25 \u2212 5 \xD7 (0 + 1 + 2 + 3) \u2212 4 \xD7 (0 + 1 + 2 + 3 + 4) = 500 \u2212 30 \u2212 40 = 430.
A node loop over all distributions also gave 430, which is option (d).`},{pn:238,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"coef",st:"Product of expansions",q:"The coefficient of x\xB2\u2075\u2076 in the expansion of (1 \u2212 x)\xB9\u2070\xB9(x\xB2 + x + 1)\xB9\u2070\u2070 is",o:["\xB9\u2070\u2070C\u2081\u2086","\xB9\u2070\u2070C\u2081\u2085","\u2212\xB9\u2070\u2070C\u2081\u2086","\u2212\xB9\u2070\u2070C\u2081\u2085"],c:1,s:`Write (1 \u2212 x)\xB9\u2070\xB9 = (1 \u2212 x)(1 \u2212 x)\xB9\u2070\u2070, so the product is (1 \u2212 x)\xB7[(1 \u2212 x)(1 + x + x\xB2)]\xB9\u2070\u2070.
Since (1 \u2212 x)(1 + x + x\xB2) = 1 \u2212 x\xB3, the product becomes (1 \u2212 x)(1 \u2212 x\xB3)\xB9\u2070\u2070.
Note: this uses the identity (1 \u2212 x)(1 + x + x\xB2) = 1 \u2212 x\xB3; it turns a messy product into a single binomial in x\xB3.
(1 \u2212 x\xB3)\xB9\u2070\u2070 has only powers x\xB3\u1D4F with coefficient (\u22121)\u1D4F \xB9\u2070\u2070C\u2096. Since 256 is not a multiple of 3, x\xB2\u2075\u2076 must come from (\u2212x) times x\xB2\u2075\u2075.
x\xB2\u2075\u2075 = (x\xB3)\u2078\u2075, so k = 85 and the coefficient is (\u22121) \xD7 (\u22121)\u2078\u2075 \xD7 \xB9\u2070\u2070C\u2088\u2085 = \xB9\u2070\u2070C\u2088\u2085.
Using \xB9\u2070\u2070C\u2088\u2085 = \xB9\u2070\u2070C\u2081\u2085, the coefficient is \xB9\u2070\u2070C\u2081\u2085, option (b).`},{pn:239,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Sums of binomial coefficients",q:"Let [x] denote greatest integer less than or equal to x. If for n \u2208 N, (1 \u2212 x + x\xB3)\u207F = \u03A3 a\u2C7Cx\u02B2 (j = 0 to 3n), then \u03A3 a\u2082\u2C7C (j = 0 to [3n/2]) + 4 \u03A3 a\u2082\u2C7C\u208A\u2081 (j = 0 to [(3n \u2212 1)/2]) is equal to",o:["2\u207F\u207B\xB9","1","n","2"],c:1,s:`The first sum is the sum of all even-position coefficients and the second is the sum of all odd-position coefficients of the polynomial.
Let f(x) = (1 \u2212 x + x\xB3)\u207F. Then f(1) = 1\u207F = 1 and f(\u22121) = (1 + 1 \u2212 1)\u207F = 1.
Even sum = (f(1) + f(\u22121))/2 = 1, and odd sum = (f(1) \u2212 f(\u22121))/2 = 0.
Note: f(1) adds all coefficients while f(\u22121) subtracts the odd ones, so half their sum or difference separates the two groups.
The required value is 1 + 4 \xD7 0 = 1, which is option (b).`},{pn:240,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sum",st:"Sums of binomial coefficients",q:"Let (1 + x + 2x\xB2)\xB2\u2070 = a\u2080 + a\u2081x + a\u2082x\xB2 + ..... + a\u2084\u2080x\u2074\u2070. Then a\u2081 + a\u2083 + a\u2085 + ...... + a\u2083\u2087 is equal to",o:["2\xB9\u2079(20\xB2\u2070 + 21)","2\xB2\u2070(2\xB2\u2070 + 21)","2\xB9\u2079(2\xB2\u2070 \u2212 21)","2\xB2\u2070(2\xB2\u2070 \u2212 21)"],c:2,s:`Let f(x) = (1 + x + 2x\xB2)\xB2\u2070. Then f(1) = 4\xB2\u2070 = 2\u2074\u2070 and f(\u22121) = (1 \u2212 1 + 2)\xB2\u2070 = 2\xB2\u2070.
The sum of all odd-position coefficients a\u2081 + a\u2083 + ... + a\u2083\u2089 is (f(1) \u2212 f(\u22121))/2 = (2\u2074\u2070 \u2212 2\xB2\u2070)/2 = 2\xB9\u2079(2\xB2\u2070 \u2212 1).
We want the sum only up to a\u2083\u2087, so subtract a\u2083\u2089.
To get x\xB3\u2079 we take 2x\xB2 from 19 factors and x from one factor: a\u2083\u2089 = 20 \xD7 2\xB9\u2079 \xD7 1.
So a\u2081 + ... + a\u2083\u2087 = 2\xB9\u2079(2\xB2\u2070 \u2212 1) \u2212 20 \xD7 2\xB9\u2079 = 2\xB9\u2079(2\xB2\u2070 \u2212 21), which is option (c).`},{pn:241,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sum",st:"Series and identities",q:"Let (1 + x + x\xB2)\xB9\u2070 = a\u2080 + a\u2081x + a\u2082x\xB2 + .... + a\u2082\u2080x\xB2\u2070. If (a\u2081 + a\u2083 + a\u2085 + ..... + a\u2081\u2089) \u2212 11a\u2082 = 121k, then k is equal to ____.",n:239,s:`Let f(x) = (1 + x + x\xB2)\xB9\u2070. Then f(1) = 3\xB9\u2070 = 59049 and f(\u22121) = 1.
The odd-position sum a\u2081 + a\u2083 + ... + a\u2081\u2089 = (f(1) \u2212 f(\u22121))/2 = (59049 \u2212 1)/2 = 29524.
For a\u2082, the x\xB2 comes either from x\xB2 of one factor (10 ways) or from x \xB7 x in two factors (\xB9\u2070C\u2082 = 45 ways), so a\u2082 = 55.
Then 29524 \u2212 11 \xD7 55 = 29524 \u2212 605 = 28919.
Set 28919 = 121k. Dividing, 28919/121 = 239, so k = 239.
Note: the printed key list also reads 239, and a node check of the expansion gave the same a\u2082 and odd sum.`},{pn:242,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"num",st:"Counting terms",q:"The sum of all rational terms in the expansion of (1 + 2^(1/3) + 3^(1/2))\u2076 is equal to ____.",n:612,s:`Each term is 6!/(a! b! c!) \xB7 1\u1D43 \xB7 (2^(1/3))\u1D47 \xB7 (3^(1/2))\u1D9C with a + b + c = 6.
It is rational when b is a multiple of 3 (so 2^(b/3) is whole) and c is even (so 3^(c/2) is whole).
The allowed (a, b, c) are (6,0,0), (4,0,2), (2,0,4), (0,0,6), (3,3,0), (1,3,2), (0,6,0).
Their values are 1, 15 \xD7 3 = 45, 15 \xD7 9 = 135, 3\xB3 = 27, 20 \xD7 2 = 40, 60 \xD7 2 \xD7 3 = 360 and 2\xB2 = 4.
Adding: 1 + 45 + 135 + 27 + 40 + 360 + 4 = 612.
Note: a node loop over all (a, b, c) confirmed these seven terms and the total 612.`},{pn:243,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"gen",st:"Multinomial and other",q:"The coefficient of x\u2077 in (1 \u2212 x + 2x\xB3)\xB9\u2070 is ____.",n:960,s:`Choose i factors giving \u2212x and j factors giving 2x\xB3; the rest give 1. We need i + 3j = 7.
The possibilities are (i, j) = (7, 0), (4, 1) and (1, 2).
Each term is 10!/(i! j! (10 \u2212 i \u2212 j)!) \xB7 (\u22121)\u2071 \xB7 2\u02B2.
(7, 0): 120 \xD7 (\u22121) = \u2212120. (4, 1): 1260 \xD7 2 = 2520. (1, 2): 360 \xD7 (\u22121) \xD7 4 = \u22121440.
Sum: \u2212120 + 2520 \u2212 1440 = 960, and a node polynomial expansion also gave 960.`},{pn:244,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"gen",st:"Multinomial and other",q:"If the coefficient of a\u2077b\u2078 in the expansion of (a + 2b + 4ab)\xB9\u2070 is K \xB7 2\xB9\u2076, then K is equal to ____.",n:315,s:`Choose i factors giving a, j factors giving 2b and k factors giving 4ab, with i + j + k = 10.
The powers need i + k = 7 (for a) and j + k = 8 (for b).
From i = 7 \u2212 k and j = 8 \u2212 k, the condition i + j + k = 10 becomes 15 \u2212 k = 10, so k = 5, i = 2, j = 3.
The term is 10!/(2! 3! 5!) \xB7 2\xB3 \xB7 4\u2075 = 2520 \xD7 8 \xD7 1024 = 20643840.
Divide by 2\xB9\u2076 = 65536: 20643840/65536 = 315, so K = 315.`},{pn:245,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"Let (2x\xB2 + 3x + 4)\xB9\u2070 = \u03A3 a\u1D63x\u02B3 (r = 0 to 20). Then a\u2087/a\u2081\u2083 is equal to ____.",n:8,s:`Let P(x) = (2x\xB2 + 3x + 4)\xB9\u2070. Look at P(2/x): 2 \xD7 4/x\xB2 + 3 \xD7 2/x + 4 = 8/x\xB2 + 6/x + 4 = (2/x\xB2)(4 + 3x + 2x\xB2).
So P(2/x) = (2/x\xB2)\xB9\u2070 P(x) = 2\xB9\u2070 x\u207B\xB2\u2070 P(x).
Expand both sides: \u03A3 a\u1D63 2\u02B3 x\u207B\u02B3 = 2\xB9\u2070 \u03A3 a\u209B x\u02E2\u207B\xB2\u2070.
Compare powers x\u207B\u02B3: on the right s \u2212 20 = \u2212r gives s = 20 \u2212 r, so a\u1D63 2\u02B3 = 2\xB9\u2070 a\u2082\u2080\u208B\u1D63.
Put r = 7: a\u2087 \xB7 2\u2077 = 2\xB9\u2070 a\u2081\u2083, so a\u2087/a\u2081\u2083 = 2\xB3 = 8.
Note: a node expansion gave a\u2087 = 534827520 and a\u2081\u2083 = 66853440, whose ratio is exactly 8.`},{pn:246,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"gen",st:"Multinomial and other",q:"The coefficient of x\u2074 in the expansion of (1 + x + x\xB2)\xB9\u2070 is ____.",n:615,s:`Choose i factors giving x and j factors giving x\xB2; the rest give 1. We need i + 2j = 4.
The possibilities are (i, j) = (4, 0), (2, 1) and (0, 2).
(4, 0): \xB9\u2070C\u2084 = 210. (2, 1): 10!/(2! 1! 7!) = 360. (0, 2): \xB9\u2070C\u2082 = 45.
Add: 210 + 360 + 45 = 615, and a node expansion also gave 615.`},{pn:247,y:"IIT-JEE 1994",tp:"coef",st:"Relations between coefficients",q:"Let n be a +ve integer and (1 + x + x\xB2)\u207F = a\u2080 + a\u2081x + ..... + a\u2082\u2099x\xB2\u207F, then show that a\u2080\xB2 \u2212 a\u2081\xB2 + ..... + a\u2082\u2099\xB2 = a\u2099.",a:"Proved: a\u2080\xB2 \u2212 a\u2081\xB2 + a\u2082\xB2 \u2212 ... + a\u2082\u2099\xB2 = a\u2099.",s:`Let f(x) = (1 + x + x\xB2)\u207F = \u03A3 a\u1D63x\u02B3. Replace x by \u22121/x and multiply by x\xB2\u207F: x\xB2\u207F(1 \u2212 1/x + 1/x\xB2)\u207F = (x\xB2 \u2212 x + 1)\u207F = \u03A3 (\u22121)\u02E2 a\u209B x\xB2\u207F\u207B\u02E2.
Multiply this with f(x): (1 + x + x\xB2)\u207F(1 \u2212 x + x\xB2)\u207F = [\u03A3 a\u1D63x\u02B3][\u03A3 (\u22121)\u02E2 a\u209B x\xB2\u207F\u207B\u02E2].
Since (1 + x + x\xB2)(1 \u2212 x + x\xB2) = 1 + x\xB2 + x\u2074, the left side is (1 + x\xB2 + x\u2074)\u207F.
Take the coefficient of x\xB2\u207F on the right: we need r + 2n \u2212 s = 2n, i.e. r = s, which gives \u03A3 (\u22121)\u02B3 a\u1D63\xB2 = a\u2080\xB2 \u2212 a\u2081\xB2 + a\u2082\xB2 \u2212 ... + a\u2082\u2099\xB2.
On the left, put y = x\xB2: (1 + y + y\xB2)\u207F, and the coefficient of x\xB2\u207F = y\u207F is a\u2099, by the definition of the a\u1D63.
Note: the trick is that replacing x by \u22121/x turns the sum into a product whose middle coefficient collects a\u1D63\xB2 with alternating signs.
Equating the two coefficients gives a\u2080\xB2 \u2212 a\u2081\xB2 + ..... + a\u2082\u2099\xB2 = a\u2099.`},{pn:248,y:"IIT-JEE 1982",tp:"sum",st:"Sums of binomial coefficients",q:"The sum of the coefficients of the polynomial (1 + x \u2212 3x\xB2)\xB2\xB9\u2076\xB3 is ......",a:"\u22121",s:`The sum of all coefficients of a polynomial is its value at x = 1.
Put x = 1: 1 + 1 \u2212 3 = \u22121.
So the sum is (\u22121)\xB2\xB9\u2076\xB3.
The power 2163 is odd, so (\u22121)\xB2\xB9\u2076\xB3 = \u22121.`}]})();
